buting/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#update-changelog-and-cargo-versions","content":"Send a PR similar to https://github.com/jj-vcs/jj/pull/5215. Feel free to\ncopy-edit the changelog in order to:"},"972":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-4","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#update-changelog-and-cargo-versions","content":"Populate \"Release highlights\" if relevant"},"973":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-5","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#update-changelog-and-cargo-versions","content":"Put more important items first so the reader doesn't miss them"},"974":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-6","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#update-changelog-and-cargo-versions","content":"Make items consistent when it comes to language and formatting"},"975":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-7","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#update-changelog-and-cargo-versions","content":"Catch any misplaced changelog items by looking at the CHANGELOG diff."},"976":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-8","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#update-changelog-and-cargo-versions","content":"To get the CHANGELOG diff, you can run"},"977":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-9","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#update-changelog-and-cargo-versions","content":"Producing the list of contributors is a bit annoying. The current suggestion is\nto run something like this:"},"978":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-10","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#update-changelog-and-cargo-versions","content":"https://docs.github.com/en/rest/commits/commits?apiVersion=2022-11-28#compare-two-commits"},"979":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-11","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#update-changelog-and-cargo-versions","content":"Alternatively, the list can be produced locally:"},"980":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-12","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#update-changelog-and-cargo-versions","content":"Then try to find the right GitHub username for each person and copy their name\nand username from the GitHub page for the person\n(e.g. https://github.com/martinvonz)."},"981":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-13","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#update-changelog-and-cargo-versions","content":"Get the PR through review and get it merged as usual."},"982":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-14","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#create-a-tag-and-a-github-release","content":"Go to https://github.com/jj-vcs/jj/releases and click \"Draft a new release\""},"983":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-15","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#create-a-tag-and-a-github-release","content":"Click \"Choose a tag\" and enter \"v0.<number>.0\" (e.g. \"v0.26.0\") to create a\nnew tag"},"984":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-16","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#create-a-tag-and-a-github-release","content":"Click \"Target\", then \"Recent commits\", and select the commit from your merged\nPR"},"985":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-17","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#create-a-tag-and-a-github-release","content":"Use the name (e.g. \"v0.26.0\") as \"Release title\". Paste the changelog entries\ninto the message body"},"986":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-18","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#create-a-tag-and-a-github-release","content":"Check \"Create a discussion for this release\""},"987":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-19","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#create-a-tag-and-a-github-release","content":"Click \"Publish release\""},"988":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-20","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#publish-the-crates-to-cratesio","content":"Go to a terminal and create a new clone of the repo :"},"989":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-21","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#publish-the-crates-to-cratesio","content":"Publish each crate:"},"990":{"id":"/contributing/releasing-22","page_id":"/contributing/releasing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/releasing#publish-the-crates-to-cratesio","content":"We recommend publishing from a new clone because cargo publish will\narchive ignored files if they match the patterns in [include]\n(example),\nso it's a security risk to run it an existing clone where you may have\nleft sensitive content in an ignored file."},"991":{"id":"/contributing/style-guide","page_id":"/contributing/style-guide","type":"page","content":"Style guide","tags":[],"url":"/contributing/style-guide"},"992":{"id":"/contributing/style-guide-0","page_id":"/contributing/style-guide","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/contributing/style-guide#panics","content":"Panics"},"993":{"id":"/contributing/style-guide-1","page_id":"/contributing/style-guide","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/contributing/style-guide#markdown","content":"Markdown"},"994":{"id":"/contributing/style-guide-2","page_id":"/contributing/style-guide","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/contributing/style-guide#prefer-lower-level-tests-to-end-to-end-tests","content":"Prefer lower-level tests to end-to-end tests"},"995":{"id":"/contributing/style-guide-3","page_id":"/contributing/style-guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/style-guide#panics","content":"Panics are not allowed, especially in code that may run on a server. Calling\n.unwrap() is okay if it's guaranteed to be safe by previous checks or\ndocumented invariants. For example, if a function is documented as requiring\na non-empty slice as input, it's fine to call slice[0] and panic."},"996":{"id":"/contributing/style-guide-4","page_id":"/contributing/style-guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/style-guide#markdown","content":"Try to wrap at 80 columns. We don't have a formatter yet."},"997":{"id":"/contributing/style-guide-5","page_id":"/contributing/style-guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/style-guide#prefer-lower-level-tests-to-end-to-end-tests","content":"When possible, prefer lower-level tests that don't use the jj binary.\nEnd-to-end tests are much slower than similar tests that create a repo using\njj-lib (roughly 100x slower). It's also often easier to test edge cases in\nlower-level tests."},"998":{"id":"/contributing/style-guide-6","page_id":"/contributing/style-guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/style-guide#prefer-lower-level-tests-to-end-to-end-tests","content":"It can still be useful to add a test case or two to check that the lower-level\nfunctionality is correctly hooked up in the CLI. For example, the end-to-end\ntests for jj log don't need to test that all kinds of revsets are evaluated\ncorrectly (we have tests in jj-lib for that), but they should check that the\n-r flag is respected."},"999":{"id":"/contributing/style-guide-7","page_id":"/contributing/style-guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/style-guide#prefer-lower-level-tests-to-end-to-end-tests","content":"Use end-to-end tests for testing the CLI commands themselves."},"1000":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","type":"page","content":"Getting Community Buy-in for Working Group Proposals","tags":[],"url":"/contributing/temporary-voting"},"1001":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-0","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#introduction","content":"Introduction"},"1002":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-1","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#context","content":"Context"},"1003":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-2","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#goals-and-non-goals","content":"Goals and Non-Goals"},"1004":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-3","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#process","content":"Process"},"1005":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-4","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-1-advance-notice-of-effort","content":"Stage 1: Advance Notice of Effort"},"1006":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-5","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-2-proposal-review-period","content":"Stage 2: Proposal Review Period"},"1007":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-6","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"Stage 3: Proposal Voting Period"},"1008":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-7","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-4-implementation","content":"Stage 4: Implementation"},"1009":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-8","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#introduction","content":"We're introducing a temporary process to describe how we'll gain approval to\nadopt permanent governance policies - basically, how we make social and\ntechnical decisions as a community. This temporary process describes how the\ngovernance working group can propose these policies and how community members\ncan influence them and vote on them. Once permanent governance policies are in\nplace, the temporary process will stop being used, and the permanent governance\npolicies will be used instead."},"1010":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-9","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#context","content":"The governance working group was appointed by recommendation from Martin (jj's\noriginal author and current sole maintainer), without recommendation or approval\nfrom the broader jj community. This isn't a problem in itself - but it does\nmean that the governance working group (Austin Seipp/aseipp, Waleed\nKhan/arxanas, Martin von Zweigbergk/martinvonz, and Emily Shaffer/nasamuffin)\nneeds to get some community approval before setting policy for the entire jj\nproject. If we skip this step, we risk being perceived as exercising excessive\ncontrol over the project."},"1011":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-10","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#goals-and-non-goals","content":"This process will be used to approve things like a governance.md (describing\nthe formal structure of governance used for this project), technical design\napproval process, and code review process."},"1012":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-11","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#goals-and-non-goals","content":"This is not a process that will be used forever. It is intended as a\ntemporary process, only used to approve more permanent processes and policies\nfor the project."},"1013":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-12","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#goals-and-non-goals","content":"This process is used to gather feedback, approval, and acceptance from\ninvested jj community members. Current members of the community should be able\nto participate in voting without hardship."},"1014":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-13","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#goals-and-non-goals","content":"Current community members include code committers, code reviewers, those\nproviding user support, those providing quality, actionable feedback, those\nproviding documentation (first-party or third-party), developers of\njj-compatible tools and add-ons (like GUIs or IDE extensions), and those\nproviding design input and feedback."},"1015":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-14","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#goals-and-non-goals","content":"If you feel that you are a member of the community but do not fit into one\nof these buckets, please reach out to one of the members of the working\ngroup to have this list expanded."},"1016":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-15","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#goals-and-non-goals","content":"This process is the primary way for general community members to influence\ngovernance policies and processes. It should invite constructive feedback and\nhelp us form policies that are acceptable to the jj group as a whole."},"1017":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-16","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#goals-and-non-goals","content":"It's intended to meet community members where they are - on GitHub and on\nDiscord, where all development occurs and most support and technical\ndiscussion occurs."},"1018":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-17","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#goals-and-non-goals","content":"This is not a process for gaining unanimous agreement - there are too\nmany of us for that to be feasible. Instead, it is a process for gaining\nwidespread community approval."},"1019":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-18","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-1-advance-notice-of-effort","content":"The working group lets the community know about upcoming policy drafts they're\nintending to share for approval. This must happen at least a week before\nentering stage 3, and ideally should happen even earlier."},"1020":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-19","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-1-advance-notice-of-effort","content":"At this time, the working group should:"},"1021":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-20","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-1-advance-notice-of-effort","content":"Describe why the working group feels this policy is needed"},"1022":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-21","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-1-advance-notice-of-effort","content":"Describe the basic goals the policy should achieve"},"1023":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-22","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-1-advance-notice-of-effort","content":"Describe implementation details that are being considered, if any"},"1024":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-23","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-1-advance-notice-of-effort","content":"Create discussion thread on GitHub (and link to it from Discord). The GitHub\ndiscussion thread is the canonical thread for discussion and will be reused\nthrough the lifetime of a proposal as it moves through this process."},"1025":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-24","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-1-advance-notice-of-effort","content":"At this time, the community is invited to:"},"1026":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-25","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-1-advance-notice-of-effort","content":"Recommend additional goals, or discuss nuances of the stated goals the working\ngroup has already shared"},"1027":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-26","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-1-advance-notice-of-effort","content":"Recommend implementation details"},"1028":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-27","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-1-advance-notice-of-effort","content":"The working group will consider these recommendations in good faith, but may\nchoose not to adopt them."},"1029":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-28","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-2-proposal-review-period","content":"This stage lasts until the working group feels major concerns have been\naddressed and the proposal is ready for a vote. However, at least 72 hours\nmust elapse between the proposal being published and the vote starting, to allow\ncommunity members around the globe to read and comment. Typically, this stage\nshould last at least one week."},"1030":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-29","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-2-proposal-review-period","content":"At this time, the working group should:"},"1031":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-30","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-2-proposal-review-period","content":"Share the full text of the proposal as a GitHub pull request (PR)"},"1032":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-31","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-2-proposal-review-period","content":"Link this GitHub PR to the existing Discord notification thread and GitHub\ndiscussion"},"1033":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-32","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-2-proposal-review-period","content":"Explain how the proposal meets the goals stated in Stage 1, either within the\nproposal itself or in commentary next to the proposal"},"1034":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-33","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-2-proposal-review-period","content":"At this time, the community is invited to:"},"1035":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-34","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-2-proposal-review-period","content":"Share constructive recommendations in GitHub to modify the text of the\nproposal, or discuss nuances of the proposal's wording"},"1036":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-35","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-2-proposal-review-period","content":"Share showstopper concerns in GitHub about the proposal, including details\nabout how and why the concern is especially dire"},"1037":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-36","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-2-proposal-review-period","content":"Think of this like a code review; the goal of this stage is to build a proposal\nthat is representative of the community's will. Keep recommendations actionable\nand constructive: \"This clause discourages X; if we phrase it like \"foo bar baz\"\nit could be less exclusive\" is much more productive than \"It's obvious that the\ngovernance working group doesn't want X!\""},"1038":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-37","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-2-proposal-review-period","content":"At the discretion of the working group, but based on the outcome of the\ndiscussion, the proposal will go to a vote or the proposal will be dropped."},"1039":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-38","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"When the working group feels that major concerns have been addressed and is\nhappy with the text of the proposal, the working group will open voting on the\nproposal."},"1040":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-39","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"Voting occurs on GitHub using the poll feature and is advertised heavily on\nDiscord during the voting period."},"1041":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-40","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"If community members want to vote but aren't able to use GitHub, they can\nmessage nasamuffin@ (on Discord, or nasamuffin at google dot com) with their\nvote to have it manually included. Only one working group member is listed\nin order to avoid accidental double-counting."},"1042":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-41","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"When voting against, community members should comment on the post explaining\nwhy and describe what change would be required for them to abstain or vote\nin favor."},"1043":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-42","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"Generally, assume that the votes may be publicly visible or may be made\npublicly visible at a later time."},"1044":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-43","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"Voting is open for at least 1 week, but may be open as long as 2 weeks when\nappropriate. After that deadline, the GitHub poll will be locked."},"1045":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-44","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"The deadline must be announced at the beginning of the voting period -\nonce voting has begun, the deadline cannot change."},"1046":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-45","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"The working group may set the voting period longer to encompass two\nweekends (for more participation around day jobs), for less urgent or more\ncomplex proposals, or to account for holidays during the voting period."},"1047":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-46","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"Participants can vote in favor or against."},"1048":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-47","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"\"Participants\" means the group of community members as enumerated at the\nbeginning of this document."},"1049":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-48","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"Proposals with 2/3 or more votes in favor at the end of the voting period will\nbe approved."},"1050":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-49","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"After voting has concluded, either:"},"1051":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-50","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"The proposal will be implemented (if accepted)"},"1052":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-51","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"The proposal may be revised and begin again at stage 2 (if rejected)"},"1053":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-52","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"The proposal may be abandoned (if rejected)"},"1054":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-53","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-3-proposal-voting-period","content":"Deciding whether to revise or abandon is up to the discretion of the governance\nworking group. The working group is expected to double-check their assumption\nthat the goals the proposal is attempting to meet are desirable after the\nproposal fails to be accepted."},"1055":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-54","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-4-implementation","content":"Typically, implementation will look like merging the document with the policy\ninto the jj codebase and remembering to use that policy in conversations moving\nforward."},"1056":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-55","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-4-implementation","content":"In some cases, implementation may also involve nomination of individuals to a\ngroup or committee. When this is necessary, expect the policy being proposed to\ndescribe how these individuals will be nominated, both initially and moving into\nthe future."},"1057":{"id":"/contributing/temporary-voting-56","page_id":"/contributing/temporary-voting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/contributing/temporary-voting#stage-4-implementation","content":"It's possible (but unlikely) that during implementation, some obstacle will\narise that means the policy doesn't actually work. If this does happen, expect\nthe working group to be transparent with the community about the situation. We\nmay reuse some of all of this process to figure out how to move forward."},"1058":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"page","content":"Copy Tracking and Tracing Design","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking"},"1059":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-0","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#objective","content":"Objective"},"1060":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-1","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#desired-ux","content":"Desired UX"},"1061":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-2","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#restoring-from-a-commit-should-preserve-copies","content":"Restoring from a commit should preserve copies"},"1062":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-3","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#diff-after-restore","content":"Diff after restore"},"1063":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-4","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#lossless-round-trip-of-rebase","content":"Lossless round-trip of rebase"},"1064":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-5","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#backing-out-the-parent-commit-should-be-a-no-op","content":"Backing out the parent commit should be a no-op"},"1065":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-6","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#parallelizeserialize","content":"Parallelize/serialize"},"1066":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-7","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#copies-inside-merge-commit","content":"Copies inside merge commit"},"1067":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-8","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#copies-across-merge-commit","content":"Copies across merge commit"},"1068":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-9","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#high-level-design","content":"High-level Design"},"1069":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-10","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#diffing","content":"Diffing"},"1070":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-11","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename","content":"Example: Divergent copy and rename"},"1071":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-12","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename-best-rename-target","content":"Example: Divergent copy and rename (best rename target)"},"1072":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-13","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-copy-onto-deleted-file","content":"Example: Copy onto deleted file"},"1073":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-14","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#merging","content":"Merging"},"1074":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-15","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#propagating-changes-across-copies","content":"Propagating changes across copies?"},"1075":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-16","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-propagate-changes-to-copied-file-then-rebase-back","content":"Example: Propagate changes to copied file, then rebase back"},"1076":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-17","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-multiple-copies","content":"Example: Multiple copies"},"1077":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-18","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-convergent-renames","content":"Example: Convergent renames"},"1078":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-19","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-rebasing","content":"Example: Rebasing"},"1079":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-20","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-rename-added-file","content":"Example: Rename added file"},"1080":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-21","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-renames","content":"Example: Divergent renames"},"1081":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-22","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-jonathantanmys-test-case","content":"Example: @jonathantanmy's test case:"},"1082":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-23","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#log","content":"Log"},"1083":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-24","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#annotate","content":"Annotate"},"1084":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-25","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#representation-in-git","content":"Representation in Git"},"1085":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-26","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#representation-in-cloud-repo-eg-google","content":"Representation in cloud repo (e.g. Google)"},"1086":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-27","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#implementation-plan","content":"Implementation plan"},"1087":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-28","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#alternatives-considered","content":"Alternatives considered"},"1088":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-29","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#detect-copies-like-git","content":"Detect copies (like Git)"},"1089":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-30","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#record-logical-file-identifiers-in-trees-bitkeeper-like-model","content":"Record logical file identifiers in trees (BitKeeper-like model)"},"1090":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-31","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#include-copy-info-in-the-fileid-mercurial-like-model","content":"Include copy info in the FileId (Mercurial-like model)"},"1091":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-32","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#hybrid-snapshotpatch-model-with-copy-info-stored-in-commits","content":"Hybrid snapshot/patch model with copy info stored in commits"},"1092":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-33","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","content":"Authors: Daniel Ploch, Martin von Zweigbergk"},"1093":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-34","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","content":"Summary: This Document documents an approach to tracking and detecting copy\ninformation in jj repos, in a way that is compatible with both Git's detection\nmodel and with custom backends that have more complicated tracking of copy\ninformation. This design affects the output of diff commands as well as the\nresults of rebasing across remote copies."},"1094":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-35","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#objective","content":"Add support for copy information that is sufficient for at least the following\nuse cases:"},"1095":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-36","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#objective","content":"Diffing: If a file has been copied, show a diff compared to the source version\ninstead of showing a full addition."},"1096":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-37","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#objective","content":"Merging: When one side of a merge (or rebase) has renamed a file and the other\nside has modified it, propagate the changes to the other side. (There are many\nother cases to handle too.)"},"1097":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-38","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#objective","content":"Log: It should be possible to run something like jj log -p <file> and follow\nthe file backwards when it had been created by copying."},"1098":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-39","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#objective","content":"Annotate (blame): Similar to the log use case, we should follow the file\nbackwards when it had been created by copying."},"1099":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-40","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#objective","content":"The solution should support recording and retrieving copy info in a way that\nis performant both for Git, which synthesizes copy info on the fly between\narbitrary trees, and for custom backends which may explicitly record and\nre-serve copy info over arbitrarily large commit ranges."},"1100":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-41","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#objective","content":"The APIs should be defined in a way that makes it easy for custom backends to\nignore copy info entirely until they are ready to implement it."},"1101":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-42","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#desired-ux","content":"The following sections describe some scenarios and how we would ideally handle\nthem."},"1102":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-43","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#desired-ux","content":"We have not seen much reason to distinguish copies from renames, so a rename\nis simply the same thing as a copy plus a deletion. This means that we cannot\ndistinguish \"copy foo to bar and rename foo to baz\" from \"copy foo\nto baz and rename foo to bar\"."},"1103":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-44","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#restoring-from-a-commit-should-preserve-copies","content":"For example, jj new X--; jj restore --from X should restore any copies\nmade in X- and X into the new working copy. Transitive copies should\nbe \"flattened\". For example, if X- renamed foo to bar and X renamed\nbar to baz, then the restored commit should rename foo to baz."},"1104":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-45","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#restoring-from-a-commit-should-preserve-copies","content":"This also applies to reparenting in general, such as for\n\"verbatim rebase\"."},"1105":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-46","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#diff-after-restore","content":"jj restore --from X; jj diff --from X should be empty, at least when it comes\nto file contents. It may indicate that renamed file have different history."},"1106":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-47","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#lossless-round-trip-of-rebase","content":"Except for the A+(A-B)=A rule, rebasing is currently never\nlossy; rebasing a commit and then rebasing it back yields the same content. We\nshould ideally preserve this property when possible."},"1107":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-48","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#lossless-round-trip-of-rebase","content":"For example:"},"1108":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-49","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#backing-out-the-parent-commit-should-be-a-no-op","content":"Patches should be reversible so you can make a change and then back it out, and\nend up with an empty diff across both commits."},"1109":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-50","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#backing-out-the-parent-commit-should-be-a-no-op","content":"For example:"},"1110":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-51","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#parallelizeserialize","content":"This is a special case of the lossless rebase."},"1111":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-52","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#copies-inside-merge-commit","content":"We should be able to resolve a naming conflict:"},"1112":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-53","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#copies-inside-merge-commit","content":"We should also be able to back out that resolution and get back into the\nname-conflicted state."},"1113":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-54","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#copies-inside-merge-commit","content":"We should be able to rename files that exist on only one side:"},"1114":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-55","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#copies-across-merge-commit","content":"jj diff --from C --to D should now show a baz->bar rename (just like\njj diff --from C --to B would). jj diff --from B --to D should show\nno renames. That's despite there being a rename in C."},"1115":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-56","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#high-level-design","content":"Jujutsu uses a snapshot-based model similar to Git's. The algebra for our\nfirst-class conflicts is also based on snapshots and being able to calculate\npatches as differences between states. That means that we have to fit copy\ninformation into that snapshot-based model too ."},"1116":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-57","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#high-level-design","content":"The proposal is to update tree objects to also contain information about a\nfile's past names. For example, if file foo gets renamed to bar in one\ncommit and then to baz in another commit, we will record that baz previously\nhad names bar and foo."},"1117":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-58","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#high-level-design","content":"To support merging two files into one, the list of past names is actually a DAG.\nMerging can happen in a merge commit when two sides copy/rename different\nsource files to the same target file. By having support for it in the model, we\ncan also support merging multiple files into one in a regular non-merge commit."},"1118":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-59","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#high-level-design","content":"To avoid having to store all past paths in the tree object entry, we will write\nthe copy history as an object and the tree will refer to the object by ID. Each\nID refers to a node in the copy history DAG, similar to how commit IDs refer to\na node in the commit DAG."},"1119":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-60","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#high-level-design","content":"Each node in the copy history DAG stores the path. Having the path in the copy\ngraph can be useful for finding copy sources without having to scan the whole\ntree or having to ask the backend."},"1120":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-61","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#high-level-design","content":"If we use only the file name as only input to the ID, then we get deterministic\ntree IDs. On the other hand, if we add a salt to the copy graph node, then we\ncan represent that a file was rewritten from scratch. For example, a foo might\nhave copy ID 123 in the previous commit and when the file gets rewritten in\nthe current commit, it gets copy ID 456 even though there was no copy from an\nexisting file involved. That makes logical sense, but I'm not sure how useful\nit will be."},"1121":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-62","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#high-level-design","content":"The data structure might look like this:"},"1122":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-63","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#high-level-design","content":"Should we support copy tracking for symlinks? Their history is not very useful\nfor annotation purposes, but knowing the history may at least be useful for\ndetecting directory renames (if all files and symlinks in a directory were\nrenamed)."},"1123":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-64","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#high-level-design","content":"We probably should not support tracking copied directories because it seems\ncomplicated. I haven't spent much thinking about it, so it's also possible that\nit's not that complicated."},"1124":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-65","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#diffing","content":"When diffing two trees, we first diff the trees without considering copy info.\nFor any copy IDs that changed in that diff, we walk all of their copy graphs to\nfigure out how they're related and which source file to associate with which\ndestination file."},"1125":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-66","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#diffing","content":"The details of the algorithm is left for the implementation. The following\nsections provide some examples to hopefully show that it's feasible."},"1126":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-67","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename","content":"Let's look at an example of how this model would look in this scenario:"},"1127":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-68","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename","content":"Assuming the new files are different in each commit, we get the following trees.\nNotation:"},"1128":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-69","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename","content":"idis the hash of the contents (the FileId)"},"1129":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-70","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename","content":"The 2:bar->1:foo means that copy ID 2 (i.e. hash of the CopyHistory\nstruct) has file bar, which was copied from copy ID 1, where it was\ncalled foo."},"1130":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-71","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename","content":"This graph also shows the relationship between the copy IDs and which commits\nthey appear in:"},"1131":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-72","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename","content":"Let's first consider the diff from K to M. Looking at just the trees, that\ndiff finds that copy IDs 1,4,5 were affected. By walking their graphs, we find\n1 and 5 are related, while 4 is not. Considering that copy graph (involving IDs\n1 and 5), since foo doesn't exist in the destination and baz doesn't exist\nin the source, we consider it a rename."},"1132":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-73","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename","content":"Let's now consider the diff from L to M. This is the same as the diff of the\ncommit M2 we'd get by running\njj new L; jj bookmark create M2; jj restore --from M --to M2 (which would\nresult in the commit M2 having the same tree as M). Diffing from L to\nM (or M2) finds 1,2,3,4,5 as changed copy IDs. By walking their graphs, we\nfind that 1,2, and 5 are related, while 3 and 4 are not."},"1133":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-74","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename","content":"The bar and baz files have unrelated copy graphs, i.e. the copy graphs for\nthe bar file in commit L and the bar file in commit M are disjoint, and\nthe same is true for the baz file. Therefore, we break up their diffs into two\nseparate diffs for each file."},"1134":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-75","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename","content":"Among the remaining copy IDs, the shortest path in the copy graph is between\nfoo on the source side and baz on the destination side, so we start with.\nSince foo doesn't exist on the destination side and baz doesn't exist on the\nsource side (with a related copy ID), we consider it a rename."},"1135":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-76","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename","content":"The remaining file is bar on the source side. Its closest relative on the\ndestination side is baz. Since we already used baz as a rename target for\nfoo, we won't consider bar renamed to it. So we consider bar as copied\ninto baz."},"1136":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-77","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename","content":"So we get these diffs:"},"1137":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-78","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename","content":"baz is deleted (deleting content L)"},"1138":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-79","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename","content":"bar is created (with content M)"},"1139":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-80","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename","content":"foo is renamed to baz (showing diff from K to M)"},"1140":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-81","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename","content":"bar is merged into baz (showing diff from L to M)"},"1141":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-82","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename-best-rename-target","content":"When diffing L to N, we find that all files are related. Since bar does\nnot exist in the destination, we should find a rename target to match it with.\nWe pick baz because it's closer in the graph than qux is. So the diff is:"},"1142":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-83","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename-best-rename-target","content":"bar is renamed to baz"},"1143":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-84","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-copy-and-rename-best-rename-target","content":"bar is copied to qux"},"1144":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-85","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-copy-onto-deleted-file","content":"When diffing from K to M, we notice that bar has different and unrelated\ncopy IDs. We present one record saying that bar was deleted, and one record\nsaying that bar was copied from foo."},"1145":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-86","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-copy-onto-deleted-file","content":"When diffing from M to K, we will instead present one record that says that\nbar was created, and one record that says that bar was merged into foo."},"1146":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-87","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#merging","content":"When merging, we need to add a phase before the content-level merging where we\nhandle copies. As before, we start by creating the completely unresolved merged\ntree based on the input trees. To find the relevant copy information, we look at\nthe files changed in each diff and then look up the full copy graph for each.\nFor each copy graph, we can then walk the copy graph to find possible target\npaths, which we then look up in the other side of the merge. If the path exists\nin the tree and has the right copy ID, then we know that the files are related."},"1147":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-88","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#merging","content":"We assume that the differences between the bases and the first term in the\nconflict can be very large, so we don't look at that diff. Assuming that the\ncommit backend can look up the full copy graph based on a given copy ID, we\ndon't need that diff for correctness."},"1148":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-89","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#merging","content":"Once we have found all copies involved in the merge, we analyze them to find\nconflicts, such as when two sides of the merge rename a file to the same\ntarget. If there are conflicts, we leave the trees unchanged. The user can then\nresolve the name conflicts using jj resolve (once we've added support for\nthat). Depending on how slow the naming conflict phase turns out to be, we may\nwant to write a flag to commits indicating that they have unresolved naming\nconflicts, so subsequent calls can avoid that phase."},"1149":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-90","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#merging","content":"When merging trees, we start by rewriting each diff to match any different names\nin the destination tree. For example, if the tree conflict is A+(B-C)+(D-E),\nthen we will rewrite the (B-C) diff and the (D-E) diff to the paths in A.\nTo translate the (B-C) diff, we calculate renames from C to A and then we\napply those renames to both C and B. This may result in conflicts."},"1150":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-91","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#merging","content":"If a file has a conflict in the copy ID, it will appear as if it doesn't exist\nwhen materialized. It will therefore not show up in the working copy until the\nuser has resolved the conflict."},"1151":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-92","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#merging","content":"For example:"},"1152":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-93","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#merging","content":"When rebasing M onto L, we apply the foo->bar rename to the trees in M\nand its parent."},"1153":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-94","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#merging","content":"Another example:"},"1154":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-95","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#merging","content":"When rebasing M onto N, we find the foo->bar rename in N, but since it\nis unrelated to the foo file in M (assuming the foo file created in M\nused a different salt), we will not perform any renames. The new foo file\nis then simply created in the rebased M just like it was before the rebase."},"1155":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-96","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#propagating-changes-across-copies","content":"Should we propagate changes to copies? For example, if you've modified file\nfoo and then rebase it onto a commit that copied foo to bar, should we\napply your change to bar too? Mercurial does that but Git doesn't. It's\nparticularly useful when a file has been split in two. For example, let's say\nyou've made various changes in file foo and then rebase those change onto a\ncommit that split foo into foo1 and foo2 (or foo and bar). If we\npropagate the changes to both files, each change will apply successfully in one\nfile (assuming the changes do not overlap with the split boundary). Each change\nwill have a modify/delete conflicts in the other file. Those can relatively\neasily be resolved in favor of the deleted hunk. If we do not propagate changes,\nthen changes that belong in one of the files will instead only appear as\nmodify/delete conflicts in the first file and you will have to manually copy\nover the changes to the copied file."},"1156":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-97","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#propagating-changes-across-copies","content":"Propagating changes to copies means that rebasing a commit and then rebasing it\nback is no longer a no-op even when ignoring the \"same-change rule\". For\nexample, if your commit modifies file foo and you rebase that commit onto a\ncommit that copied foo to bar, and then you rebase it back, the same change\nwill be applied twice to foo. However, thanks to the same-change rule, we\nwon't consider it a conflict, so maybe it actually works well in practice."},"1157":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-98","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#propagating-changes-across-copies","content":"A third option is to not leave it up to the user whether to propagate the\nchange across the copy. We can do this by leaving the relevant paths in the\ninput trees unchanged in the conflicted commit. Then we will redo the copy\ntracking process every time the commit is inspected. We can have jj resolve\nask the user if they want to propagate the changes to the copy target with a\nsimple yes/no question per copy target."},"1158":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-99","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#propagating-changes-across-copies","content":"Decision: Asking the user about propagating copies seems like the best option.\nIt avoids surprises, and it makes the conflict algebra work in more cases."},"1159":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-100","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-propagate-changes-to-copied-file-then-rebase-back","content":"Let's say we rebase M onto L. Since we decided to not automatically\npropagate changes to copies, we will leave the M+(L-K) tree unresolved (i.e.\nwithout making any changes to the three trees). If the user does not resolve\nthe conflict, and instead rebases L back onto K, the conflict will be\nresolved automatically per the usual conflict simplification."},"1160":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-101","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-multiple-copies","content":"Let's say we rebase M onto N. The changes to foo, foo2, and foo3 will\nthen all apply to foo, which means we get a 4-sided conflict."},"1161":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-102","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-convergent-renames","content":"Consider this \"convergent copy/rename\" scenario:"},"1162":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-103","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-convergent-renames","content":"It seems clear that baz's copy graph should inherit from both foo and bar,\nproducing a merge in copy graph. The trees would look like this:"},"1163":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-104","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-convergent-renames","content":"We used the same content for both foo and bar above to simplify. If they\nhad been different, we would have had a conflict in the contents but the copy\nID would still have been clear."},"1164":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-105","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-rename-added-file","content":"A well-known and thorny problem in Mercurial occurs in the following scenario:"},"1165":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-106","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-rename-added-file","content":"The problem here for Mercurial is that after squashing C into A, the new A has\nfile bar but no record that it used to be called foo. The design proposed\nabove handles this case because we keep the copy ID of bar after squashing,\nso we can detect that the modifications to foo in commit B should be\npropagated to bar."},"1166":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-107","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-renames","content":"Consider this \"divergent rename\" scenario:"},"1167":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-108","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-renames","content":"In this scenario, the regular 3-way merge of the trees without considering copy\ninfo results in a tree without conflicts. However, the user might reasonably\nexpect to have to choose between the bar and baz names. Here's what Git says\nin this scenario:"},"1168":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-109","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-renames","content":"Interestingly, Git seems to represent this state by using index states that\nwould not normally end up in the index as a result of conflicts."},"1169":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-110","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-renames","content":"Here's what Mercurial says:"},"1170":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-111","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-renames","content":"Mercurial doesn't have a place to record this state, so it just prints that\nnote and leaves it at that."},"1171":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-112","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-divergent-renames","content":"The model and algorithm described in this document would result in a conflict\nin the copy ID at both paths after propagating the renames."},"1172":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-113","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-jonathantanmys-test-case","content":"TODO: fill this out"},"1173":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-114","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#example-jonathantanmys-test-case","content":"If F is empty (auto-merged), it should have the same state as E before."},"1174":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-115","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#log","content":"The copy graph contains all past paths and copy IDs of a file, so when doing\njj log <filename>, we might want to translate that to a revset that's similar\nto files() but matches specific (path, copy ID) pairs instead of specific\npaths."},"1175":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-116","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#annotate","content":"TBD"},"1176":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-117","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#representation-in-git","content":"Do we ever want to record renames in the Git backend? If we do, we would\npresumably store it outside the Git object, similar to how we store the change\nid for commits."},"1177":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-118","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#representation-in-git","content":"What do we use for trees where we don't have any copy graph recorded? If we\nsimply create a new copy graph based on the current path, then the caller will\nnever find any copies. Do we need an indexing pass to detect all renames in a\nrepo when running jj git init? That can be very expensive for large repos.\nFor reference, git log --summary --find-copies-harder takes about 165 seconds\nin the git.git repo on my computer, and about 13 hours in the Nixpkgs repo."},"1178":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-119","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#representation-in-git","content":"An alternative is to do copy indexing in the background after cloning a repo.\nThat would mean that copy information would not show up until some time later.\nIt would also be more work to implement it this way."},"1179":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-120","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#representation-in-git","content":"How to deal with two trees having the same content but different file ids?\nActually store the additional data linked from the commit object? That would\nnot work if we point to trees from somewhere that's not a commit. We point to a\ntree from the working-copy state."},"1180":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-121","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#representation-in-git","content":"One could imagine not storing any copy info in Git and instead making the model\ndescribed above an implementation detail of the backend. Then it could be used\nby the native backend and the Google backend, while we still use on-the-fly\ncopy detection in the Git backend. However, if we want to be able to tell the\nuser about details of conflicting copy IDs so they can decide how to resolve\nsuch conflicts, then we would have to somehow represent that abstractly too."},"1181":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-122","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#representation-in-cloud-repo-eg-google","content":"Let's say you have a commit with some files you've modified. You now want to\nsync (rebase) that to an updated main branch. If some of the files you modified\nno longer exist on the main branch, we want to figure out if they were renamed\nso we should propagate your changes to the new file location. As described\nearlier, we can do that by finding files that have a different copy ID since the\nlast time you synced with the main branch. However, if there are 10 million new\ncommits on the main branch, there's perhaps tens of thousands of such files\nspread across the entire tree. That can therefore can be very expensive to\ncalculate. We therefore need to be able to get help from a custom backend\nimplementation with this query."},"1182":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-123","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#representation-in-cloud-repo-eg-google","content":"Since we are only interested in copy graphs that involve files modified in the\nrebased commit, it should be sufficient if the backend provides a method to\nfetch the whole copy graph for a given copy ID (or list of copy IDs). We would\nthen first find all copy IDs involved in the diff of the rebased commit. Then\nwe query the backend to get the full copy graphs. We then need to walk the copy\ngraphs to see if a node exists in the destination tree."},"1183":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-124","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#representation-in-cloud-repo-eg-google","content":"A weakness of this solution is that the search gets expensive if there are very\nmany related files. That's probably not much of a problem in practice. The\nserver might want to populate the the index only for public/immutable commits.\nOtherwise, a user could poison the index by creating tons of copies\n(intentionally or by mistake), which would make all future queries about those\nfiles expensive."},"1184":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-125","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#implementation-plan","content":"A rough implementation plan may look like this:"},"1185":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-126","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#implementation-plan","content":"Implement support for copy-tracking in the test backend"},"1186":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-127","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#implementation-plan","content":"Implement diff algorithm and test it"},"1187":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-128","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#implementation-plan","content":"Implement merge algorithm and test it"},"1188":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-129","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#implementation-plan","content":"Implement blame algorithm and test it"},"1189":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-130","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#implementation-plan","content":"Implement file-following log algorithm and test it"},"1190":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-131","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#implementation-plan","content":"Extract some queries to the commit backend trait so cloud-based backends\n(like the Google backend) can provide versions implemented using database\nindexes"},"1191":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-132","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#implementation-plan","content":"Implement support for copy-tracking in the Git backend. This may involve\nbackfilling, possibly lazily. Or it may involve new abstractions in the\ncommit backend trait."},"1192":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-133","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#implementation-plan","content":"Implement CLI for recording copies and for resolving conflicts in copies"},"1193":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-134","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#detect-copies-like-git","content":"Git doesn't record copy info. Instead, it infers it when comparing two trees."},"1194":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-135","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#detect-copies-like-git","content":"It's hard to make this model scale to very large repos. For example, let's say\nyou're rebasing your local commit to a new upstream commit that's 1 million\ncommits ahead. We would then want to find if any of the files in your local\ncommit has been copied upstream. That's very expensive to do by comparing the\nold and the new base trees. However, since the query APIs defined above take\ncommits (not trees) as input, we allow the backend to take the history into\naccount when calculating the copies. A backend can then create an index based\non the input files (in your local commit) and find if it's been copied without\ncomparing the full trees."},"1195":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-136","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#record-logical-file-identifiers-in-trees-bitkeeper-like-model","content":"BitKeeper records a file ID (which identifies a logical file, unlike our FileId\ntype) for each path (or maybe it's a path for each file ID). That way you can\ncompare two arbitrary trees, find the added and deleted files and just compare\nthe file IDs to figure out which of them are renames."},"1196":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-137","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#record-logical-file-identifiers-in-trees-bitkeeper-like-model","content":"This model doesn't seem to be easily extensible to support copies (it only\nsupports renames)."},"1197":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-138","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#record-logical-file-identifiers-in-trees-bitkeeper-like-model","content":"To perform a rebase across millions of commits, we would not want to diff the\nfull trees because that would be too expensive (probably millions of modified\nfiles). We could perhaps instead find renames by bisecting to find commits that\ndeleted any of the files modified in the commit we're rebasing."},"1198":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-139","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#record-logical-file-identifiers-in-trees-bitkeeper-like-model","content":"Another problem is how to synthesize the file IDs in the Git backend. That could\nperhaps be done by walking from the root commits and persisting an index."},"1199":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-140","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#include-copy-info-in-the-fileid-mercurial-like-model","content":"Mercurial stores copy info in a metadata section in the file content itself\n. That means that a file will get a new file\n(content) ID if its copy history changes. That's quite similar to the proposal\nin this document. One difference is that Mercurial's model stores information\nonly about the most recent copy. If the file is then modified, it will get a new\nfile ID. One therefore has to walk the history of the file to find the previous\nname (which is usually not much of a problem because Mercurial stores a revision\nDAG per file in addition to the revision DAG at the commit level)."},"1200":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-141","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#hybrid-snapshotpatch-model-with-copy-info-stored-in-commits","content":"We considered storing copy info about the copies/renames in the commit object.\nThat has some significant impact on the data model:"},"1201":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-142","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#hybrid-snapshotpatch-model-with-copy-info-stored-in-commits","content":"Without copy info, if there's a linear chain of commits A..D, you can find\nthe total diff by diffing just D-A. That works because (B-A)+(C-B)+(D-C)\nsimplifies to just D-A. However, if there is copy info, the total diff will\ninvolve copy info. If that's associated with the individual commits, we will\nneed to aggregate it somehow."},"1202":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-143","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#hybrid-snapshotpatch-model-with-copy-info-stored-in-commits","content":"Restoring from another tree is no longer just a matter of copying that tree;\nwe also need to figure out copies between the old tree and the new tree."},"1203":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-144","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#hybrid-snapshotpatch-model-with-copy-info-stored-in-commits","content":"Conflict states are represented by a series of states to add and remove. This\ndoes not work with the patch-based copy info. We spent a lot of time trying\nto figure out a solution that works, but it seems like the snapshot-based\nconflict model and the patch-based copy info model are not reconcilable.\nTherefore, we won't track conflicted copy info, such as between a foo->baz\nrename and a bar->baz rename."},"1204":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-145","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#hybrid-snapshotpatch-model-with-copy-info-stored-in-commits","content":"Since copy records are relative to the auto-merged parents, that unfortunately\nmeans that the records will depend on the merge algorithm, so it's possible\nthat a future change to the merge algorithm will make some copy records\ninvalid. We will therefore need to not assume that the copy source exists."},"1205":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-146","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#hybrid-snapshotpatch-model-with-copy-info-stored-in-commits","content":"For the state in conflicted commits, we considered using a representation like\nthis:"},"1206":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-147","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#hybrid-snapshotpatch-model-with-copy-info-stored-in-commits","content":"That works for calculating the resulting tree, but it does not seem to allow for\ndoing the conflict algebra we currently do. That means that things like\nparallelizing commits and then serializing them again would lose copy\ninformation."},"1207":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-148","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#hybrid-snapshotpatch-model-with-copy-info-stored-in-commits","content":"This took me (@martinvonz) months to really understand."},"1208":{"id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking-149","page_id":"/design-docs/copy-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/copy-tracking#hybrid-snapshotpatch-model-with-copy-info-stored-in-commits","content":"From around\nhttps://repo.mercurial-scm.org/hg/rev/49ad315b39ee, Mercurial also\nsupports storing copy info in commits. That made it the kind of\nsnapshot/patch model we described above as not working well."},"1209":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","type":"page","content":"Git submodule storage","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage"},"1210":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-0","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#objective","content":"Objective"},"1211":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-1","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#use-cases-to-consider","content":"Use cases to consider"},"1212":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-2","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#fetching-submodule-commits","content":"Fetching submodule commits"},"1213":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-3","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#jj-op-restore-and-operation-log-format","content":"\"jj op restore\" and operation log format"},"1214":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-4","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#nested-submodules","content":"Nested submodules"},"1215":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-5","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#supporting-future-extensions","content":"Supporting future extensions"},"1216":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-6","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#proposed-design","content":"Proposed design"},"1217":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-7","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#fetching-submodule-commits-1","content":"Fetching submodule commits"},"1218":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-8","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#jj-op-restore-and-operation-log-format-1","content":"\"jj op restore\" and operation log format"},"1219":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-9","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#nested-submodules-1","content":"Nested submodules"},"1220":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-10","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#extending-to-colocated-git-repos","content":"Extending to colocated Git repos"},"1221":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-11","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#alternatives-considered","content":"Alternatives considered"},"1222":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-12","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#git-repos-in-the-main-git-backend","content":"Git repos in the main Git backend"},"1223":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-13","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#store-git-submodules-as-alternate-git-backends","content":"Store Git submodules as alternate Git backends"},"1224":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-14","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#objective","content":"Decide what approach(es) to Git submodule storage we should pursue.\nThe decision will be recorded in /git-submodules."},"1225":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-15","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#use-cases-to-consider","content":"The submodule storage format should support the workflows specified in the\nsubmodules roadmap. It should be obvious how \"Phase 1\"\nrequirements will be supported, and we should have an idea of how \"Phases 2,3,X\"\nmight be supported."},"1226":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-16","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#use-cases-to-consider","content":"Notable use cases and workflows are noted below."},"1227":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-17","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#fetching-submodule-commits","content":"Git's protocol is designed for communicating between copies of the same\nrepository. Notably, a Git fetch calculates the list of required objects by\nperforming reachability checks between the refs on the local and the remote\nside. We should expect that this will only work well if the submodule repository\nis stored as a local Git repository."},"1228":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-18","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#fetching-submodule-commits","content":"Rolling our own Git fetch is too complex to be worth the effort."},"1229":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-19","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#jj-op-restore-and-operation-log-format","content":"We want jj op restore to restore to an \"expected\" state in the submodule.\nThere is a potential distinction between running jj op restore in the\nsuperproject vs in the submodule, and the expected behavior may be different in\neach case, e.g. in the superproject, it might be enough to restore the submodule\nworking copy, but in the submodule, refs also need to be restored."},"1230":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-20","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#jj-op-restore-and-operation-log-format","content":"Currently, the operation log only references objects and refs in the\nsuperproject, so it is likely that proposed approaches will need to extend this\nformat. It is also worth considering that submodules may be added, updated or\nremoved in superproject commits, thus the list of submodules is likely to change\nover the repository's lifetime."},"1231":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-21","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#nested-submodules","content":"Git submodules may contain submodules themselves, so our chosen storage schemes\nshould support that."},"1232":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-22","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#nested-submodules","content":"We should consider limiting the recursion depth to avoid nasty edge cases (e.g.\ncyclical submodules.) that might surprise users."},"1233":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-23","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#supporting-future-extensions","content":"There are certain extensions we may want to make in the future, but we don't\nhave a timeline for them today. Proposed approaches should take these\nextensions into account (e.g. the approach should be theoretically extensible),\nbut a full proposal for implementing them is not necessary."},"1234":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-24","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#supporting-future-extensions","content":"These extensions are:"},"1235":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-25","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#supporting-future-extensions","content":"Non-git subrepos"},"1236":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-26","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#supporting-future-extensions","content":"Colocated Git repos"},"1237":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-27","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#supporting-future-extensions","content":"The superproject using a non-git backend"},"1238":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-28","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#proposed-design","content":"Git submodules will be stored as full jj repos. In the code, jj commands will\nonly interact with the submodule's repo as an entire unit, e.g. it cannot query\nthe submodule's commit backend directly. A well-abstracted submodule will extend\nwell to non-git backends and non-git subrepos."},"1239":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-29","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#proposed-design","content":"The main challenge with this approach is that the submodule repo can be in a\nstate that is internally valid (when considering only the submodule's repo), but\ninvalid when considering the superproject-submodule system. This will be managed\nby requiring all submodule interactions go through the superproject so that\nsuperproject-submodule coordination can occur. For example, jj will not allow\nthe user to work on the submodule's repo without going through the superproject\n(unlike Git)."},"1240":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-30","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#proposed-design","content":"The notable workflows could be addressed like so:"},"1241":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-31","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#fetching-submodule-commits-1","content":"The submodule would fetch using the equivalent of jj git fetch. It remains to\nbe decided how a \"recursive\" fetch should work, especially if a newly fetched\nsuperproject commit references an unfetched submodule commit. A reasonable\napproximation would be to fetch all branches in the submodule, and then, if the\nsubmodule commit is still missing, gracefully handle it."},"1242":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-32","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#jj-op-restore-and-operation-log-format-1","content":"As full repos, each submodule will have its own operation log. We will continue\nto use the existing operation log format, where each operation log tracks their\nown repo's commits. As commands are run in the superproject, corresponding\ncommands will be run in the submodule as necessary, e.g. checking out a\nsuperproject commit will cause a submodule commit to also be checked out."},"1243":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-33","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#jj-op-restore-and-operation-log-format-1","content":"Since there is no association between a superproject operation and a submodule\noperation, jj op restore in the superproject will not restore the submodule to\na previous operation. Instead, the appropriate submodule operation(s) will be\ncreated. This is sufficient to preserve the superproject-submodule relationship;\nit precludes \"recursive\" restore (e.g. restoring branches in the superproject\nand submodules) but it seems unlikely that we will need such a thing."},"1244":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-34","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#nested-submodules-1","content":"Since submodules are full repos, they can contain submodules themselves. Nesting\nis unlikely to complicate any of the core features, since the top-level\nsuperproject/submodule relationship is almost identical to the submodule/nested\nsubmodule relationship."},"1245":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-35","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#extending-to-colocated-git-repos","content":"Git expects submodules to be in .git/modules, so it will not understand this\nstorage format. To support colocated Git repos, we will have to change Git to\nallow a submodule's gitdir to be in an alternate location (e.g. we could add a\nnew submodule.<name>.gitdir config option). This is a simple change, so it\nshould be feasible."},"1246":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-36","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#git-repos-in-the-main-git-backend","content":"Since the Git backend contains a Git repository, an 'obvious' default would be\nto store them in the Git superproject the same way Git does, i.e. in\n.git/modules. Since Git submodules are full repositories that can have\nsubmodules, this storage scheme naturally extends to nested submodules."},"1247":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-37","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#git-repos-in-the-main-git-backend","content":"Most of the work in storing submodules and querying them would be well-isolated\nto the Git backend, which gives us a lot of flexibility to make changes without\naffecting the rest of jj. However, the operation log will need a significant\nrework since it isn't designed to reference submodules, and handling edge cases\n(e.g. a submodule being added/removed, nested submodules) will be tricky."},"1248":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-38","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#git-repos-in-the-main-git-backend","content":"This is rejected because handling that operation log complexity isn't worth it\nwhen very little of the work extends to non-Git backends."},"1249":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-39","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#store-git-submodules-as-alternate-git-backends","content":"Teach jj to use multiple commit backends and store Git submodules as Git\nbackends. Since submodules are separate from the 'main' backend, a repository\ncan use whatever backend it wants as its 'main' one, while still having Git\nsubmodules in the 'alternate' Git backends."},"1250":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage-40","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodule-storage#store-git-submodules-as-alternate-git-backends","content":"This approach extends fairly well to non-Git submodules (which would be stored\nin non-Git commit backends). However, this requires significantly reworking the\noperation log to account for multiple commit backends. It is also not clear how\nnested submodules will be supported since there isn't an obvious way to\nrepresent a nested submodule's relationship to its superproject."},"1251":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"page","content":"Git submodules","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules"},"1252":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-0","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#objective","content":"Objective"},"1253":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-1","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#non-goals","content":"Non-goals"},"1254":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-2","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#background","content":"Background"},"1255":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-3","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#intro-to-git-submodules","content":"Intro to Git Submodules"},"1256":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-4","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#roadmap","content":"Roadmap"},"1257":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-5","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#phase-1-readonly-submodules","content":"Phase 1: Readonly submodules"},"1258":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-6","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#outcomes","content":"Outcomes"},"1259":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-7","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#phase-2-snapshotting-new-changes","content":"Phase 2: Snapshotting new changes"},"1260":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-8","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#outcomes-1","content":"Outcomes"},"1261":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-9","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#phase-3-mergingrebasingconflicts","content":"Phase 3: Merging/rebasing/conflicts"},"1262":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-10","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#outcomes-2","content":"Outcomes"},"1263":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-11","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#phase--an-ideal-world","content":"Phase ?: An ideal world"},"1264":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-12","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#design","content":"Design"},"1265":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-13","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#guiding-principles","content":"Guiding principles"},"1266":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-14","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#storing-submodules","content":"Storing submodules"},"1267":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-15","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#snapshotting-new-submodule-changes","content":"Snapshotting new submodule changes"},"1268":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-16","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#mergingrebasing-with-submodules","content":"Merging/rebasing with submodules"},"1269":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-17","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules","content":"This is an aspirational document that describes how jj will support Git\nsubmodules. Readers are assumed to have some familiarity with Git and Git\nsubmodules."},"1270":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-18","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules","content":"This document is a work in progress; submodules are a big feature, and relevant\ndetails will be filled in incrementally."},"1271":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-19","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#objective","content":"This proposal aims to replicate the workflows users are used to with Git\nsubmodules, e.g.:"},"1272":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-20","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#objective","content":"Cloning submodules"},"1273":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-21","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#objective","content":"Making new submodule commits and updating the superproject"},"1274":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-22","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#objective","content":"Fetching and pushing updates to the submodule's remote"},"1275":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-23","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#objective","content":"Viewing submodule history"},"1276":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-24","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#objective","content":"When it is convenient, this proposal will also aim to make submodules easier to\nuse than Git's implementation."},"1277":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-25","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#non-goals","content":"Non-Git 'submodules' (e.g. native jj submodules, other VCSes)"},"1278":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-26","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#non-goals","content":"Non-Git backends (e.g. Google internal backend)"},"1279":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-27","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#non-goals","content":"Changing how Git submodules are implemented in Git"},"1280":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-28","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#background","content":"We mainly want to support Git submodules for feature parity, since Git\nsubmodules are a standard feature in Git and are popular enough that we have\nreceived user requests for them. Secondarily (and distantly so), Git submodules\nare notoriously difficult to use, so there is an opportunity to improve the UX\nover Git's implementation."},"1281":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-29","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#intro-to-git-submodules","content":"Git submodules are a feature of Git\nthat allow a repository (submodule) to be embedded inside another repository\n(the superproject). Notably, a submodule is a full repository, complete with its\nown index, object store and ref store. It can be interacted with like any other\nrepository, regardless of the superproject."},"1282":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-30","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#intro-to-git-submodules","content":"In a superproject commit, submodule information is captured in two places:"},"1283":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-31","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#intro-to-git-submodules","content":"A gitlink entry in the commit's tree, where the value of the gitlink entry\nis the submodule commit id. This tells Git what to populate in the working\ntree."},"1284":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-32","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#intro-to-git-submodules","content":"A top level .gitmodules file. This file is in Git's config syntax and\nentries take the form submodule.<submodule-name>.*. These include many\nsettings about the submodules, but most importantly:"},"1285":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-33","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#intro-to-git-submodules","content":"submodule<submodule-name>.path contains the path from the root of the tree\nto the gitlink being described."},"1286":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-34","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#intro-to-git-submodules","content":"submodule<submodule-name>.url contains the url to clone the submodule\nfrom."},"1287":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-35","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#intro-to-git-submodules","content":"In the working tree, Git notices the presence of a submodule by the .git entry\n(signifying the root of a Git repository working tree). This is either the\nsubmodule's actual Git directory (an \"old-form\" submodule), or a .git file\npointing to <superproject-git-directory>/modules/<submodule-name>. The latter\nis sometimes called the \"absorbed form\", and is Git's preferred mode of\noperation."},"1288":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-36","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#roadmap","content":"Git submodules should be implemented in an order that supports an increasing set\nof workflows, with the goal of getting feedback early and often. When support is\nincomplete, jj should not crash, but instead provide fallback behavior and warn\nthe user where needed."},"1289":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-37","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#roadmap","content":"The goal is to land good support for pure Jujutsu repositories, while colocated\nrepositories will be supported when convenient."},"1290":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-38","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#roadmap","content":"This section should be treated as a set of guidelines, not a strict order of\nwork."},"1291":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-39","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#phase-1-readonly-submodules","content":"This includes work that inspects submodule contents but does not create new\nobjects in the submodule. This requires a way to store submodules in a jj\nrepository that supports readonly operations."},"1292":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-40","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#outcomes","content":"Submodules can be cloned anew"},"1293":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-41","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#outcomes","content":"New submodule commits can be fetched"},"1294":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-42","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#outcomes","content":"Submodule history and branches can be viewed"},"1295":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-43","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#outcomes","content":"Submodule contents are populated in the working copy"},"1296":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-44","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#outcomes","content":"Superproject gitlink can be updated to an existing submodule commit"},"1297":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-45","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#outcomes","content":"Conflicts in the superproject gitlink can be resolved to an existing submodule\ncommit"},"1298":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-46","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#phase-2-snapshotting-new-changes","content":"This allows a user to write new contents to a submodule and its remote."},"1299":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-47","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#outcomes-1","content":"Changes in the working copy can be recorded in a submodule commit"},"1300":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-48","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#outcomes-1","content":"Submodule branches can be modified"},"1301":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-49","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#outcomes-1","content":"Submodules and their branches can be pushed to their remote"},"1302":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-50","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#phase-3-mergingrebasingconflicts","content":"This allows merging and rebasing of superproject commits in a content-aware way\n(in contrast to Git, where only the gitlink commit ids are compared), as well as\nworkflows that make resolving conflicts easy and sensible."},"1303":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-51","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#phase-3-mergingrebasingconflicts","content":"This can be done in tandem with Phase 2, but will likely require a significant\namount of design work on its own."},"1304":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-52","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#outcomes-2","content":"Merged/rebased submodules result in merged/rebased working copy content"},"1305":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-53","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#outcomes-2","content":"Merged/rebased working copy content can be committed, possibly by creating\nsensible merged/rebased submodule commits"},"1306":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-54","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#outcomes-2","content":"Merge/rebase between submodule and non-submodule gives a sensible result"},"1307":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-55","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#outcomes-2","content":"Merge/rebase between submodule A and submodule B gives a sensible result"},"1308":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-56","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#phase--an-ideal-world","content":"I.e. outcomes we would like to see if there were no constraints whatsoever."},"1309":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-57","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#phase--an-ideal-world","content":"Rewriting submodule commits rewrites descendants correctly and updates\nsuperproject gitlinks."},"1310":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-58","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#phase--an-ideal-world","content":"Submodule conflicts automatically resolve to the 'correct' submodule commits,\ne.g. a merge between superproject commits creating a merge of the submodule\ncommits."},"1311":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-59","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#phase--an-ideal-world","content":"Nested submodules are as easy to work with as non-nested submodules."},"1312":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-60","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#phase--an-ideal-world","content":"The operation log captures changes in the submodule."},"1313":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-61","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#guiding-principles","content":"TODO"},"1314":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-62","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#storing-submodules","content":"Possible approaches under discussion. See\n/git-submodule-storage."},"1315":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-63","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#snapshotting-new-submodule-changes","content":"TODO"},"1316":{"id":"/design-docs/git-submodules-64","page_id":"/design-docs/git-submodules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/git-submodules#mergingrebasing-with-submodules","content":"TODO"},"1317":{"id":"/design-docs/run","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"page","content":"Introducing JJ run","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run"},"1318":{"id":"/design-docs/run-0","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#preface","content":"Preface"},"1319":{"id":"/design-docs/run-1","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#context-and-scope","content":"Context and Scope"},"1320":{"id":"/design-docs/run-2","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#goals-and-non-goals","content":"Goals and Non-Goals"},"1321":{"id":"/design-docs/run-3","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#goals","content":"Goals"},"1322":{"id":"/design-docs/run-4","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#non-goals","content":"Non-Goals"},"1323":{"id":"/design-docs/run-5","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#use-cases-of-jj-run","content":"Use-Cases of jj run"},"1324":{"id":"/design-docs/run-6","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#design","content":"Design"},"1325":{"id":"/design-docs/run-7","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#base-design","content":"Base Design"},"1326":{"id":"/design-docs/run-8","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#modifying-the-working-copy","content":"Modifying the Working Copy"},"1327":{"id":"/design-docs/run-9","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#modifying-the-repo","content":"Modifying the Repo"},"1328":{"id":"/design-docs/run-10","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#rewriting-the-revisions","content":"Rewriting the revisions"},"1329":{"id":"/design-docs/run-11","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#execution-orderparallelism","content":"Execution order/parallelism"},"1330":{"id":"/design-docs/run-12","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#dealing-with-failure","content":"Dealing with failure"},"1331":{"id":"/design-docs/run-13","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#resource-constraints","content":"Resource constraints"},"1332":{"id":"/design-docs/run-14","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#command-options","content":"Command Options"},"1333":{"id":"/design-docs/run-15","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#integrating-with-other-commands","content":"Integrating with other commands"},"1334":{"id":"/design-docs/run-16","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#open-points","content":"Open Points"},"1335":{"id":"/design-docs/run-17","page_id":"/design-docs/run","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/run#future-possibilities","content":"Future possibilities"},"1336":{"id":"/design-docs/run-18","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run","content":"Authors: Philip Metzger, Martin von Zweigberk, Danny Hooper, Waleed Khan"},"1337":{"id":"/design-docs/run-19","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run","content":"Initial Version, 10.12.2022 (view full history here)"},"1338":{"id":"/design-docs/run-20","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run","content":"Summary: This Document documents the design of a new run command for\nJujutsu which will be used to seamlessly integrate with build systems, linters\nand formatters. This is achieved by running a user-provided command or script\nacross multiple revisions. For more details, read the\nUse-Cases of jj run."},"1339":{"id":"/design-docs/run-21","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#preface","content":"The goal of this Design Document is to specify the correct behavior of jj run.\nThe points we decide on here I (Philip Metzger) will try to implement. There\nexists some prior work in other DVCS:"},"1340":{"id":"/design-docs/run-22","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#preface","content":"git test: part of git-branchless. Similar to this proposal for jj run."},"1341":{"id":"/design-docs/run-23","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#preface","content":"hg run: Google's internal Mercurial extension. Similar to this proposal for\njj run.\nDetails not available."},"1342":{"id":"/design-docs/run-24","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#preface","content":"hg fix: Google's open source Mercurial extension: source code. A\nmore specialized approach to rewriting file content without full context of the\nworking directory."},"1343":{"id":"/design-docs/run-25","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#preface","content":"git rebase -x: runs commands opportunistically as part of rebase."},"1344":{"id":"/design-docs/run-26","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#preface","content":"git bisect run: run a command to determine which commit introduced a bug."},"1345":{"id":"/design-docs/run-27","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#context-and-scope","content":"The initial need for some kind of command runner integrated in the VCS, surfaced\nin a github discussion. In a discussion on discord about\nthe git-hook model, there was consensus about not repeating their mistakes."},"1346":{"id":"/design-docs/run-28","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#context-and-scope","content":"For jj run there is prior art in Mercurial, git branchless and Google's\ninternal Mercurial. Currently git-branchless git test and hg fix implement\nsome kind of command runner. The Google internal hg run works in conjunction\nwith CitC (Clients in the Cloud) which allows it to lazily apply the current\ncommand to any affected file. Currently no open-source Jujutsu backend (Git,\nSimple) has a fancy virtual filesystem supporting it, so we can't apply this\noptimization. We could do the same once we have an implementation of the working\ncopy based on a virtual file system. Until then, we have to run the commands in\nregular local-disk working copies."},"1347":{"id":"/design-docs/run-29","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#goals","content":"We should be able to apply the command to any revision, published or unpublished."},"1348":{"id":"/design-docs/run-30","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#goals","content":"We should be able to parallelize running the actual command, while preserving a\ngood console output."},"1349":{"id":"/design-docs/run-31","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#goals","content":"The run command should be able to work in any commit, the working-copy commit\nitself or any other commit."},"1350":{"id":"/design-docs/run-32","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#goals","content":"There should exist some way to signal hard failure."},"1351":{"id":"/design-docs/run-33","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#goals","content":"The command should build enough infrastructure for jj test, jj fix and\njj format."},"1352":{"id":"/design-docs/run-34","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#goals","content":"The main goal is to be good enough, as we can always expand the functionality\nin the future."},"1353":{"id":"/design-docs/run-35","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#non-goals","content":"While we should build a base for jj test, jj format and jj fix, we\nshouldn't mash their use-cases into jj run."},"1354":{"id":"/design-docs/run-36","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#non-goals","content":"The command shouldn't be too smart, as too many assumptions about workflows\nmakes the command confusing for users."},"1355":{"id":"/design-docs/run-37","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#non-goals","content":"The smart caching of outputs, as user input commands can be unpredictable.\nmakes the command confusing for users."},"1356":{"id":"/design-docs/run-38","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#non-goals","content":"Avoid the smart caching of outputs, as user input commands can be\nunpredictable."},"1357":{"id":"/design-docs/run-39","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#non-goals","content":"Fine grained user facing configuration, as it's unwarranted complexity."},"1358":{"id":"/design-docs/run-40","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#non-goals","content":"A fix subcommand as it cuts too much design space."},"1359":{"id":"/design-docs/run-41","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#use-cases-of-jj-run","content":"Linting and Formatting:"},"1360":{"id":"/design-docs/run-42","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#use-cases-of-jj-run","content":"jj run 'pre-commit run' -r $revset"},"1361":{"id":"/design-docs/run-43","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#use-cases-of-jj-run","content":"jj run 'cargo clippy' -r $revset"},"1362":{"id":"/design-docs/run-44","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#use-cases-of-jj-run","content":"jj run 'cargo +nightly fmt'"},"1363":{"id":"/design-docs/run-45","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#use-cases-of-jj-run","content":"Large scale changes across repositories, local and remote:"},"1364":{"id":"/design-docs/run-46","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#use-cases-of-jj-run","content":"jj run 'sed /some/test/' -r 'mine() & ~remote_bookmarks(exact:\"origin\")'"},"1365":{"id":"/design-docs/run-47","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#use-cases-of-jj-run","content":"jj run '$rewrite-tool' -r '$revset'"},"1366":{"id":"/design-docs/run-48","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#use-cases-of-jj-run","content":"Build systems:"},"1367":{"id":"/design-docs/run-49","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#use-cases-of-jj-run","content":"jj run 'bazel build //some/target:somewhere'"},"1368":{"id":"/design-docs/run-50","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#use-cases-of-jj-run","content":"jj run 'ninja check-lld'"},"1369":{"id":"/design-docs/run-51","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#use-cases-of-jj-run","content":"Some of these use-cases should get a specialized command, as this allows\nfurther optimization. A command could be jj format, which runs a list of\nformatters over a subset of a file in a revision. Another command could be\njj fix, which runs a command like rustfmt --fix or cargo clippy --fix over\na subset of a file in a revision."},"1370":{"id":"/design-docs/run-52","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#base-design","content":"All the work will be done in the .jj/ directory. This allows us to hide all\ncomplexity from the users, while preserving the user's current workspace."},"1371":{"id":"/design-docs/run-53","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#base-design","content":"We will copy the approach from git-branchless's git test of creating a\ntemporary working copy for each parallel command. The working copies will be\nreused between jj run invocations. They will also be reused within jj run\ninvocation if there are more commits to run on than there are parallel jobs."},"1372":{"id":"/design-docs/run-54","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#base-design","content":"We will leave ignored files in the temporary directory between runs. That\nenables incremental builds (e.g. by letting cargo reuse its target/\ndirectory). However, it also means that runs potentially become less\nreproducible. We will provide a flag for removing ignored files from the\ntemporary working copies to address that."},"1373":{"id":"/design-docs/run-55","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#base-design","content":"Another problem with leaving ignored files in the temporary directories is that\nthey take up space. That is especially problematic in the case of cargo (the\ntarget/ directory often takes up tens of GBs). The same flag for cleaning up\nignored files can be used to address that. We may want to also have a flag for\ncleaning up temporary working copies after running the command."},"1374":{"id":"/design-docs/run-56","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#base-design","content":"An early version of the command will directly use Treestate to\nto manage the temporary working copies. That means that running jj inside the\ntemporary working copies will not work . We can later extend that to use a full\nWorkspace. To prevent operations in the working copies from\nimpacting the repo, we can use a separate OpHeadsStore for it."},"1375":{"id":"/design-docs/run-57","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#modifying-the-working-copy","content":"Since the subprocesses will run in temporary working copies, they\nwon't interfere with the user's working copy. The user can therefore continue\nto work in it while jj run is running."},"1376":{"id":"/design-docs/run-58","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#modifying-the-working-copy","content":"We want subprocesses to be able to make changes to the repo by updating their\nassigned working copy. Let's say the user runs jj run on just commits A and\nB, where B's parent is A. Any changes made on top of A would be squashed into\nA, forming A'. Similarly B' would be formed by squasing it into B. We can then\neither do a normal rebase of B' onto A', or we can simply update its parent to\nA'. The former is useful, e.g. when the subprocess only makes a partial update\nof the tree based on the parent commit. In addition to these two modes, we may\nwant to have an option to ignore any changes made in the subprocess's working\ncopy."},"1377":{"id":"/design-docs/run-59","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#modifying-the-repo","content":"Once we give the subprocess access to a fork of the repo via separate\nOpHeadsStore, it will be able to create new operations in its fork.\nIf the user runs jj run -r foo and the subprocess checks out another commit,\nit's not clear what that should do. We should probably just verify that the\nworking-copy commit's parents are unchanged after the subprocess returns. Any\noperations created by the subprocess will be ignored."},"1378":{"id":"/design-docs/run-60","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#rewriting-the-revisions","content":"Like all commands, jj run will refuse to rewrite public/immutable commits.\nFor private/unpublished revisions, we either amend or reparent the changes,\nwhich are available as command options."},"1379":{"id":"/design-docs/run-61","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#execution-orderparallelism","content":"It may be useful to execute commands in topological order. For example,\ncommands with costs proportional to incremental changes, like build systems.\nThere may also be other relevant heuristics, but topological order is an easy\nand effective way to start."},"1380":{"id":"/design-docs/run-62","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#execution-orderparallelism","content":"Parallel execution of commands on different commits may choose to schedule\ncommits to still reduce incremental changes in the working copy used by each\nexecution slot/\"thread\". However, running the command on all commits\nconcurrently should be possible if desired."},"1381":{"id":"/design-docs/run-63","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#execution-orderparallelism","content":"Executing commands in topological order allows for more meaningful use of any\npotential features that stop execution \"at the first failure\". For example,\nwhen running tests on a chain of commits, it might be useful to proceed in\ntopological/chronological order, and stop on the first failure, because it\nmight imply that the remaining executions will be undesirable because they will\nalso fail."},"1382":{"id":"/design-docs/run-64","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#dealing-with-failure","content":"It will be useful to have multiple strategies to deal with failures on a single\nor multiple revisions. The reason for these strategies is to allow customized\nconflict handling. These strategies then can be exposed in the ui with a\nmatching option."},"1383":{"id":"/design-docs/run-65","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#dealing-with-failure","content":"Continue: If any subprocess fails, we will continue the work on child\nrevisions. Notify the user on exit about the failed revisions."},"1384":{"id":"/design-docs/run-66","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#dealing-with-failure","content":"Stop: Signal a fatal failure and cancel any scheduled work that has not\nyet started running, but let any already started subprocess finish. Notify the\nuser about the failed command and display the generated error from the\nsubprocess."},"1385":{"id":"/design-docs/run-67","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#dealing-with-failure","content":"Fatal: Signal a fatal failure and immediately stop processing and kill any\nrunning processes. Notify the user that we failed to apply the command to the\nspecific revision."},"1386":{"id":"/design-docs/run-68","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#dealing-with-failure","content":"We will leave any affected commit in its current state, if any subprocess fails.\nThis allows us to provide a better user experience, as leaving revisions in an\nundesirable state, e.g partially formatted, may confuse users."},"1387":{"id":"/design-docs/run-69","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#resource-constraints","content":"It will be useful to constrain the execution to prevent resource exhaustion.\nRelevant resources could include:"},"1388":{"id":"/design-docs/run-70","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#resource-constraints","content":"CPU and memory available on the machine running the commands. jj run can\nprovide some simple mitigations like limiting parallelism to \"number of CPUs\"\nby default, and limiting parallelism by dividing \"available memory\" by some\nestimate or measurement of per-invocation memory use of the commands."},"1389":{"id":"/design-docs/run-71","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#resource-constraints","content":"External resources that are not immediately known to jj. For example,\ncommands run in parallel may wish to limit the total number of connections\nto a server. We might choose to defer any handling of this to the\nimplementation of the command being invoked, instead of trying to\ncommunicate that information to jj."},"1390":{"id":"/design-docs/run-72","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#command-options","content":"The base command of any jj command should be usable. By default jj run works\non the @ the current working copy."},"1391":{"id":"/design-docs/run-73","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#command-options","content":"--command, explicit name of the first argument"},"1392":{"id":"/design-docs/run-74","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#command-options","content":"-x, for git compatibility (may alias another command)"},"1393":{"id":"/design-docs/run-75","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#command-options","content":"-j, --jobs, the amount of parallelism to use"},"1394":{"id":"/design-docs/run-76","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#command-options","content":"-k, --keep-going, continue on failure (may alias another command)"},"1395":{"id":"/design-docs/run-77","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#command-options","content":"--show, display the diff for an affected revision"},"1396":{"id":"/design-docs/run-78","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#command-options","content":"--dry-run, do the command execution without doing any work, logging all\nintended files and arguments"},"1397":{"id":"/design-docs/run-79","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#command-options","content":"--rebase, rebase all parents on the consulitng diff (may alias another\ncommand)"},"1398":{"id":"/design-docs/run-80","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#command-options","content":"--reparent, change the parent of an effected revision to the new change\n(may alias another command)"},"1399":{"id":"/design-docs/run-81","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#command-options","content":"--clean, remove existing workspaces and remove the ignored files"},"1400":{"id":"/design-docs/run-82","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#command-options","content":"--readonly, ignore changes across multiple run invocations"},"1401":{"id":"/design-docs/run-83","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#command-options","content":"--error-strategy=continue|stop|fatal, see Dealing with failure"},"1402":{"id":"/design-docs/run-84","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#integrating-with-other-commands","content":"jj log: No special handling needed\njj diff: No special handling needed\njj st: For now reprint the final output of jj run\njj op log: No special handling needed, but awaits further discussion in\n#963\njj undo/jj op revert: No special handling needed"},"1403":{"id":"/design-docs/run-85","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#open-points","content":"Should the command be working copy backend specific?\nHow do we manage the Processes which the command will spawn?\nConfiguration options, User and Repository Wide?"},"1404":{"id":"/design-docs/run-86","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#future-possibilities","content":"We could rewrite the file in memory, which is a neat optimization"},"1405":{"id":"/design-docs/run-87","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#future-possibilities","content":"Exposing some internal state, to allow preciser resource constraints"},"1406":{"id":"/design-docs/run-88","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#future-possibilities","content":"Integration options for virtual filesystems, which allow them to cache the\nneeded working copies."},"1407":{"id":"/design-docs/run-89","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#future-possibilities","content":"A Jujutsu wide concept for a cached working copy, as they could be expensive\nto materialize."},"1408":{"id":"/design-docs/run-90","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#future-possibilities","content":"Customized failure messages, this maybe useful for bots, it could be similar\nto Bazel's select(..., message = \"arch not supported for $project\")."},"1409":{"id":"/design-docs/run-91","page_id":"/design-docs/run","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/run#future-possibilities","content":"Make jj run asynchronous by spawning a main process, directly return to the\nuser and incrementally updating the output of jj st."},"1410":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","type":"page","content":"Sparse Patterns v2 redesign","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2"},"1411":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-0","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#objective","content":"Objective"},"1412":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-1","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#current-state-as-of-jj-0130","content":"Current State (as of jj 0.13.0)"},"1413":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-2","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#proposed-state-sparse-patterns-v2","content":"Proposed State (Sparse Patterns v2)"},"1414":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-3","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax","content":"Command Syntax"},"1415":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-4","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#canonicalization","content":"Canonicalization"},"1416":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-5","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#working-copy-map","content":"Working Copy Map"},"1417":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-6","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax-1","content":"Command Syntax"},"1418":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-7","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#versioning-and-storage","content":"Versioning and Storage"},"1419":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-8","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#view-updates","content":"View Updates"},"1420":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-9","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#appendix","content":"Appendix"},"1421":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-10","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#related-work","content":"Related Work"},"1422":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-11","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#patterns-via-configuration","content":"Patterns via configuration"},"1423":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-12","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","content":"Authors: Daniel Ploch"},"1424":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-13","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","content":"Summary: This Document documents a redesign of the sparse command and\nit's internal storage format in jj, in order to facilitate several desirable\nimprovements for large repos. It covers both the migration path and the planned\nend state."},"1425":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-14","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#objective","content":"Redesign Sparse Patterns to accommodate more advanced features for native\nand custom implementations. This includes three main goals:"},"1426":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-15","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#objective","content":"Sparse Patterns should be versioned with the working copy"},"1427":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-16","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#objective","content":"Sparse Patterns should support more flexible matching rules"},"1428":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-17","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#objective","content":"Sparse Patterns should support client path remapping"},"1429":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-18","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#current-state-as-of-jj-0130","content":"Sparse patterns are an effectively unordered list of prefix strings:"},"1430":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-19","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#current-state-as-of-jj-0130","content":"The set of files identified by the Sparse Patterns is all paths which match\nany provided prefix. This governs what gets materialized in the working copy on\ncheckout, and what is updated on snapshot. The set is stored in working copy\nstate files which are not versioned in the Op Store."},"1431":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-20","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#current-state-as-of-jj-0130","content":"Because all paths are bare strings with no escaping or higher-level formatting,\nthe current design makes it difficult to add new features like exclusions or\npath remappings."},"1432":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-21","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#proposed-state-sparse-patterns-v2","content":"Sparse Patterns v2 will be stored as objects in the Op Store, referenced\nby a WorkingCopyPatternsId from the active View. They will have a new,\nordered structure which can fully represent previous patterns."},"1433":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-22","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#proposed-state-sparse-patterns-v2","content":"To support these more complex behaviors, a new WorkingCopyPatterns trait will\nbe introduced, initially only as a thin wrapper around the existing prefix\nformat, but soon to be expanded with richer types and functionality."},"1434":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-23","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax","content":"SparsePatternsPath rules can be specified on the CLI and in an editor via a\ncompact syntax:"},"1435":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-24","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax","content":"If both prefix terms are omitted, then include:dir: is assumed. If any prefix\nis specified, both must be specified. The editor and CLI will both accept path\nrules in either format going forward."},"1436":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-25","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax","content":"jj sparse set --add foo/bar is equal to jj sparse set --add include:dir:foo/bar"},"1437":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-26","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax","content":"jj sparse set --add exclude:dir:foo/bar adds a new Dir type rule with include = false"},"1438":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-27","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax","content":"jj sparse set --exclude foo/bar as a possible shorthand for the above"},"1439":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-28","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax","content":"jj sparse list will print the explicit rules"},"1440":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-29","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax","content":"Paths will be stored in an ordered, canonical form which unambiguously describes\nthe set of files to be included. Every --add command will append to the end of\nthis list before the patterns are canonicalized. Whether a file is included is\ndetermined by the first matching rule in reverse order."},"1441":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-30","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax","content":"For example:"},"1442":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-31","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax","content":"Produces rule set which includes \"foo/file.txt\", excludes \"foo/bar/file.txt\",\nincludes \"foo/bar/baz/file.txt\", and excludes \"foo/bar/baz/qux/file.txt\"."},"1443":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-32","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax","content":"If the rules are subtly re-ordered, they become canonicalized to a smaller, but\nfunctionally equivalent form:"},"1444":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-33","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#canonicalization","content":"There are many ways to represent functionally equivalent WorkingCopyPatterns.\nFor instance, the following 4 rule sets are all functionally equivalent:"},"1445":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-34","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#canonicalization","content":"Because these patterns are stored in the Op Store now, it is useful for all of\nthese representations to be rewritten into a minimal, canonical form before\nserialization. In this case, Set 1 will be the canonical set. The canonical\nform of a WorkingCopyPatterns is defined as the form such that:"},"1446":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-35","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#canonicalization","content":"Every rule affects the functionality (there are no redundant rules)"},"1447":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-36","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#canonicalization","content":"Rules are sorted lexicographically, but with '/' sorted before all else"},"1448":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-37","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#canonicalization","content":"This special sorting order is useful for constructing path tries"},"1449":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-38","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#working-copy-map","content":"type: warning"},"1450":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-39","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#working-copy-map","content":"This section is intentionally lacking, more research is needed."},"1451":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-40","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#working-copy-map","content":"All WorkingCopyPatterns will come equipped with a default no-op mapping.\nThese mappings are inspired by and similar to Perforce client views."},"1452":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-41","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#working-copy-map","content":"WorkingCopyPatterns will provide an interface to map working copy paths into\nrepo paths and vice versa. The `WorkingCopy`` trait will apply this mapping to\nall snapshot and checkout operations, and jj commands which accept relative\npaths will need to be updated to perform working copy path -> repo path\ntranslations as needed. It's not clear at this time which commands will need\nchanging, as some are more likely to refer to repo paths rather than working\ncopy paths."},"1453":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-42","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#working-copy-map","content":"TODO: Expand this section."},"1454":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-43","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#working-copy-map","content":"In particular, the path rules for sparse patterns will always be repo paths,\nnot working copy paths. Thus, if the working copy wants to track \"foo\" and\nrename it to \"subdir/bar\", they must jj sparse set --add foo and\njj map set --from foo --to bar. In other words, the mapping operation can\nbe thought of as always after the sparse operation."},"1455":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-44","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax-1","content":"New commands will enable editing of the WorkingCopyMappings:"},"1456":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-45","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax-1","content":"TODO: Maybe this should be jj workspace map ...?"},"1457":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-46","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax-1","content":"jj map list will print all mapping pairs."},"1458":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-47","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax-1","content":"jj map add --from foo --to bar will add a new mapping to the end of the list."},"1459":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-48","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax-1","content":"jj map remove --from foo will remove a specific mapping rule."},"1460":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-49","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax-1","content":"jj map edit will pull up a text editor for manual editing."},"1461":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-50","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax-1","content":"Like sparse paths, mappings will have a compact text syntax for editing in file\nform, or for adding a rule textually on the CLI:"},"1462":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-51","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax-1","content":"Like sparse paths, mapping rules are defined to apply in order and on any\nsave operation will be modified to a minimal canonical form. Thus,\njj map set --from \"\" --to \"\" will always completely wipe the map.\nThe first matching rule in reverse list order determines how a particular\nrepo path should be mapped into the working copy, and likewise how a particular\nworking copy path should be mapped into the repo. For simplicity, the\n'last rule wins' applies both for repo->WC conversions, as well as WC->repo\nconversions, using the same ordering."},"1463":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-52","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax-1","content":"If a working copy mapping places the same repo file at two distinct working\ncopy paths, snapshotting will fail unless these files are identical. Some\nspecialized filesystems may even treat these as the 'same' file, allowing this\nto work in some cases."},"1464":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-53","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#command-syntax-1","content":"If a working copy mapping places two distinct repo files at the same working\ncopy path, checkout will fail with an error regardless of equivalence."},"1465":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-54","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#versioning-and-storage","content":"Updating the active WorkingCopyPatterns for a particular working copy will now\ntake place in two separate steps: one transaction which updates the op store,\nand a separate LockedWorkingCopy operation which actually updates the working\ncopy. The working copy proto will no longer store WorkingCopyPatterns\ndirectly, instead storing only a WorkingCopyPatternsId. On mismatch with the\ncurrent op head, the user will be prompted to run jj workspace update-stale."},"1466":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-55","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#versioning-and-storage","content":"This gives the user the ability to update the active WorkingCopyPatterns\nwhilst not interacting with the local working copy, which is useful for custom\nintegrations which may not be able to check out particular working copy\npatterns due to problems with the backend (encoding, permission errors, etc.). A\nbad jj sparse set --add oops command can thus be undone, even via jj op undo\nif desired."},"1467":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-56","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#view-updates","content":"The View object will be migrated to store working copy patterns via id. The\nindirection will save on storage since working copy patterns are not expected to\nchange very frequently."},"1468":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-57","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#view-updates","content":"A View object with no stored working copy patterns will be modified at read\ntime to include the current working copy patterns, thus all read_view\noperations will need to pass in the current working copy patterns for a\nmigration period of at least 6 months. After that, we may choose to auto-fill\nmissing working copy infos with a default WorkingCopyPatterns as needed."},"1469":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-58","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#related-work","content":"Perforce client maps\nare very similar in concept to the entirety of WorkingCopyPatterns, and this\ndesign aims to achieve similar functionality."},"1470":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-59","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#related-work","content":"The Josh Project implements partial git\nclones in a way similar to how sparse patterns try to work."},"1471":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-60","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#patterns-via-configuration","content":"There may be some scenarios where it is valuable to configure working copy\npatterns via a configuration file, rather than through explicit commands.\nGenerally this only makes sense for automated repos, with the configuration\ncoming from outside the repo - there are too many caveats and edge cases if the\nconfiguration comes from inside the repo and/or is fought with by a human."},"1472":{"id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2-61","page_id":"/design-docs/sparse-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/sparse-v2#patterns-via-configuration","content":"No configuration syntax is planned at this time but if we add any, we should\nprobably reuse the compact line syntaxes as much as possible for consistency."},"1473":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"page","content":"Remote/`@git` tracking branches","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches"},"1474":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-0","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#objective","content":"Objective"},"1475":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-1","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#current-data-model-as-of-jj-080","content":"Current data model (as of jj 0.8.0)"},"1476":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-2","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#proposed-data-model","content":"Proposed data model"},"1477":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-3","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#importexport-data-flow","content":"Import/export data flow"},"1478":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-4","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#tracking-state","content":"Tracking state"},"1479":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-5","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#mapping-to-the-current-data-model","content":"Mapping to the current data model"},"1480":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-6","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#common-command-behaviors","content":"Common command behaviors"},"1481":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-7","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#fetchimport","content":"fetch/import"},"1482":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-8","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#pushexport","content":"push/export"},"1483":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-9","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#initclone","content":"init/clone"},"1484":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-10","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#branch","content":"branch"},"1485":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-11","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#command-behavior-examples","content":"Command behavior examples"},"1486":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-12","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#fetchimport-1","content":"fetch/import"},"1487":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-13","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#push","content":"push"},"1488":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-14","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#export","content":"export"},"1489":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-15","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#undo-fetch","content":"undo fetch"},"1490":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-16","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#git-remote","content":"@git remote"},"1491":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-17","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#remaining-issues","content":"Remaining issues"},"1492":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-18","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#references","content":"References"},"1493":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-19","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","content":"This is a plan to implement more Git-like remote tracking branch UX."},"1494":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-20","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#objective","content":"jj imports all remote branches to local branches by default. As described in\n#1136, this doesn't interact nicely with Git if we have multiple Git remotes\nwith a number of branches. The git.auto-local-bookmark config can mitigate this\nproblem, but we'll get locally-deleted branches instead."},"1495":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-21","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#objective","content":"The goal of this plan is to implement"},"1496":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-22","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#objective","content":"proper support for tracking/non-tracking remote branches"},"1497":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-23","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#objective","content":"logically consistent data model for importing/exporting Git refs"},"1498":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-24","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#current-data-model-as-of-jj-080","content":"Under the current model, all remote branches are \"tracking\" branches, and\nremote changes are merged into the local counterparts."},"1499":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-25","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#current-data-model-as-of-jj-080","content":"Remote branches are stored in both branches[name].remote_targets and\ngit_refs[\"refs/remotes\"]. These two are mostly kept in sync, but there\nare two scenarios where remote-tracking branches and git refs can diverge:"},"1500":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-26","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#current-data-model-as-of-jj-080","content":"jj branch forget"},"1501":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-27","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#current-data-model-as-of-jj-080","content":"jj op revert/restore in colocated repo"},"1502":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-28","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#current-data-model-as-of-jj-080","content":"Pseudo @git tracking branches are stored in git_refs[\"refs/heads\"]. We\nneed special case to resolve @git branches, and their behavior is slightly\ndifferent from the other remote-tracking branches."},"1503":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-29","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#proposed-data-model","content":"We'll add a per-remote-branch state to distinguish non-tracking branches\nfrom tracking ones."},"1504":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-30","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#proposed-data-model","content":"We'll add a per-remote view-like object to record the last known remote\nbranches. It will replace branches[name].remote_targets in the current model.\n@git branches will be stored in remotes[\"git\"]."},"1505":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-31","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#proposed-data-model","content":"With the proposed data model, we can"},"1506":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-32","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#proposed-data-model","content":"naturally support remote branches which have no local counterparts"},"1507":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-33","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#proposed-data-model","content":"deduplicate branches[name].remote_targets and git_refs[\"refs/remotes\"]"},"1508":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-34","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/tracking-branches#importexport-data-flow","content":"jj git import applies diff between git_refs and remotes[]. git_refs is\nalways copied from the backing Git repo."},"1509":{"id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches-35","page_id":"/design-docs/tracking-branches","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/design-docs/