ory","content":"Working in a Jujutsu repository"},"1671":{"id":"/getting-started/github-6","page_id":"/getting-started/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/github#addressing-review-comments","content":"Addressing review comments"},"1672":{"id":"/getting-started/github-7","page_id":"/getting-started/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/github#adding-new-commits","content":"Adding new commits"},"1673":{"id":"/getting-started/github-8","page_id":"/getting-started/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/github#rewriting-commits","content":"Rewriting commits"},"1674":{"id":"/getting-started/github-9","page_id":"/getting-started/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/github#working-with-other-peoples-bookmarks","content":"Working with other people's bookmarks"},"1675":{"id":"/getting-started/github-10","page_id":"/getting-started/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/github#using-github-cli","content":"Using GitHub CLI"},"1676":{"id":"/getting-started/github-11","page_id":"/getting-started/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/github#useful-revsets","content":"Useful Revsets"},"1677":{"id":"/getting-started/github-12","page_id":"/getting-started/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/github#merge-conflicts","content":"Merge conflicts"},"1678":{"id":"/getting-started/github-13","page_id":"/getting-started/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/github#using-several-remotes","content":"Using several remotes"},"1679":{"id":"/getting-started/github-14","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github","content":"This guide assumes a basic understanding of either Git or Mercurial."},"1680":{"id":"/getting-started/github-15","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#basic-workflow","content":"The simplest way to start with Jujutsu is to create a stack of commits first.\nYou will only need to create a bookmark when you need to push the stack to a\nremote. There are two primary workflows: using a generated bookmark name or\nnaming a bookmark."},"1681":{"id":"/getting-started/github-16","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#using-a-generated-bookmark-name","content":"In this example we're letting Jujutsu auto-create a bookmark."},"1682":{"id":"/getting-started/github-17","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#using-a-named-bookmark","content":"In this example, we create a bookmark named bar and then push it to the remote."},"1683":{"id":"/getting-started/github-18","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#using-a-named-bookmark","content":"While it's possible to create a bookmark in advance and commit on top of it in a\nGit-like manner, you will then need to move the bookmark manually when you create\na new commits. Unlike Git, Jujutsu will not do it automatically."},"1684":{"id":"/getting-started/github-19","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#updating-the-repository","content":"As of October 2023, Jujutsu has no equivalent to a git pull command (see\nissue #1039). Until such a command is added, you need to use\njj git fetch followed by a jj rebase -d $main_bookmark to update your\nchanges."},"1685":{"id":"/getting-started/github-20","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#working-in-a-git-colocated-repository","content":"After doing jj git init, which\ncolocates the .jj and\n.git directories, Git will be in a detached HEAD state, which is\nunusual, as Git mainly works with named branches; jj does not."},"1686":{"id":"/getting-started/github-21","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#working-in-a-git-colocated-repository","content":"In a colocated repository, every jj command will automatically synchronize\nJujutsu's view of the repo with Git's view. For example, jj commit updates the\nHEAD of the Git repository, enabling an incremental migration."},"1687":{"id":"/getting-started/github-22","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#working-in-a-jujutsu-repository","content":"In a Jujutsu repository, the workflow is simplified. If there's no need for\nexplicitly named bookmarks, you can just generate one for a change. As Jujutsu is\nable to create a bookmark for a revision."},"1688":{"id":"/getting-started/github-23","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#addressing-review-comments","content":"There are two workflows for addressing review comments, depending on your\nproject's preference. Many projects prefer that you address comments by adding\ncommits to your bookmark. Some projects (such as Jujutsu and LLVM) instead\nprefer that you keep your commits clean by rewriting them and then\nforce-pushing."},"1689":{"id":"/getting-started/github-24","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#adding-new-commits","content":"If your project prefers that you address review comments by adding commits on\ntop, you can do that by doing something like this:"},"1690":{"id":"/getting-started/github-25","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#adding-new-commits","content":"Notably, the above workflow creates a new commit for you. The same can be\nachieved without creating a new commit."},"1691":{"id":"/getting-started/github-26","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#adding-new-commits","content":"type: warning"},"1692":{"id":"/getting-started/github-27","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#adding-new-commits","content":"We strongly suggest to jj new after the example below, as all further edits\nstill get amended to the previous commit."},"1693":{"id":"/getting-started/github-28","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#rewriting-commits","content":"If your project prefers that you keep commits clean, you can do that by doing\nsomething like this:"},"1694":{"id":"/getting-started/github-29","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#rewriting-commits","content":"The hyphen after your-feature comes from the revset syntax."},"1695":{"id":"/getting-started/github-30","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#working-with-other-peoples-bookmarks","content":"By default, jj git clone imports the default remote bookmark (which is usually\nmain or master), but jj git fetch doesn't import new remote bookmarks to\nlocal bookmarks. This means that if you want to iterate or test another\ncontributor's bookmark, you'll need to do jj new <bookmark>@<remote> onto it."},"1696":{"id":"/getting-started/github-31","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#working-with-other-peoples-bookmarks","content":"If you want to import all remote bookmarks including inactive ones, set\ngit.auto-local-bookmark = true in the config file. Then you can specify a\ncontributor's bookmark as jj new <bookmark> instead of jj new <bookmark>@<remote>."},"1697":{"id":"/getting-started/github-32","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#working-with-other-peoples-bookmarks","content":"You can find more information on that setting here."},"1698":{"id":"/getting-started/github-33","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#using-github-cli","content":"GitHub CLI will have trouble finding the proper Git repository path in jj repos\nthat aren't colocated\n(see issue #1008). You can configure the $GIT_DIR environment variable to\npoint it to the right path:"},"1699":{"id":"/getting-started/github-34","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#using-github-cli","content":"You can make that automatic by installing direnv and\ndefining hooks in a .envrc file in the repository root to configure $GIT_DIR.\nJust add this line into .envrc:"},"1700":{"id":"/getting-started/github-35","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#using-github-cli","content":"and run direnv allow to approve it for direnv to run. Then GitHub CLI will\nwork automatically even in repos that aren't colocated so you can execute\ncommands like gh issue list normally."},"1701":{"id":"/getting-started/github-36","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#useful-revsets","content":"Log all revisions across all local bookmarks that aren't on the main bookmark nor\non any remote:"},"1702":{"id":"/getting-started/github-37","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#useful-revsets","content":"Log all revisions that you authored, across all bookmarks that aren't on any\nremote:"},"1703":{"id":"/getting-started/github-38","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#useful-revsets","content":"Log all remote bookmarks that you authored or committed to:"},"1704":{"id":"/getting-started/github-39","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#useful-revsets","content":"Log all ancestors of the current working copy that aren't on any remote:"},"1705":{"id":"/getting-started/github-40","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#merge-conflicts","content":"For a detailed overview, how Jujutsu handles conflicts, revisit\nthe tutorial."},"1706":{"id":"/getting-started/github-41","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#merge-conflicts","content":"This is a GitHub-style review, as GitHub currently only is able to compare\nbookmarks."},"1707":{"id":"/getting-started/github-42","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#merge-conflicts","content":"If you're wondering why we prefer clean commits in this project, see\ne.g. this blog post"},"1708":{"id":"/getting-started/github-43","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#using-several-remotes","content":"It is common to use several remotes when contributing to a shared repository.\nFor example, \"upstream\" can designate the remote where the changes will be\nmerged through a pull-request while \"origin\" is your private fork of the\nproject."},"1709":{"id":"/getting-started/github-44","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#using-several-remotes","content":"This will automatically setup your repository to track the main\nbookmark from the upstream repository, typically main@upstream\nor master@upstream."},"1710":{"id":"/getting-started/github-45","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#using-several-remotes","content":"You might want to jj git fetch from \"upstream\" and to jj git push\nto \"origin\". You can configure the default remotes to fetch from and\npush to in your configuration file (for example,\n.jj/repo/config.toml):"},"1711":{"id":"/getting-started/github-46","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#using-several-remotes","content":"The default for both git.fetch and git.push is \"origin\"."},"1712":{"id":"/getting-started/github-47","page_id":"/getting-started/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/github#using-several-remotes","content":"If you usually work on a project from several computers, you may\nconfigure jj to fetch from both repositories by default, in order to\nkeep your own bookmarks synchronized through your origin repository:"},"1713":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","type":"page","content":"Installation and setup","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup"},"1714":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-0","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#installation","content":"Installation"},"1715":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-1","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#download-pre-built-binaries-for-a-release","content":"Download pre-built binaries for a release"},"1716":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-2","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#cargo-binstall","content":"Cargo Binstall"},"1717":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-3","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#linux","content":"Linux"},"1718":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-4","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#from-source","content":"From Source"},"1719":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-5","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#arch-linux","content":"Arch Linux"},"1720":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-6","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#nixos","content":"NixOS"},"1721":{"id":"/getting-star