3":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-49","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#windows","content":"via winget:"},"1764":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-50","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#windows","content":"via scoop:"},"1765":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-51","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#initial-configuration","content":"You may want to configure your name and email so commits are made in your name."},"1766":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-52","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#command-line-completion","content":"Jujutsu provides 2 different command-line completion scripts."},"1767":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-53","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#standard-completions","content":"The standard completion script provides completions for jj subcommmands and\noptions."},"1768":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-54","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#dynamic-completions","content":"The dynamic completion script provides completions for jj subcommands and\noptions, as well as additional completions, including bookmarks,\naliases, revisions, operations and files. Dynamic completions can be context\naware, for example they respect the global flags --repository and\n--at-operation as well as some command-specific ones like --revision,\n--from, and --to."},"1769":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-55","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#dynamic-completions","content":"Dynamic completions are not the default/only option since the underlying engine\nis still labeled unstable. We expect to transition to them as the\ndefault once the engine is stabilized. Please let us know if you encounter any\nissues with dynamic completions."},"1770":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-56","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#dynamic-completions","content":"title: Which completion script should I use?"},"1771":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-57","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#dynamic-completions","content":"Generally, dynamic completions provide a much better completion experience.\nAlthough the underlying engine is deemed unstable, there have not been many\nissues in practice. Dynamic completions are the preferred option for many\ncontributors and users."},"1772":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-58","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#dynamic-completions","content":"We recommend using the dynamic completion script, and falling back to the\nstandard completion script if there are any issues."},"1773":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-59","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#fish","content":"No configuration is required with fish >= 4.0.2 which loads dynamic\ncompletions by default."},"1774":{"id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup-60","page_id":"/getting-started/install-and-setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/install-and-setup#powershell","content":"Insert this line in your $PROFILE file\n(usually $HOME\\Documents\\PowerShell\\Microsoft.PowerShell_profile.ps1):"},"1775":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","type":"page","content":"Tutorial","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/tutorial"},"1776":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-0","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/tutorial#preparation","content":"Preparation"},"1777":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-1","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/tutorial#cloning-a-git-repository","content":"Cloning a Git repository"},"1778":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-2","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/tutorial#changes","content":"Changes"},"1779":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-3","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/tutorial#creating-our-first-change","content":"Creating our first change"},"1780":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-4","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/tutorial#the-log-command-and-revsets","content":"The log command and \"revsets\""},"1781":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-5","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/tutorial#conflicts","content":"Conflicts"},"1782":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-6","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/tutorial#the-operation-log","content":"The operation log"},"1783":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-7","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/tutorial#moving-content-changes-between-commits","content":"Moving content changes between commits"},"1784":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-8","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial","content":"This tutorial has become somewhat out of date. Many people find the\nalternative (not quite finished) tutorial by Steve\nKlabnik helpful."},"1785":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-9","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial","content":"This text assumes that the reader is familiar with Git."},"1786":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-10","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#preparation","content":"If you haven't already, make sure you\ninstall and configure Jujutsu."},"1787":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-11","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#cloning-a-git-repository","content":"Most identifiers used in this tutorial will be different when you try this at\nhome!"},"1788":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-12","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#cloning-a-git-repository","content":"Let's start by cloning GitHub's Hello-World repo using jj:"},"1789":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-13","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#cloning-a-git-repository","content":"Running jj st (short for jj status) now yields something like this:"},"1790":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-14","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#cloning-a-git-repository","content":"Let's look at that output as it introduces new concepts. You can see two\ncommits: Parent and working copy. Both are identified using two separate\nidentifiers: the \"change ID\" and the \"commit ID\"."},"1791":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-15","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#cloning-a-git-repository","content":"The parent commit, for example, has the change ID orrkosyo and the commit ID\n7fd1a60b."},"1792":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-16","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#cloning-a-git-repository","content":"Git users: The commit ID/hash is what you're used to from Git and should\nmatch what you see when you look at the repository using git log in a Git\ncheckout of the repository. The change ID however, is a new concept, unique to\nJujutsu."},"1793":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-17","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#cloning-a-git-repository","content":"We can also see from the output above that our working copy is an actual commit\nwith a commit ID (d7439b06 in the example). When you make a change in the\nworking copy, the working-copy commit gets automatically amended by the next\njj command."},"1794":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-18","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#cloning-a-git-repository","content":"Git users: This is a huge difference from Git where the working copy is a\nseparate concept and not yet a commit."},"1795":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-19","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#changes","content":"A change is a commit that can evolve while keeping a stable identifier (similar\nto Gerrit's Change-Id). In other words: You can make changes to files in a\nchange, resulting in a new commit hash, but the change ID will remain the same."},"1796":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-20","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#changes","content":"You can see that our clone operation automatically created a new change:"},"1797":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-21","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#changes","content":"This new change has the ID kntqzsqt and it is currently empty (contains no\nchanges compared to the parent) and has no description."},"1798":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-22","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#creating-our-first-change","content":"Let's say we want to edit the README file in the repo to say \"Goodbye\"\ninstead of \"Hello\". Start by describing the change (adding a commit message) so\nwe don't forget what we're working on:"},"1799":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-23","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#creating-our-first-change","content":"Now make the change in the README:"},"1800":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-24","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#creating-our-first-change","content":"Note that you didn't have to tell Jujutsu to add the change like you would with\ngit add. You actually don't even need to tell it when you add new files or\nremove existing files. To untrack a path, add it to your .gitignore and run\njj file untrack <path>."},"1801":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-25","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#creating-our-first-change","content":"Also note that the commit hash for our current change (kntqzsqt) changed from\ne427edcf to 5d39e19d!"},"1802":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-26","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#creating-our-first-change","content":"To see the diff, run jj diff:"},"1803":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-27","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#creating-our-first-change","content":"Jujutsu's diff format currently defaults to inline coloring of the diff (like\ngit diff --color-words), so we used --git above to make the diff readable in\nthis tutorial."},"1804":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-28","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#creating-our-first-change","content":"As you may have noticed, the working-copy commit's ID changed both when we\nedited the description and when we edited the README. However, the parent commit\nstayed the same. Each change to the working-copy commit amends the previous\nversion. So how do we tell Jujutsu that we are done amending the current change\nand want to start working on a new one? That is what jj new is for. That will\ncreate a new commit on top of your current working-copy commit. The new commit\nis for the working-copy changes."},"1805":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-29","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#creating-our-first-change","content":"So, let's say we're now done with this change, so we create a new change:"},"1806":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-30","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#creating-our-first-change","content":"If we later realize that we want to make further changes, we can make them in\nthe working copy and then run jj squash. That command squashes (moves) the\nchanges from a given commit into its parent commit. Like most commands, it acts\non the working-copy commit by default. When run on the working-copy commit, it\nbehaves very similar to git commit --amend."},"1807":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-31","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#creating-our-first-change","content":"Alternatively, we can use jj edit <commit> to resume editing a commit in the\nworking copy. Any further changes in the working copy will then amend the\ncommit. Whether you choose to create a new change and squash, or to edit,\ntypically depends on how done you are with the change; if the change is almost\ndone, it makes sense to use jj new so you can easily review your adjustments\nwith jj diff before running jj squash."},"1808":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-32","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#creating-our-first-change","content":"To view how a change has evolved over time, we can use jj evolog to see each\nrecorded change for the current commit. This records changes to the working\ncopy, message, squashes, rebases, etc."},"1809":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-33","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#the-log-command-and-revsets","content":"You're probably familiar with git log. Jujutsu has very similar functionality\nin its jj log command:"},"1810":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-34","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#the-log-command-and-revsets","content":"The @ indicates the working-copy commit. The first ID on a line\n(e.g. \"mpqrykyp\" above) is the change ID. The second ID is the commit ID. You\ncan give either ID to commands that take revisions as arguments. We will\ngenerally prefer change IDs because they stay the same when the commit is\nrewritten."},"1811":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-35","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#the-log-command-and-revsets","content":"By default, jj log lists your local commits, with some remote commits added\nfor context. The ~ indicates that the commit has parents that are not included\nin the graph. We can use the --revisions/-r flag to select a different set\nof revisions to list. The flag accepts a \"revset\", which is an\nexpression in a simple language for specifying revisions. For example, @\nrefers to the working-copy commit, root() refers to the root commit,\nbookmarks() refers to all commits pointed to by bookmarks (similar to Git's\nbranches). We can combine expressions with | for union, & for intersection\nand ~ for difference. For example:"},"1812":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-36","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#the-log-command-and-revsets","content":"The 00000000 commit (change ID zzzzzzzz) is a virtual commit that's called\nthe \"root commit\". It's the root commit of every repo. The root()\nfunction in the revset matches it."},"1813":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-37","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#the-log-command-and-revsets","content":"There are also operators for getting the parents (foo-), children (foo+),\nancestors (::foo), descendants (foo::), DAG range (foo::bar, like\ngit log --ancestry-path), range (foo..bar, same as Git's). See\nthe revset documentation for all revset operators and functions."},"1814":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-38","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#the-log-command-and-revsets","content":"If the default jj log omits some commits you expect to see, you can always\nrun jj log -r :: (or, equivalently, jj log -r 'all()') to see all the\ncommits."},"1815":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-39","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#conflicts","content":"Now let's see how Jujutsu deals with merge conflicts. We'll start by making some\ncommits. We use jj new with the --message/-m option to set change\ndescriptions (commit messages) right away."},"1816":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-40","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#conflicts","content":"We now have a few commits, where A, B1, and B2 modify the same file, while C\nmodifies a different file. Let's now rebase B2 directly onto A. We use the\n--source/-s option on the change ID of B2, and --destination/-d option\non A."},"1817":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-41","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#conflicts","content":"There are several things worth noting here. First, the jj rebase command said\n\"Rebased 2 commits\". That's because we asked it to rebase commit B2 with the\n-s option, which also rebases descendants (commit C in this case). Second,\nbecause B2 modified the same file (and word) as B1, rebasing it resulted in\nconflicts, as the output indicates. Third, the conflicts did not prevent the\nrebase from completing successfully, nor did it prevent C from getting rebased\non top."},"1818":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-42","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#conflicts","content":"Now let's resolve the conflict in B2. We'll do that by creating a new commit on\ntop of B2. Once we've resolved the conflict, we'll squash the conflict\nresolution into the conflicted B2. That might look like this:"},"1819":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-43","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#conflicts","content":"Note that commit C automatically got rebased on top of the resolved B2, and that\nC is also resolved (since it modified only a different file)."},"1820":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-44","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#conflicts","content":"By the way, if we want to get rid of B1 now, we can run jj abandon\novknlmro. That will hide the commit from the log output and will rebase any\ndescendants to its parent."},"1821":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-45","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#the-operation-log","content":"Jujutsu keeps a record of all changes you've made to the repo in what's called\nthe \"operation log\". Use the jj op (short for jj operation) family of\ncommands to interact with it. To list the operations, use jj op log:"},"1822":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-46","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#the-operation-log","content":"The most useful command is jj undo, which will undo your last operation."},"1823":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-47","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#the-operation-log","content":"As you can perhaps see, that undid the jj squash invocation we used for\nsquashing the conflict resolution into commit B2 earlier. Notice that it also\nupdated the working copy."},"1824":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-48","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#the-operation-log","content":"You can also view the repo the way it looked after some earlier operation. For\nexample, if you want to see jj log output right after the jj rebase\noperation, try jj log --at-op=367400773f87 but use the hash from your own\njj op log."},"1825":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-49","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#moving-content-changes-between-commits","content":"You have already seen how jj squash can combine the changes from two commits\ninto one. There are several other commands for changing the contents of existing\ncommits."},"1826":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-50","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#moving-content-changes-between-commits","content":"We'll need some more complex content to test these commands, so let's create a\nfew more commits:"},"1827":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-51","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#moving-content-changes-between-commits","content":"We \"forgot\" to capitalize \"c\" in the second commit when we capitalized the other\nletters. We then fixed that in the third commit when we also added \"D\". It would\nbe cleaner to move the capitalization of \"c\" into the second commit. We can do\nthat by running jj squash with the --interactive/-i option on the third\ncommit. Remember that jj squash moves all the changes from one commit into its\nparent. jj squash -i moves only part of the changes into its parent. Now try\nthat:"},"1828":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-52","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#moving-content-changes-between-commits","content":"That will bring up the built-in diff editor with a\ndiff of the changes in the \"ABCD\" commit. Expand the file by clicking on (+)\nor with right arrow, then select the sections/line to include by clicking or\nusing space. Once complete, press c to confirm changes, or q to exit without\nsaving. You can also use the mouse to click on the menu items to see more\noptions (keyboard navigation is currently limited)."},"1829":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-53","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#moving-content-changes-between-commits","content":"There are many other diff editors you could use.\nFor example, if you have Meld installed and in the\nPATH, you can use it via jj squash -i --tool meld or a fancier config with jj\nsquash -i --tool meld-3. You can configure the default with the\nui.diff-editor option; those docs also explain how\nto specify a path to an executable if it is not in the PATH."},"1830":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-54","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#moving-content-changes-between-commits","content":"If we look at the diff of the second commit, we now see that all three lines got\ncapitalized:"},"1831":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-55","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#moving-content-changes-between-commits","content":"The child change (\"ABCD\" in our case) will have the same content state after\nthe jj squash command. That means that you can move any changes you want into\nthe parent change, even if they touch the same word, and it won't cause any\nconflicts."},"1832":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-56","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#moving-content-changes-between-commits","content":"Let's try one final command for changing the contents of an existing commit. That\ncommand is jj diffedit, which lets you edit the changes in a commit without\nchecking it out."},"1833":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-57","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#moving-content-changes-between-commits","content":"In the diff editor, use the arrow keys and spacebar to select all lines but the\nlast. Press 'c' to save the changes and close it. You can now inspect the\nrewritten commit with jj diff -r @- again, and you should see your deletion of\nthe last line. Unlike jj squash -i, which left the content state of the commit\nunchanged, jj diffedit (typically) results in a different state, which means\nthat descendant commits may have conflicts."},"1834":{"id":"/getting-started/tutorial-58","page_id":"/getting-started/tutorial","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/tutorial#moving-content-changes-between-commits","content":"Another command for rewriting contents of existing commits is jj split. Now\nthat you've seen how jj squash -i and jj diffedit work, you can hopefully\nfigure out how it works (with the help of the instructions in the diff)."},"1835":{"id":"/getting-started/windows","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","type":"page","content":"Working on Windows","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/windows"},"1836":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-0","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/windows#line-endings-conversion","content":"Line endings conversion"},"1837":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-1","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/windows#pagination","content":"Pagination"},"1838":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-2","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/windows#typing--in-powershell","content":"Typing @ in PowerShell"},"1839":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-3","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/windows#wsl-sets-the-execute-bit-on-all-files","content":"WSL sets the execute bit on all files"},"1840":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-4","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/getting-started/windows#symbolic-link-support","content":"Symbolic link support"},"1841":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-5","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows","content":"Jujutsu works the same on all platforms, but there are some caveats that Windows\nusers should be aware of."},"1842":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-6","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#line-endings-conversion","content":"Jujutsu currently has a setting,\nworking-copy.eol-conversion, similar to\nGit's core.autocrlf, but does not currently honor\n.gitattributes and the core.autocrlf git config, so it is recommended to\nkeep the working-copy.eol-conversion setting and the core.autocrlf git\nconfig in sync."},"1843":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-7","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#line-endings-conversion","content":"If you created a colocated git repo, forget to keep these 2 settings in sync,\nand result in a dirty working copy with only EOL diffs, you can set the\nworking-copy.eol-conversion setting correctly and run jj abandon to fix\nit."},"1844":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-8","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#line-endings-conversion","content":"The line endings conversion won't be applied to files detected as a binary files\nvia a heuristics regardless of the settings. This behavior is subject to\nchange when we support the text git attribute."},"1845":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-9","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#line-endings-conversion","content":"Jujutsu may make incorrect decision on whether a file is a binary file and apply\nline conversion incorrectly, but currently, Jujutsu doesn't support configuring\nline endings conversion for particular files. If this issue is hit, one should\nnot enable the line conversion setting."},"1846":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-10","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#line-endings-conversion","content":"If Jujutsu applies line endings conversion on incorrect files, you should not\nenable the line conversion setting and the git core.autocrlf setting. See\nbelow."},"1847":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-11","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#line-endings-conversion","content":"To disable line conversion, set the core.autocrlf setting to none or just\nremove the setting."},"1848":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-12","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#line-endings-conversion","content":"This means that line endings will be checked out exactly as they are committed\nand committed exactly as authored."},"1849":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-13","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#line-endings-conversion","content":"This setting ensures Git will check out files with LF line endings without\nconverting them to CRLF. You'll want to make sure any tooling you use,\nespecially IDEs, preserve LF line endings."},"1850":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-14","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#line-endings-conversion","content":"This poses the question if we should support reading the core.autocrlf\nsetting in colocated repos. See details at the\nissue."},"1851":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-15","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#line-endings-conversion","content":"To detect if a file is binary, Jujutsu currently checks if there is 0 byte\nin the file which is different from the algorithm of\ngitoxide or git. Jujutsu\ndoesn't plan to align the binary detection logic with git."},"1852":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-16","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#pagination","content":"On Windows, jj will use its integrated pager called streampager by default,\nunless the environment variable %PAGER% or the config ui.pager is explicitly\nset. See the pager section of the config docs for more\ndetails."},"1853":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-17","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#pagination","content":"If the built-in pager doesn't meet your needs and you have Git installed, you\ncan switch to using Git's pager as follows:"},"1854":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-18","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#typing--in-powershell","content":"PowerShell uses @ as part the array sub-expression operator, so it\noften needs to be escaped or quoted in commands:"},"1855":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-19","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#typing--in-powershell","content":"One solution is to create a revset alias. For example, to make HEAD an alias\nfor @:"},"1856":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-20","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#wsl-sets-the-execute-bit-on-all-files","content":"When viewing a Windows drive from WSL (via /mnt/c or a similar path), Windows\nexposes all files with the execute bit set. Since Jujutsu automatically records\nchanges to the working copy, this sets the execute bit on all files committed in\nyour repository."},"1857":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-21","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#wsl-sets-the-execute-bit-on-all-files","content":"If you only need to access the repository in WSL, the best solution is to clone\nthe repository in the Linux file system (for example, in\n~/my-repo)."},"1858":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-22","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#wsl-sets-the-execute-bit-on-all-files","content":"If you need to use the repository in both WSL and Windows, one solution is to\ncreate a workspace in the Linux file system:"},"1859":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-23","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#wsl-sets-the-execute-bit-on-all-files","content":"Then only use the ~/my-repo workspace from Linux."},"1860":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-24","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#symbolic-link-support","content":"jj supports symlinks on Windows only when they are enabled by the operating\nsystem. This requires Windows 10 version 14972 or higher, as well as Developer\nMode. If those conditions are not satisfied, jj will materialize symlinks as\nordinary files."},"1861":{"id":"/getting-started/windows-25","page_id":"/getting-started/windows","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started/windows#symbolic-link-support","content":"For colocated repositories, Git support must also be enabled using the\ngit config option core.symlinks=true."},"1862":{"id":"/guides/divergence","page_id":"/guides/divergence","type":"page","content":"Handling divergent changes","tags":[],"url":"/guides/divergence"},"1863":{"id":"/guides/divergence-0","page_id":"/guides/divergence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/guides/divergence#what-are-divergent-changes","content":"What are divergent changes?"},"1864":{"id":"/guides/divergence-1","page_id":"/guides/divergence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/guides/divergence#how-do-i-resolve-divergent-changes","content":"How do I resolve divergent changes?"},"1865":{"id":"/guides/divergence-2","page_id":"/guides/divergence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/guides/divergence#strategy-1-abandon-one-of-the-commits","content":"Strategy 1: Abandon one of the commits"},"1866":{"id":"/guides/divergence-3","page_id":"/guides/divergence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/guides/divergence#strategy-2-duplicate-and-abandon","content":"Strategy 2: Duplicate and abandon"},"1867":{"id":"/guides/divergence-4","page_id":"/guides/divergence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/guides/divergence#strategy-3-squash-the-commits-together","content":"Strategy 3: Squash the commits together"},"1868":{"id":"/guides/divergence-5","page_id":"/guides/divergence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/guides/divergence#strategy-4-ignore-the-divergence","content":"Strategy 4: Ignore the divergence"},"1869":{"id":"/guides/divergence-6","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#what-are-divergent-changes","content":"A divergent change occurs when multiple visible commits have the same change\nID."},"1870":{"id":"/guides/divergence-7","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#what-are-divergent-changes","content":"Normally, when commits are rewritten, the original version (the \"predecessor\")\nbecomes hidden and the new commit (the \"successor\") is visible. Thus, only one\ncommit with a given change ID is visible at a time."},"1871":{"id":"/guides/divergence-8","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#what-are-divergent-changes","content":"But, a hidden commit can become visible again. This can happen if:"},"1872":{"id":"/guides/divergence-9","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#what-are-divergent-changes","content":"A visible descendant is added locally. For example, jj new REV will make\nREV visible even if it was hidden before."},"1873":{"id":"/guides/divergence-10","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#what-are-divergent-changes","content":"A visible descendant is fetched from a remote. If the hidden commit was pushed\nto a remote, others may base new commits off of them. When their new commits are\nfetched, their visibility makes the hidden commit visible again."},"1874":{"id":"/guides/divergence-11","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#what-are-divergent-changes","content":"It is made the working copy. jj edit REV will make REV and all its\nancestors visible if it wasn't already."},"1875":{"id":"/guides/divergence-12","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#what-are-divergent-changes","content":"Some other operations make hidden commits visible. For example, adding a\nbookmark to a hidden commit makes it visible with the assumption that you are\nnow working with that commit again."},"1876":{"id":"/guides/divergence-13","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#what-are-divergent-changes","content":"Divergent changes also occur if two different users or processes amend the same\nchange, creating two visible successors. This can happen when:"},"1877":{"id":"/guides/divergence-14","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#what-are-divergent-changes","content":"Another author modifies commits in a branch that you have also modified\nlocally."},"1878":{"id":"/guides/divergence-15","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#what-are-divergent-changes","content":"You perform operations on the same change from different workspaces of the\nsame repository."},"1879":{"id":"/guides/divergence-16","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#what-are-divergent-changes","content":"Two programs modify the repository at the same time. For example, you run\njj describe and, while writing your commit description, an IDE integration\nfetches and rebases the branch you're working on."},"1880":{"id":"/guides/divergence-17","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#how-do-i-resolve-divergent-changes","content":"When you encounter divergent changes, you have several strategies to choose\nfrom. The best approach depends on whether you want to keep the content from one\ncommit, both commits, or merge them together."},"1881":{"id":"/guides/divergence-18","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#how-do-i-resolve-divergent-changes","content":"Note that revsets must refer to the divergent commit using its commit ID since\nthe change ID is ambiguous."},"1882":{"id":"/guides/divergence-19","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#strategy-1-abandon-one-of-the-commits","content":"If one of the divergent commits is clearly obsolete or incorrect, simply abandon\nit:"},"1883":{"id":"/guides/divergence-20","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#strategy-1-abandon-one-of-the-commits","content":"This is the simplest solution when you know which version to keep."},"1884":{"id":"/guides/divergence-21","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#strategy-2-duplicate-and-abandon","content":"If you want to keep both versions as separate changes with different change IDs,\nyou can duplicate one of the commits to generate a new change ID, then abandon\nthe original:"},"1885":{"id":"/guides/divergence-22","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#strategy-2-duplicate-and-abandon","content":"This preserves both versions of the content while resolving the divergence."},"1886":{"id":"/guides/divergence-23","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#strategy-3-squash-the-commits-together","content":"When you want to combine the content from both divergent commits:"},"1887":{"id":"/guides/divergence-24","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#strategy-3-squash-the-commits-together","content":"This combines the changes from both commits into a single commit. The source\ncommit will be abandoned."},"1888":{"id":"/guides/divergence-25","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#strategy-4-ignore-the-divergence","content":"Divergence isn't an error. If the divergence doesn't cause immediate problems,\nyou can leave it as-is. If both commits are part of immutable history, this may\nbe your only option."},"1889":{"id":"/guides/divergence-26","page_id":"/guides/divergence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/guides/divergence#strategy-4-ignore-the-divergence","content":"However, it can be inconvenient since you cannot refer to divergent changes\nunambiguously using their change ID."},"1890":{"id":"/reference/config","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"page","content":"Configuration","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config"},"1891":{"id":"/reference/config-0","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#config-files-and-toml","content":"Config files and TOML"},"1892":{"id":"/reference/config-1","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#dotted-style-and-headings","content":"Dotted style and headings"},"1893":{"id":"/reference/config-2","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#user-settings","content":"User settings"},"1894":{"id":"/reference/config-3","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#ui-settings","content":"UI settings"},"1895":{"id":"/reference/config-4","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#colorizing-output","content":"Colorizing output"},"1896":{"id":"/reference/config-5","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#custom-colors-and-styles","content":"Custom colors and styles"},"1897":{"id":"/reference/config-6","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#default-command","content":"Default command"},"1898":{"id":"/reference/config-7","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#default-description","content":"Default description"},"1899":{"id":"/reference/config-8","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#duplicate-commit-description","content":"Duplicate commit description"},"1900":{"id":"/reference/config-9","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#bookmark-listing-order","content":"Bookmark listing order"},"1901":{"id":"/reference/config-10","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#commit-trailers","content":"Commit trailers"},"1902":{"id":"/reference/config-11","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#diff-colors-and-styles","content":"Diff colors and styles"},"1903":{"id":"/reference/config-12","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#diff-format","content":"Diff format"},"1904":{"id":"/reference/config-13","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#color-words-diff-options","content":"Color-words diff options"},"1905":{"id":"/reference/config-14","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#git-diff-options","content":"Git diff options"},"1906":{"id":"/reference/config-15","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#generating-diffs-by-external-command","content":"Generating diffs by external command"},"1907":{"id":"/reference/config-16","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#conflict-marker-style","content":"Conflict marker style"},"1908":{"id":"/reference/config-17","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#set-of-immutable-commits","content":"Set of immutable commits"},"1909":{"id":"/reference/config-18","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#behavior-of-prev-and-next-commands","content":"Behavior of prev and next commands"},"1910":{"id":"/reference/config-19","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#list","content":"List"},"1911":{"id":"/reference/config-20","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#default-template","content":"Default Template"},"1912":{"id":"/reference/config-21","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#log","content":"Log"},"1913":{"id":"/reference/config-22","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#default-revisions","content":"Default revisions"},"1914":{"id":"/reference/config-23","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#prioritize-revsets-in-the-log-over-","content":"Prioritize Revsets in the Log over @"},"1915":{"id":"/reference/config-24","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#default-template-1","content":"Default Template"},"1916":{"id":"/reference/config-25","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#graph-style","content":"Graph style"},"1917":{"id":"/reference/config-26","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#node-style","content":"Node style"},"1918":{"id":"/reference/config-27","page_id":"/reference/config","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/reference/config#wrap-log-con