ting\nworkflows quite easy. Take a look at its README.md as it quite descriptive."],[3381,"Gilad Woloch (@giladwo)"],[2931,"Git"],[371,"git add -p; git commit --amend"],[1344,"git bisect run: run a command to determine which commit introduced a bug."],[424,"git blame <file>"],[399,"git branch"],[411,"git branch --delete <name>"],[405,"git branch -f <name> <revision>"],[408,"git branch -f <name> <revision>"],[402,"git branch <name> <revision>"],[4533,"git checkout (without using jj) in colocated repo no longer abandons\nthe previously checked-out anonymous branch.\n#1042."],[355,"git checkout v1.0.1"],[257,"git clone <source> <destination> [--origin <remote name>]"],[393,"git co <destination>; git cherry-pick <source>"],[250,"Git Command"],[247,"Git command table"],[342,"git commit --amend --only"],[368,"git commit --amend -a"],[374,"git commit --fixup=X; git rebase -i --autosquash X^"],[306,"git commit -a"],[380,"git commit -p"],[426,"Git compatibility"],[285,"git diff <revision>"],[282,"git diff <revision>^ <revision>"],[288,"git diff A B"],[291,"git diff A...B"],[279,"git diff HEAD"],[1905,"Git diff options"],[1193,"Git doesn't record copy info. Instead, it infers it when comparing two trees."],[1245,"Git expects submodules to be in .git/modules, so it will not understand this\nstorage format. To support colocated Git repos, we will have to change Git to\nallow a submodule's gitdir to be in an alternate location (e.g. we could add a\nnew submodule.<name>.gitdir config option). This is a simple change, so it\nshould be feasible."],[261,"git fetch [<remote>]"],[324,"git grep foo"],[254,"git init"],[534,"Git interop: Sapling supports cloning, pushing, and pulling from a remote\nGit repo. jj also does, and it also supports sharing a working copy with a Git\nrepo, so you can use jj and git interchangeably in the same repo."],[459,"Git LFS: No. (#80)"],[309,"git log --oneline --graph --decorate"],[312,"git log --oneline --graph --decorate --branches"],[318,"git log -G stuff"],[321,"git ls-files --cached"],[352,"git merge A"],[265,"git push --all [<remote>]"],[269,"git push <remote> <bookmark name>"],[362,"git rebase --onto B A^ <some descendant bookmark> (may need to rebase other descendant bookmarks separately)"],[365,"git rebase -i A"],[1343,"git rebase -x: runs commands opportunistically as part of rebase."],[359,"git rebase B A (may need to rebase other descendant branches separately)"],[2369,"Git ref"],[273,"git remote add <remote> <url>"],[2241,"Git remote interactions are handled by spawning a git subprocess.\nIf git is not on your OS path, or you want to specify a particular binary,\nyou can:"],[1222,"Git repos in the main Git backend"],[3234,"Git repositories are now colocated by default. Configure\ngit.colocate = false to keep the previous behavior."],[4460,"Git repository managed by the repo tool can now be detected as a \"colocated\"\nrepository.\n#2011"],[327,"git reset --hard (cannot be undone)"],[330,"git reset --hard (same as abandoning a change since Git has no concept of a \"change\")"],[333,"git reset --soft HEAD~"],[336,"git restore <paths>... or git checkout HEAD -- <paths>..."],[396,"git rev-parse --show-toplevel"],[421,"git revert <revision>"],[300,"git rm filename"],[1954,"Git settings"],[294,"git show <revision>"],[345,"git stash"],[276,"git status"],[1209,"Git submodule storage"],[1251,"Git submodules"],[1281,"Git submodules are a feature of Git\nthat allow a repository (submodule) to be embedded inside another repository\n(the superproject). Notably, a submodule is a full repository, complete with its\nown index, object store and ref store. It can be interacted with like any other\nrepository, regardless of the superproject."],[4665,"Git submodules are now ignored completely. Earlier, files present in the\nsubmodule directory in the working copy would become added (tracked), and\nlater removed if you checked out another commit. You can now use git to\npopulate the submodule directory and jj will leave it alone."],[192,"Git submodules are used frequently enough in large Git repos that we will\nprobably need to support them. There are still big open\nquestions around UX."],[1231,"Git submodules may contain submodules themselves, so our chosen storage schemes\nshould support that."],[1288,"Git submodules should be implemented in an order that supports an increasing set\nof workflows, with the goal of getting feedback early and often. When support is\nincomplete, jj should not crash, but instead provide fallback behavior and warn\nthe user where needed."],[1238,"Git submodules will be stored as full jj repos. In the code, jj commands will\nonly interact with the submodule's repo as an entire unit, e.g. it cannot query\nthe submodule's commit backend directly. A well-abstracted submodule will extend\nwell to non-git backends and non-git subrepos."],[1963,"Git subprocessing behavior"],[349,"git switch -c topic main or git checkout -b topic main (may need to stash or commit first)"],[1340,"git test: part of git-branchless. Similar to this proposal for jj run."],[472,"Git tools will have trouble with revisions that contain conflicted files. While\njj renders these files with conflict markers in the working copy, they are\nstored in a non-human-readable fashion inside the repo. Git tools will often\nsee this non-human-readable representation."],[1792,"Git users: The commit ID/hash is what you're used to from Git and should\nmatch what you see when you look at the repository using git log in a Git\ncheckout of the repository. The change ID however, is a new concept, unique to\nJujutsu."],[1794,"Git users: This is a huge difference from Git where the working copy is a\nseparate concept and not yet a commit."],[2482,"git_head(): The Git HEAD target as of the last import."],[2481,"git_refs(): All Git ref targets as of the last import. If a Git ref\nis in a conflicted state, all its possible targets are included."],[1526,"git_refs[\"refs/heads\"] - remotes[\"git\"].branches"],[1529,"git_refs[\"refs/remotes/{remote}\"] - remotes[remote]"],[1527,"git_refs[\"refs/tags\"] - remotes[\"git\"].tags"],[508,"git-branchless: Helps you use a\nbranchless workflow in your Git repo. Supports anonymous branching, undo,\nand faster rebase (git move). Under heavy development and quickly gaining\nnew features."],[3023,"Git-interop: Git is everywhere. We need to have good interop to be adopted."],[456,"git-worktree: No. However, there's native support for multiple working\ncopies backed by a single repo. See the jj workspace family of commands."],[3840,"git.auto-local-bookmark replaces git.auto-local-branch. The latter remains\nsupported for now (at lower precedence than the former)."],[3454,"git.subprocess = false has been deprecated, and the old libgit2\ncode path for fetches and pushes will be removed entirely in 0.30.\nPlease report any remaining issues you have with the Git\nsubprocessing path."],[3589,"git.subprocess is now enabled by default, improving compatibility with Git\nfetches and pushes by spawning an external git process. Users can opt out\nof this by setting git.subprocess = false, but this will likely be removed\nin a future release. Please report any issues you run into."],[504,"Git's \"index\" has\nmultiple roles. One role is as a cache of file system information. Jujutsu has\nsomething similar. Unfortunately, Git exposes the index to the user, which makes\nthe CLI unnecessarily complicated (learning what the different flavors of\ngit reset do, especially when combined with commits and/or paths, usually\ntakes a while). Jujutsu, like Mercurial, doesn't make that mistake."],[4666,"Git's GC could remove commits that were referenced from jj in some cases. We\nare now better at adding Git refs to prevent that.\n#815"],[1227,"Git's protocol is designed for communicating between copies of the same\nrepository. Notably, a Git fetch calculates the list of required objects by\nperforming reachability checks between the refs on the local and the remote\nside. We should expect that this will only work well if the submodule repository\nis stored as a local Git repository."],[2921,"GitBackend"],[513,"GitButler: A Git client that works with multiple\nvirtual branches simultaneously, first-class conflicts, and operations history."],[867,"GitHub CI checks require that the code is formatted with the nightly\nversion of rustfmt. To do this on your computer, install the nightly\ntoolchain and use cargo +nightly fmt."],[1698,"GitHub CLI will have trouble finding the proper Git repository path in jj repos\nthat aren't colocated\n(see issue #1008). You can configure the $GIT_DIR environment variable to\npoint it to the right path:"],[172,"GitHub repo for jj"],[511,"Gitless: Another attempt at providing a simpler\ninterface for Git. Like Jujutsu, does not have an \"index\"/\"staging area\"\nconcept. Also doesn't move the working-copy changes between branches (which\nwe do simply as a consequence of making the working copy a commit)."],[510,"GitUp: A Mac-only GUI for Git. Like Jujutsu, supports\nundo and restoring the repo to an earlier snapshot. Backed by its\nGitUpKit library."],[2390,"Given this history:"],[2519,"Given this history:"],[749,"Giving and gracefully accepting constructive feedback"],[4108,"Glen Choo (@chooglen)"],[4469,"Glen Choo (@chooglen)"],[4548,"Glen Choo (@chooglen)"],[4624,"Glen Choo (@chooglen)"],[4672,"Glen Choo (@chooglen)"],[4407,"glencbz (@glencbz)"],[2321,"Glob characters aren't considered meta characters, but shell quotes are still\nrequired:"],[3318,"Glob patterns now support {foo,bar} syntax. There may be subtle behavior\nchanges as we use the globset library now."],[2327,"glob-i:\"pattern\" or cwd-glob-i:\"pattern\": Like glob: but\ncase-insensitive. For example, glob-i:\"*.TXT\" will match both file.txt\nand FILE.TXT."],[2326,"glob:\"pattern\" or cwd-glob:\"pattern\": Matches file paths with cwd-relative\nUnix-style shell wildcard pattern. For example, glob:\"*.c\" will\nmatch all .c files