,"jj rebase -b A -d B"],[3795,"jj rebase -b can now be used with the --insert-after and --insert-before\noptions, like jj rebase -r and jj rebase -s."],[366,"jj rebase -r C --before B"],[4378,"jj rebase -r gained the ability to rebase a revision A onto a descendant\nof A."],[4144,"jj rebase -r now accepts --insert-after and --insert-before options to\ncustomize the location of the rebased revisions."],[363,"jj rebase -s A -d B"],[3943,"jj rebase -s can now be used with the --insert-after and --insert-before\noptions, like jj rebase -r."],[4694,"jj rebase now accepts a --branch/-b <revision> argument, which can be used\ninstead of -r or -s to specify which commits to rebase. It will rebase the\nwhole branch, relative to the destination. The default mode has changed from\n-r @ to -b @."],[4600,"jj rebase now accepts a new --allow-large-revsets argument that allows the\nrevset in the -d argument to expand to several revisions. For example,\njj rebase -s B -d B- -d C now works even if B is a merge commit."],[4501,"jj rebase now accepts multiple -s and -b arguments. Revsets with\nmultiple commits are allowed with --allow-large-revsets."],[4143,"jj rebase now accepts revsets resolving to multiple revisions with the\n--revisions/-r option."],[3399,"jj rebase now automatically abandons divergent commits if another commit\nwith the same change ID is already present in the destination with identical\nchanges."],[3404,"jj rebase now automatically abandons divergent commits if another commit\nwith the same change ID is already present in the destination with identical\nchanges. To keep these divergent commits, use the --keep-divergent flag."],[4280,"jj rebase now preserves the shape of history even for merge commits where\none parent is an ancestor of another. You can follow the jj rebase by\njj rebase -s <merge commit> -d <single parent> if you want to linearize the\nhistory."],[4727,"jj rebase now requires at least one destination (allowing no arguments was a\nUX bug)."],[4338,"jj rebase now takes the flag --skip-empty, which doesn't copy over commits\nthat would become empty after a rebase."],[4582,"jj resolve --list can now describe the complexity of conflicts."],[3758,"jj resolve no longer removes the executable bit on resolved files when using\nan external merge tool."],[3512,"jj resolve now accepts built-in tools :ours and :theirs."],[3550,"jj resolve now accepts new built-in merge tools :ours and :theirs.\nThese merge tools accept side #1 and side #2 of the conflict respectively."],[4293,"jj resolve now displays the file being resolved."],[4583,"jj resolve now notifies the user of remaining conflicts, if any, on success.\nThis can be prevented by the new --quiet option."],[3662,"jj resolve will now attempt to resolve all conflicted files instead of\nresolving the first conflicted file. To resolve a single file, pass a file\npath to jj resolve."],[331,"jj restore"],[1105,"jj restore --from X; jj diff --from X should be empty, at least when it comes\nto file contents. It may indicate that renamed file have different history."],[4728,"jj restore --to <rev> now restores from the working copy (it used to restore\nfrom the working copy's parent)."],[337,"jj restore <paths>..."],[4440,"jj restore gained a new option --changes-in to restore files\nfrom a merge revision's parents. This undoes the changes that jj diff -r\nwould show."],[3673,"jj restore now supports -i/--interactive selection."],[4500,"jj restore without --from works correctly even if @ is a merge\ncommit."],[422,"jj revert -r <revision> -B @"],[1365,"jj run '$rewrite-tool' -r '$revset'"],[1367,"jj run 'bazel build //some/target:somewhere'"],[1362,"jj run 'cargo +nightly fmt'"],[1361,"jj run 'cargo clippy' -r $revset"],[1368,"jj run 'ninja check-lld'"],[1360,"jj run 'pre-commit run' -r $revset"],[1364,"jj run 'sed /some/test/' -r 'mine() & ~remote_bookmarks(exact:\"origin\")'"],[295,"jj show <revision>"],[4229,"jj show now accepts -T/--template option to render its output using\ntemplate"],[3458,"jj show patches can now be suppressed with --no-patch."],[2210,"jj sign always signs commits, even if they are already signed by the user.\nWhile this is cumbersome for users signing via hardware devices, we cannot\nreliably check if a commit is already signed without creating a signature (see\nthis issue)."],[3529,"jj sign can now sign with PKCS#12 certificates through the gpgsm backend."],[3530,"jj sign will automatically use the gpg key associated with the author's email\nin the absence of a signing.key configuration."],[3811,"jj simplify-parents now supports configuring the default revset when no\n--source or --revisions arguments are provided with the\nrevsets.simplify-parents config."],[1439,"jj sparse list will print the explicit rules"],[1437,"jj sparse set --add exclude:dir:foo/bar adds a new Dir type rule with include = false"],[1436,"jj sparse set --add foo/bar is equal to jj sparse set --add include:dir:foo/bar"],[1438,"jj sparse set --exclude foo/bar as a possible shorthand for the above"],[4523,"jj sparse set now accepts an --edit flag which brings up the $EDITOR to\nedit sparse patterns."],[381,"jj split"],[4029,"jj split --siblings is deprecated in favor of jj split --parallel (to\nmatch jj parallelize)."],[384,"jj split -r <revision>"],[4598,"jj split accepts creating empty commits when given a path. jj split .\ninserts an empty commit between the target commit and its children if any,\nand jj split any-non-existent-path inserts an empty commit between the\ntarget commit and its parents."],[3400,"jj split has gained --message, --insert-before, --insert-after, and\n--destination options."],[3412,"jj split has gained a --message option to set the description of the\ncommit with the selected changes."],[3413,"jj split has gained the ability to place the revision with the selected\nchanges anywhere in the revision tree with the --insert-before,\n--insert-after and --destination command line flags."],[3912,"jj split now lets the user select all changes in interactive mode. This may be used\nto keeping all changes into the first commit while keeping the current commit\ndescription for the second commit (the newly created empty one)."],[4699,"jj split now lets you specify on the CLI which paths to include in the first\ncommit. The interactive diff-editing is not started when you do that."],[4190,"jj split now supports a --siblings/-s option that splits the target\nrevision into siblings with the same parents and children."],[4401,"jj split supports the --interactive flag. (This is already the default if\nno paths are provided.)"],[4447,"jj split will now leave the description empty on the second part if the\ndescription was empty on the input commit."],[4076,"jj split will now refuse to split an empty commit."],[369,"jj squash"],[334,"jj squash --from @-"],[375,"jj squash --into X"],[372,"jj squash -i"],[378,"jj squash -i --from X --into Y"],[4149,"jj squash <path> is now a no-op if the path argument didn't match any paths\n(it used to create new commits with bumped timestamp).\n#3334"],[4511,"jj squash AKA jj amend now accepts a --message option to set the\ndescription of the squashed commit on the command-line."],[4180,"jj squash now accepts --from and --into (also aliased as --to) if -r\nis not specified. It can now be used for all use cases where jj move could\npreviously be used. The --from argument accepts a revset that resolves to\nmore than one revision."],[4052,"jj squash now accepts a --keep-emptied option to keep the source commit."],[4141,"jj squash now accepts a --use-destination-message/-u option that uses the\ndescription of the destination for the new squashed revision and discards the\ndescriptions of the source revisions."],[3843,"jj squash now supports -f/-t shorthands for --from/--[in]to."],[3907,"jj squash: the -k flag can be used as a shorthand for --keep-emptied."],[277,"jj st"],[4124,"jj status no longer scans through the entire history to look for ancestors\nwith conflicts."],[3311,"jj status now collapses fully untracked directories into one line.\nIt still fully traverses them while snapshotting but they won't clutter up\nthe output with all of their contents."],[3610,"jj status now shows untracked files under untracked directories.\n#5389"],[3684,"jj status now shows untracked files when they reside directly under a\ntracked directory. There's still an issue that files under untracked\ndirectories aren't listed. #5389"],[4139,"jj status now supports filtering by paths. For example, jj status . will\nonly list changed files that are descendants of the current directory."],[3993,"jj status will show different messages in a conflicted tree, depending\non the state of the working commit. In particular, if a child commit fixes\na conflict in the parent, this will be reflected in the hint provided\nby jj status"],[1860,"jj supports symlinks on Windows only when they are enabled by the operating\nsystem. This requires Windows 10 version 14972 or higher, as well as Developer\nMode. If those conditions are not satisfied, jj will materialize symlinks as\nordinary files."],[4291,"jj tag list command prints imported git tags."],[6,"JJ TUI"],[418,"jj undo"],[3250,"jj undo can now undo multiple operations progressively by calling it\nrepeatedly, whereas previously, running jj undo twice was previously a no-op\n(it only undid the last change)."],[3243,"jj undo is now sequential: invoking it multiple times in sequence\nrepeatedly undoes actions in the operation log. Previously, jj undo would\nonly undo the most recent operation in the operation log. As a result, a new\njj redo command has been added."],[3609,"jj undo now shows a hint when undoing an undo operation that the user may\nbe looking for jj op restore instead."],[4613,"jj undo now works after jj duplicate."],[3896,"jj unsquash has been deprecated in favor of jj squash and\njj diffedit --restore-descendants."],[3518,"jj unsquash has been removed in favor of jj squash and\njj diffedit --restore-descendants."],[3937,"jj untrack has been renamed to jj file untrack."],[4726,"jj untrack now requires at least one path (allowing no arguments was a UX\nbug)."],[3407,"jj util completion now requires the name of the shell as a positional\nargument and no longer produces Bash completions by default. The deprecated\noptional arguments for different shells have been removed."],[4281,"jj util completion now supports powershell and elvish."],[4279,"jj util completion now takes the shell as a positional argument, not a flag.\nthe previous behavior is deprecated, but supported for now. it will be removed\nin the future."],[3370,"jj util gc --expire=now now passes the corresponding flag to git gc."],[4284,"jj util gc now removes unreachable operation, view, and Git objects."],[3663,"jj util mangen is replaced with jj util install-man-pages, which can\ninstall man pages for all jj subcommands to a given path."],[230,"jj was my first exposure to revsets and at first I was\n\"why would I ever need that\", but now after exercising that math part of my\nbrain that's been stale since high school, they're indispensable"],[577,"jj will contact the remote and check that the actual state of the remote\nbookmark matches jj's record of its last known position. If there is a\nconflict, jj will refuse to push the bookmark. In this case, you need to run\njj git fetch --remote <remote name> and resolve the resulting bookmark\nconflict. Then, you can try jj git push again."],[4426,"jj will fail attempts to snapshot new files larger than 1MiB by default.\nThis behavior\ncan be customized with the snapshot.max-new-file-size config option."],[4054,"jj will look for divergent changes outside the short prefix set even if it\nfinds the change id inside the short prefix set.\n#2476"],[4497,"jj will no longer parse br as a git_ref refs/heads/br when a branch br\ndoes not exist but the git_ref does (this is rare). Use br@git instead."],[3419,"jj will no longer warn about deprecated paths on macOS if the configured\nXDG directory is the deprecated one (~/Library/Application Support)."],[556,"jj will then update main@origin to the new target. If main@origin is\ntracked, jj will also apply the change to the local bookmark main. If the\nlocal target has also been moved compared to main@origin (probably because you\nran jj bookmark set main), then the two updates will be merged. If one is ahead\nof the other, then that target will become the new target. Otherwise, the local\nbookmark will become conflicted (see the \"Conflicts\" section below\nfor details)."],[4518,"jj with no subcommand now defaults to jj log instead of showing help. This\ncommand can be overridden by setting ui.default-command."],[4335,"jj workspace add can now take multiple --revision arguments, which will\ncreate a new workspace with its working-copy commit on top of all the parents,\nas if you had run jj new r1 r2 r3 ...."],[3902,"jj workspace add now accepts a --sparse-patterns=<MODE> option, which\nallows control of the sparse patterns for a newly created workspace: copy\n(inherit from parent; default), full (full working copy), or empty (the\nempty working copy)."],[4377,"jj workspace add now preserves all parents of the old working-copy commit\ninstead of just the first one."],[4371,"jj workspace add now takes a --revision argument."],[4372,"jj workspace forget can now forget multiple workspaces at once."],[3977,"jj workspace forget now abandons the workspace's working-copy commit if it\nwas empty."],[3308,"jj workspace list now accepts -T/--template option to customize its\noutput via templates."],[397,"jj workspace root"],[4595,"jj workspace root prints the root path of the current workspace."],[4294,"jj workspace root was aliased to jj root, for ease of discoverability"],[4531,"jj workspace update-stale now snapshots the working-copy changes before\nupdating to the new working-copy commit."],[5,"JJ-FZF"],[1935,"JJ-INSTRUCTIONS"],[160,"jj-lib is not a stable API, so you may have to make changes to your tool\nwhen the API changes."],[3511,"jj's configuration can now be split into multiple files more easily."],[4370,"jj's stable release can now be installed\nwith cargo binstall jj-cli."],[33,"jjui is a terminal user interface for working with Jujutsu version control system."],[3573,"Jo Liss (@joliss)"],[3574,"Joachim Desroches (@jedesroches)"],[3293,"Joaquín Triñanes (@JoaquinTrinanes)"],[3342,"Joaquín Triñanes (@JoaquinTrinanes)"],[3821,"Joaquín Triñanes (@JoaquinTrinanes)"],[3873,"Joaquín Triñanes (@JoaquinTrinanes)"],[3768,"Jochen Kupperschmidt (@homeworkprod)"],[3575,"Johannes Altmanninger (@krobelus)"],[3386,"Jonas Greitemann (@jgreitemann)"],[3436,"Jonas Greitemann (@jgreitemann)"],[3486,"Jonas Greitemann (@jgreitemann)"],[3634,"Jonathan Frere (@MrJohz)"],[3715,"Jonathan Frere (@MrJohz)"],[3576,"Jonathan Gilchrist (@jgilchrist)"],[3716,"Jonathan Gilchrist (@jgilchrist)"],[4162,"Jonathan Lorimer (@JonathanLorimer)"],[3635,"Jonathan Tan (@jonathantanmy)"],[4008,"Jonathan Tan (@jonathantanmy)"],[4062,"Jonathan Tan (@jonathantanmy)"],[4261,"Jonathan Tan (@jonathantanmy)"],[4309,"Jonathan Tan (@jonathantanmy)"],[952,"jonathantanmy"],[3437,"Josep Mengual (@truita)"],[3343,"Josh Steadmon (@steadmon)"],[3387,"Josh Steadmon (@steadmon)"],[3487,"Josh Steadmon (@steadmon)"],[3636,"Josh Steadmon (@steadmon)"],[3717,"Josh Steadmon (@steadmon)"],[1975,"JSON Schema Support"],[2675,"json(value: Serialize) -> String: Serialize value in JSON format."],[3388,"juemrami (@juemrami)"],[163,"Jujutsu"],[2366,"Jujutsu attempts to resolve a symbol in the following order:"],[95,"Jujutsu automatically records new files in the current working-copy commit and\ndoesn't provide a way to prevent that."],[1755,"jujutsu can be installed from the official openSUSE-Tumbleweed-Oss repository:"],[251,"Jujutsu Command"],[89,"Jujutsu commands may be a little slower in very large repositories due to\nimporting and exporting changes to Git. Most repositories are not noticeably\naffected by this."],[1842,"Jujutsu currently has a setting,\nworking-copy.eol-conversion, similar to\nGit's core.autocrlf, but does not currently honor\n.gitattributes and the core.autocrlf git config, so it is recommended to\nkeep the working-copy.eol-conversion setting and the core.autocrlf git\nconfig in sync."],[140,"Jujutsu defines the changes in a merge commit (and non-merge commits) as the\nchanges made compared to the auto-merged parents. That means that merge commits\nare often empty. As a result, jj revert or a merge commit often results in an\nempty commit. To revert the changes merged in from the second parent, instead\nuse jj restore --from <first parent> ."],[498,"Jujutsu does not have a corresponding concept of a 'current bookmark';\ninstead, you update bookmarks manually. For example, if you start work on top\nof a commit with a bookmark, new commits are created on top of the bookmark,\nthen you issue a later command to update the bookmark."],[4748,"Jujutsu Governance"],[435,"Jujutsu has two backends for storing commits. One of them uses a regular Git\nrepo, which means that you can collaborate with Git users without them even\nknowing that you're not using the git CLI."],[210,"Jujutsu is amazing... I couldn't have come up with anything remotely as\nelegant.It's so rare that a solution attacks the innermost core of a problem so\nthoroughly, I genuinely feel blessed to be in its presence. And also a bit\nvindicated in not even trying to learn to use any of the tools that felt like\nmore crutches stacked upon a sand castle"],[244,"Jujutsu is an alright tool. I guess."],[4758,"Jujutsu is an open source project, led, maintained and designed for a worldwide\ncommunity. Anyone who is interested can join, contribute, and participate in the\ndecision-making process. This document is intended to help you understand how\nyou can do that."],[245,"Jujutsu is my favorite software tool of all time. I am saying this for no\nparticular reason, definitely not because I was paid to."],[218,"Jujutsu is pretty cool, you can even keep most of your existing workflows"],[7,"Jujutsu Kaizen"],[25,"Jujutsu Kaizen is a plugin for Visual Studio Code. The goal of this extension is to bring the great UX of Jujutsu into the VS Code UI.\nIts developers are currently focused on achieving parity for commonly used features of VS Code's built-in Git extension, such as the various operations possible via the Source Control view."],[1821,"Jujutsu keeps a record of all changes you've made to the repo in what's called\nthe \"operation log\". Use the jj op (short for jj operation) family of\ncommands to interact with it. To list the operations, use jj op log:"],[551,"Jujutsu maps its bookmarks to Git branches when interacting with Git repos. For\nexample, jj git push --bookmark foo will push the state of the foo bookmark\nto the foo branch on the Git remote. Similarly, if you create a bar branch\nin the backing Git repo, then a subsequent jj git import will create a bar\nbookmark (reminder: that import happens automatically in\ncolocated repos)."],[1845,"Jujutsu may make incorrect decision on whether a file is a binary file and apply\nline conversion incorrectly, but currently, Jujutsu doesn't support configuring\nline endings conversion for particular files. If this issue is hit, one should\nnot enable the line conversion setting."],[3472,"Jujutsu now uses\nzlib-rs, a\nfast compression library written in Rust. Packagers should remove any\ndependency on CMake and drop the packaging Cargo feature."],[1766,"Jujutsu provides 2 different command-line completion scripts."],[694,"Jujutsu records each operation that modifies the repo in the \"operation log\".\nYou can see the log with jj op log. Each operation object contains a snapshot\nof how the repo looked at the end of the operation. We call this snapshot a\n\"view\" object. The view contains information about where each bookmark, tag, and\nGit ref (in Git-backed repos) pointed, as well as the set of heads in the repo,\nand the current working-copy commit in each workspace. The operation object also\n(in addition to the view) contains pointers to the operation(s) immediately\nbefore it, as well as metadata about the operation, such as timestamps,\nusername, hostname, description."],[552,"Jujutsu records the last seen position of a bookmark on each remote (just like\nGit's remote-tracking branches). This record is updated on every jj git fetch\nand jj git push of the bookmark. You can refer to the remembered remote bookmark\npositions with <bookmark name>@<remote name>, such as jj new main@origin. jj\ndoes not provide a way to manually edit these recorded positions."],[2316,"Jujutsu supports a functional language for selecting a set of files.\nExpressions in this language are called \"filesets\" (the idea comes from\nMercurial). The language\nconsists of file patterns, operators, and functions."],[2357,"Jujutsu supports a functional language for selecting a set of revisions.\nExpressions in this language are called \"revsets\" (the idea comes from\nMercurial). The language\nconsists of symbols, operators, and functions."],[675,"Jujutsu supports a functional language for selecting a set of revisions.\nExpressions in this language are called \"revsets\". See here for\ndetails. We also often use the term \"revset\" for the set of revisions selected\nby a revset."],[2646,"Jujutsu supports a functional language to customize output of commands.\nThe language consists of literals, keywords, operators, functions, and\nmethods."],[11,"Jujutsu UI"],[1115,"Jujutsu uses a snapshot-based model similar to Git's. The algebra for our\nfirst-class conflicts is also based on snapshots and being able to calculate\npatches as differences between states. That means that we have to fit copy\ninformation into that snapshot-based model too ."],[803,"Jujutsu uses Design Docs to drive technical decisions on large projects and it\nis the place to discuss your proposed design or new component. It is a very\nthorough process, in which the design doc must be approved before PRs for the\nfeature will be accepted. It shares some similarities with Rust RFCs but\nmostly addresses technical problems and gauges the technical and social\nconcerns of all stakeholders."],[474,"Jujutsu will ignore Git's staging area. It will not understand merge conflicts\nas Git represents them, unfinished git rebase states, as well as other less\ncommon states a Git repository can be in."],[1841,"Jujutsu works the same on all platforms, but there are some caveats that Windows\nusers should be aware of."],[138,"Jujutsu, like Git, is a snapshot-based VCS. That means that each commit\nlogically records the state of all current files in the repo. The changes in a\ncommit are not recorded but are instead calculated when needed by comparing the\ncommit's state to the parent commit's state. Jujutsu defines the changes in a\ncommit to be relative to the auto-merged parents (if there's only one parent,\nthen that merge is trivial - it's the parent commit's state). As a result, a\nmerge commit that was a clean merge (no conflict resolution, no additional\nchanges) is considered empty. Conversely, if the merge commit contains conflict\nresolutions or additional changes, then it will be considered non-empty."],[1803,"Jujutsu's diff format currently defaults to inline coloring of the diff (like\ngit diff --color-words), so we used --git above to make the diff readable in\nthis tutorial."],[2989,"Jujutsu's lock-free concurrency means that it's possible to update copies of the\nclone on different machines and then let rsync (or Dropbox, or NFS, etc.)\nmerge them. The working copy may mismatch what's supposed to be checked out, but\nno changes to the repo will be lost (added commits, moved bookmarks, etc.). If\nconflicting changes were made, they will appear as conflicts. For example, if a\nbookmark was moved to two different locations, they will appear in jj log in\nboth locations but with a \"?\" after the name, and jj status will also inform\nthe user about the conflict."],[4009,"Julien Vincent (@julienvincent)"],[4262,"Julien Vincent (@julienvincent)"],[4111,"jyn (@jyn514)"],[4263,"jyn (@jyn514)"],[4310,"jyn (@jyn514)"],[3294,"Kaiyi Li (@06393993)"],[3344,"Kaiyi Li (@06393993)"],[3389,"Kaiyi Li (@06393993)"],[3953,"Kaleb Pace (@kalebpace)"],[3769,"Keane Nguyen (@keanemind)"],[2213,"keep: if a change was signed before modification, and it was authored by\nyou, attempt to sign it again after the modification."],[3577,"Kenyon Ralph (@kenyon)"],[3925,"Kevin Liao (@kevincliao)"],[4471,"Kevin Liao (@kevincliao)"],[4552,"Kevin Liao (@kevincliao)"],[953,"kevincliao"],[2102,"Key"],[1926,"Key bindings"],[2600,"Keywords"],[2648,"Keywords represent objects of different types; the types are described in\na follow-up section. In addition to context-specific keywords, the top-level\nobject can be referenced as self."],[4203,"Khionu Sybiern (@khionu)"],[3438,"kkoang (@kkoang)"],[4063,"Kyle J Strand (@BatmanAoD)"],[2672,"label(label: Stringify, content: Template) -> Template: Apply label to\nthe content. The label is evaluated as a space-separated string."],[3733,"Large files in the working copy will no longer cause commands to fail; instead\nthe large files will remain intact but untracked in the working copy."],[1363,"Large scale changes across repositories, local and remote:"],[3390,"Lars Francke (@lfrancke)"],[3822,"Lars Francke (@lfrancke)"],[4747,"Last release before this changelog started."],[2487,"latest(x, [count]): Latest count commits in x, based on committer\ntimestamp. The default count is 1."],[8,"LazyJJ"],[27,"lazyjj is a lazygit inspired TUI for Jujutsu."],[818,"Learning Rust"],[2095,"less -FRX is the default pager in the absence of any other setting, except\non Windows where it is :builtin."],[1131,"Let's first consider the diff from K to M. Looking at just the trees, that\ndiff finds that copy IDs 1,4,5 were affected. By walking their graphs, we find\n1 and 5 are related, while 4 is not. Considering that copy graph (involving IDs\n1 and 5), since foo doesn't exist in the destination and baz doesn't exist\nin the source, we consider it a rename."],[1126,"Let's look at an example of how this model would look in this scenario:"],[1790,"Let's look at that output as it introduces new concepts. You can see two\ncommits: Parent and working copy. Both are identified using two separate\nidentifiers: the \"change ID\" and the \"commit ID\"."],[1132,"Let's now consider the diff from L to M. This is the same as the diff of the\ncommit M2 we'd get by running\njj new L; jj bookmark create M2; jj restore --from M --to M2 (which would\nresult in the commit M2 having the same tree as M). Diffing from L to\nM (or M2) finds 1,2,3,4,5 as changed copy IDs. By walking their graphs, we\nfind that 1,2, and 5 are related, while 3 and 4 are not."],[122,"Let's say we are editing a commit for \"featureA\", and we forgot to run jj\nnew or jj commit before doing some work that belongs in a new commit:"],[1159,"Let's say we rebase M onto L. Since we decided to not automatically\npropagate changes to copies, we will leave the M+(L-K) tree unresolved (i.e.\nwithout making any changes to the three trees). If the user does not resolve\nthe conflict, and instead rebases L back onto K, the conflict will be\nresolved automatically per the usual conflict simplification."],[1160,"Let's say we rebase M onto N. The changes to foo, foo2, and foo3 will\nthen all apply to foo, which means we get a 4-sided conflict."],[1798,"Let's say we want to edit the README file in the repo to say \"Goodbye\"\ninstead of \"Hello\". Start by describing the change (adding a commit message) so\nwe don't forget what we're working on:"],[1181,"Let's say you have a commit with some files you've modified. You now want to\nsync (rebase) that to an updated main branch. If some of the files you modified\nno longer exist on the main branch, we want to figure out if they were renamed\nso we should propagate your changes to the new file location. As described\nearlier, we can do that by finding files that have a different copy ID since the\nlast time you synced with the main branch. However, if there are 10 million new\ncommits on the main branch, there's perhaps tens of thousands of such files\nspread across the entire tree. That can therefore can be very expensive to\ncalculate. We therefore need to be able to get help from a custom backend\nimplementation with this query."],[555,"Let's say you run jj git fetch --remote origin and, during the fetch, jj\ndetermines that the remote's main bookmark has been moved so that its target is\nnow ahead of the local record in main@origin."],[1788,"Let's start by cloning GitHub's Hello-World repo using jj:"],[1832,"Let's try one final command for changing the contents of an existing commit. That\ncommand is jj diffedit, which lets you edit the changes in a commit without\nchecking it out."],[601,"Lets us define the change in a merge commit as being compared to the merged\nparents. That way, we can rebase merge commits correctly (unlike both Git and\nMercurial). That includes conflict resolutions done in the merge commit,\naddressing a common use case for\ngit rerere.\nSince the changes in a merge commit are displayed and rebased as expected,\nevil merges\nare arguably not as evil anymore."],[1378,"Like all commands, jj run will refuse to rewrite public/immutable commits.\nFor private/unpublished revisions, we either amend or reparent the changes,\nwhich are available as command options."],[1462,"Like sparse paths, mapping rules are defined to apply in order and on any\nsave operation will be modified to a minimal canonical form. Thus,\njj map set --from \"\" --to \"\" will always completely wipe the map.\nThe first matching rule in reverse list order determines how a particular\nrepo path should be mapped into the working copy, and likewise how a particular\nworking copy path should be mapped into the repo. For simplicity, the\n'last rule wins' applies both for repo->WC conversions, as well as WC->repo\nconversions, using the same ordering."],[1461,"Like sparse paths, mappings will have a compact text syntax for editing in file\nform, or for adding a rule textually on the CLI:"],[1836,"Line endings conversion"],[2243,"line: split into line hunks (default)"],[3791,"Lines prefixed with \"JJ:\" in commit descriptions and in sparse patterns (from\njj sparse edit) are now stripped even if they are not immediately followed\nby a space. #5004"],[1032,"Link this GitHub PR to the existing Discord notification thread and GitHub\ndiscussion"],[1359,"Linting and Formatting:"],[4472,"Linus Arver (@listx)"],[1717,"Linux"],[2271,"Linux and macOS"],[2158,"Linux: use your favorite package manager, e.g. sudo apt install meld."],[1910,"List"],[398,"List bookmarks/branches"],[2343,"List files in src excluding Rust sources."],[2619,"List type"],[320,"List versioned files in the working copy"],[2620,"List<Trailer> type"],[544,"Listing tracked bookmarks"],[2621,"ListTemplate type"],[2860,"Literal strings may be used, which are interpreted as case-sensitive substring\nmatching."],[693,"Loading an old version of the repo"],[1654,"Local branch name could be stored per remote branch"],[2269,"Location of <PLATFORM_SPECIFIC> dir"],[1082,"Log"],[1912,"Log"],[1704,"Log all ancestors of the current working copy that aren't on any remote:"],[1703,"Log all remote bookmarks that you authored or committed to:"],[1701,"Log all revisions across all local bookmarks that aren't on the main bookmark nor\non any remote:"],[1702,"Log all revisions that you authored, across all bookmarks that aren't on any\nremote:"],[3365,"Log node templates are now specified in toml rather than hardcoded."],[1097,"Log: It should be possible to run something like jj log -p <file> and follow\nthe file backwards when it had been created by copying."],[837,"Logging"],[1497,"logically consistent data model for importing/ex