er concerns in GitHub about the proposal, including details\nabout how and why the concern is especially dire"],[1031,"Share the full text of the proposal as a GitHub pull request (PR)"],[2628,"ShortestIdPrefix type"],[45,"Should I colocate my repository?"],[1403,"Should the command be working copy backend specific?\nHow do we manage the Processes which the command will spawn?\nConfiguration options, User and Repository Wide?"],[1155,"Should we propagate changes to copies? For example, if you've modified file\nfoo and then rebase it onto a commit that copied foo to bar, should we\napply your change to bar too? Mercurial does that but Git doesn't. It's\nparticularly useful when a file has been split in two. For example, let's say\nyou've made various changes in file foo and then rebase those change onto a\ncommit that split foo into foo1 and foo2 (or foo and bar). If we\npropagate the changes to both files, each change will apply successfully in one\nfile (assuming the changes do not overlap with the split boundary). Each change\nwill have a modify/delete conflicts in the other file. Those can relatively\neasily be resolved in favor of the deleted hunk. If we do not propagate changes,\nthen changes that belong in one of the files will instead only appear as\nmodify/delete conflicts in the first file and you will have to manually copy\nover the changes to the copied file."],[1122,"Should we support copy tracking for symlinks? Their history is not very useful\nfor annotation purposes, but knowing the history may at least be useful for\ndetecting directory renames (if all files and symlinks in a directory were\nrenamed)."],[2592,"Show all ancestors of the working copy (like plain git log)"],[2107,"Show all key bindings"],[290,"Show all the changes in A..B"],[2598,"Show commits authored by \"martinvonz\" and containing the word \"reset\" in the\ndescription:"],[2593,"Show commits not on any remote bookmark:"],[2594,"Show commits not on origin (if you have other remotes like fork):"],[293,"Show description and diff of a change"],[2342,"Show diff excluding Cargo.lock."],[284,"Show diff from another change to the current change"],[287,"Show diff from change A to change B"],[281,"Show diff of another change"],[278,"Show diff of the current change"],[2597,"Show local commits leading up to the working copy, as well as descendants of\nthose commits:"],[317,"Show log of commits adding/removing the string \"stuff\""],[314,"Show log of commits not on the main branch"],[2913,"Show machine-readable list of full commit and change IDs:"],[4035,"Show paths to config files when configuration errors occur."],[2596,"Show some important commits (like git --simplify-by-decoration):"],[275,"Show summary of current work and repo status"],[2595,"Show the initial commits in the repo (the ones Git calls \"root commits\"):"],[2591,"Show the parent(s) of the working-copy commit (like git log -1 HEAD):"],[423,"Show what revision and author last modified each line of a file"],[1929,"Showing the ruler on startup"],[1923,"Signature format"],[2629,"Signature type"],[4226,"Signature verification and an explicit sign command will hopefully come soon."],[458,"Signed commits: Yes.\nYou can sign commits automatically by configuration,\nor use the jj sign command."],[2503,"signed(): Commits that are cryptographically signed."],[666,"Similar to a bookmark conflict, when a change is rewritten locally\nand remotely, for example, then the change will be in a conflicted state. We\ncall that a divergent change."],[507,"Similar tools:"],[2225,"Similarly, you can also set the variable git.push to cause jj git push to\npush to a different remote:"],[4071,"Simon Wollwage (@Kintaro)"],[4210,"Simon Wollwage (@Kintaro)"],[2922,"SimpleBackend"],[526,"Simpler and more consistent CLI because the working copy is treated like any\nother commit."],[2517,"Since at_operation(op, x) brings all commits that were visible at the\noperation to the search space, at_operation(op, x) | all() is equivalent to\nat_operation(op, x) | ::(at_operation(op, x | visible_heads()) |\nvisible_heads())."],[127,"Since commit b800 is hidden, it is considered obsolete and jj log (without\narguments) will not show it, nor can it be accessed by its change ID lnvvtrzo.\nHowever, most jj operations work normally on such commits if you refer to them\nby their commit ID."],[1204,"Since copy records are relative to the auto-merged parents, that unfortunately\nmeans that the records will depend on the merge algorithm, so it's possible\nthat a future change to the merge algorithm will make some copy records\ninvalid. We will therefore need to not assume that the copy source exists."],[1244,"Since submodules are full repos, they can contain submodules themselves. Nesting\nis unlikely to complicate any of the core features, since the top-level\nsuperproject/submodule relationship is almost identical to the submodule/nested\nsubmodule relationship."],[100,"Since the changes are already in the working-copy commit, the equivalent to\ngit add -p && git commit/git commit -p/hg commit -i is to split the\nworking-copy commit with jj split -i (or the practically identical\njj commit -i)."],[1246,"Since the Git backend contains a Git repository, an 'obvious' default would be\nto store them in the Git superproject the same way Git does, i.e. in\n.git/modules. Since Git submodules are full repositories that can have\nsubmodules, this storage scheme naturally extends to nested submodules."],[1375,"Since the subprocesses will run in temporary working copies, they\nwon't interfere with the user's working copy. The user can therefore continue\nto work in it while jj run is running."],[2946,"Since there are non-commit backends, the Backend trait should probably be\nrenamed to CommitBackend."],[1243,"Since there is no association between a superproject operation and a submodule\noperation, jj op restore in the superproject will not restore the submodule to\na previous operation. Instead, the appropriate submodule operation(s) will be\ncreated. This is sufficient to preserve the superproject-submodule relationship;\nit precludes \"recursive\" restore (e.g. restoring branches in the superproject\nand submodules) but it seems unlikely that we will need such a thing."],[1182,"Since we are only interested in copy graphs that involve files modified in the\nrebased commit, it should be sufficient if the backend provides a method to\nfetch the whole copy graph for a given copy ID (or list of copy IDs). We would\nthen first find all copy IDs involved in the diff of the rebased commit. Then\nwe query the backend to get the full copy graphs. We then need to walk the copy\ngraphs to see if a node exists in the destination tree."],[4757,"Single-Company Influence"],[3586,"Siva Mahadevan (@svmhdvn)"],[2630,"SizeHint type"],[4016,"Skyler Grey (@Minion3665)"],[1968,"Snapshot settings"],[726,"Snapshot the working copy (which gets recorded as an operation)"],[1267,"Snapshotting new submodule changes"],[804,"So if you want to start building a native backend or the server component for\nJujutsu, you'll need to go through this process."],[1136,"So we get these diffs:"],[1805,"So, let's say we're now done with this change, so we create a new change:"],[963,"solson"],[156,"Solution: Configure Vite to ignore the .jj directory by adding it to the\nserver.watch.ignored array inside your Vite configuration, for example:"],[3786,"Some deprecated commands have been removed (jj move, jj checkout, jj merge)."],[2143,"Some examples:"],[2508,"Some file patterns might need quoting because the expression must also be\nparsable as a revset. For example, . has to be quoted in files(\".\")."],[615,"Some files may contain lines which could be confused for conflict markers. For\ninstance, a line could start with =======, which looks like a Git-style\nconflict marker. To ensure that it's always unambiguous which lines are conflict\nmarkers and which are just part of the file contents, jj sometimes uses\nconflict markers which are longer than normal:"],[2190,"Some fix tools may be stored inside the workspace. For example, a binary may be\nstored inside node_modules. Use the $root variable to create an absolute\npath to such a program:"],[2120,"Some formatters (like delta) require\ngit style diffs for formatting. You can configure this style of\ndiff as the default with the ui.diff-formatter setting. For example:"],[1369,"Some of these use-cases should get a specialized command, as this allows\nfurther optimization. A command could be jj format, which runs a list of\nformatters over a subset of a file in a revision. Another command could be\njj fix, which runs a command like rustfmt --fix or cargo clippy --fix over\na subset of a file in a revision."],[1875,"Some other operations make hidden commits visible. For example, adding a\nbookmark to a hidden commit makes it visible with the assumption that you are\nnow working with that commit again."],[2026,"Some ready-to-use trailer templates are available for frequently used trailers:"],[2183,"Some tools (e.g. vimdiff) can present a multi-way diff but don't resolve\nconflict themselves. When using such tools, jj\ncan help you by populating the output file with conflict markers before starting\nthe merge tool (instead of leaving the output file empty and letting the merge\ntool fill it in). To do that, set the\nmerge-tools.vimdiff.merge-tool-edits-conflict-markers = true option."],[613,"Some tools expect Git-style conflict markers, so Jujutsu also supports Git's\n\"diff3\" style\nconflict markers by setting the ui.conflict-marker-style config option to\n\"git\":"],[166,"Some useful links"],[3884,"Sora (@SoraTenshi)"],[1943,"Sort and remove duplicate lines from a file"],[4700,"Sparse checkouts are now supported. In fact, all working copies are now\n\"sparse\", only to different degrees. Use the jj sparse command to manage\nthe paths included in the sparse checkout."],[457,"Sparse checkouts: No. However, there's native support for sparse\ncheckouts. See the jj sparse command."],[1429,"Sparse patterns are an effectively unordered list of prefix strings:"],[1426,"Sparse Patterns should be versioned with the working copy"],[1428,"Sparse Patterns should support client path remapping"],[1427,"Sparse Patterns should support more flexible matching rules"],[1410,"Sparse Patterns v2 redesign"],[1432,"Sparse Patterns v2 will be stored as objects in the Op Store, referenced\nby a WorkingCopyPatternsId from the active View. They will have a new,\nordered structure which can fully represent previous patterns."],[1434,"SparsePatternsPath rules can be specified on the CLI and in an editor via a\ncompact syntax:"],[1976,"Specifying config on the command-line"],[964,"spectral54"],[2344,"Split a revision in two, putting foo into the second commit."],[206,"Spread the word yourself"],[4225,"SSH"],[4459,"SSH authentication can now use ed25519 and ed25519-sk keys. They still need\nto be password-less."],[4458,"SSH authentication could hang when ssh-agent couldn't be reached\n#1970"],[1950,"SSH Signing"],[4707,"ssh-agent is now detected even if $SSH_AGENT_PID is not set (as long as\n$SSH_AUTH_SOCK is set). This should help at least macOS users where\nssh-agent is launched by default and only $SSH_AUTH_SOCK is set."],[2933,"StackedTable"],[2971,"StackedTable (actually ReadonlyTable and MutableTable) is a simple disk\nformat for storing key-value pairs sorted by key. The keys have to have the same\nsize but the values can have different sizes. We use our own format because we\nwant lock-free concurrency and there doesn't seem to be an\nexisting key-value store we could use."],[1005,"Stage 1: Advance Notice of Effort"],[1006,"Stage 2: Proposal Review Period"],[1007,"Stage 3: Proposal Voting Period"],[1008,"Stage 4: Implementation"],[450,"Staging area: Kind of. The staging area will be ignored. For example,\njj diff will show a diff from the Git HEAD to the working copy. There are\nways of fulfilling your use cases without a staging\narea."],[710,"Stale working copy"],[1731,"Standard completions"],[348,"Start working on a new change based on the main bookmark/branch"],[1517,"state = new|tracking doesn't exist in the current model. It's determined\nby git.auto-local-bookmark config."],[777,"State of the Feature as of $VERSION (optional)"],[4359,"Status messages are now printed to stderr."],[965,"steadmon"],[53,"Step 1: Find the commit ID for the \"last good version\""],[54,"Step 2: Create a new change on top of the original revision"],[55,"Step 3: Move any bookmarks to the original revision"],[56,"Step 4: Abandon the unwanted revision"],[3645,"Stephan Hügel (@urschrei)"],[3354,"Stephen Jennings (@jennings)"],[3726,"Stephen Jennings (@jennings)"],[3778,"Stephen Jennings (@jennings)"],[3885,"Stephen Jennings (@jennings)"],[3932,"Stephen Jennings (@jennings)"],[3959,"Stephen Jennings (@jennings)"],[4017,"Stephen Jennings (@jennings)"],[4268,"Stephen Jennings (@jennings)"],[4315,"Stephen Jennings (@jennings)"],[3495,"Steve Fink (@hotsphink)"],[4211,"Steve Klabnik (@steveklabnik)"],[1384,"Stop: Signal a fatal failure and cancel any scheduled work that has not\nyet started running, but let any already started subprocess finish. Notify the\nuser about the failed command and display the generated error from the\nsubprocess."],[2983,"Storage"],[4566,"Storage of the \"HEAD@git\" reference changed and can now have conflicts.\nOperations written by a new jj binary will have a \"HEAD@git\" reference that\nis not visible to older binaries."],[2917,"Storage-independent APIs"],[2923,"Store"],[1223,"Store Git submodules as alternate Git backends"],[1266,"Storing submodules"],[1865,"Strategy 1: Abandon one of the commits"],[1866,"Strategy 2: Duplicate and abandon"],[1867,"Strategy 3: Squash the commits together"],[1868,"Strategy 4: Ignore the divergence"],[2632,"String literals"],[3851,"String literals in filesets, revsets and templates now support hex bytes\n(with \\e as escape / shorthand for \\x1b)."],[2845,"String literals must be surrounded by single or double quotes (' or \").\nA double-quoted string literal supports the following escape sequences:"],[2351,"String patterns"],[3981,"String patterns now support case‐insensitive matching by suffixing any\npattern kind with -i. mine() uses case‐insensitive matching on your email\naddress unconditionally. Only ASCII case folding is currently implemented,\nbut this will likely change in the future."],[3982,"String patterns now support regex:\"pattern\"."],[2631,"String type"],[2633,"Stringify type"],[2674,"stringify(content: Stringify) -> String: Format content to string. This\neffectively removes color labels."],[2634,"StringPattern type"],[819,"Style guide"],[991,"Style guide"],[2309,"Subcommands are space-separated and matched by prefix."],[2493,"subject(pattern): Commits that have a subject matching the given string\npattern. A subject is the first line of the description\n(without newline character.)"],[4787,"Submit feedback about planned features, use cases, or bugs"],[4783,"Submit high-quality bug reports, reproduce reported bugs, and verifying fixes"],[4784,"Submit patches or pull requests"],[4789,"Submitting a single bug report and never returning"],[1300,"Submodule branches can be modified"],[1310,"Submodule conflicts automatically resolve to the 'correct' submodule commits,\ne.g. a merge between superproject commits creating a merge of the submodule\ncommits."],[1295,"Submodule contents are populated in the working copy"],[1294,"Submodule history and branches can be viewed"],[182,"Submodule support"],[1285,"submodule<submodule-name>.path contains the path from the root of the tree\nto the gitlink being described."],[1286,"submodule<submodule-name>.url contains the url to clone the submodule\nfrom."],[1301,"Submodules and their branches can be pushed to their remote"],[1292,"Submodules can be cloned anew"],[453,"Submodules: No. They will not show up in the working copy, but they will\nnot be lost either."],[470,"Such interleaving can happen unknowingly. For example, some IDEs can cause\nit because they automatically run git fetch in the background from time to\ntime."],[2136,"Suggestion: If possible, it is recommended to try an external diff tool like\nMeld (see below for some other possibilities)\nfor splitting commits and other diff editing, in addition to the built-in diff\neditor. It is good to know the capabilities of both. The built-in diff editor\ndoes not require external tools to be available, is faster for tasks like\npicking hunks, and does not require leaving the terminal. External tools give\nyou the flexibility of picking out portions of lines from the diff or even\narbitrarily editing the text of the files."],[776,"Summary"],[821,"Summary"],[1424,"Summary: This Document documents a redesign of the sparse command and\nit's internal storage format in jj, in order to facilitate several desirable\nimprovements for large repos. It covers both the migration path and the planned\nend state."],[1093,"Summary: This Document documents an approach to tracking and detecting copy\ninformation in jj repos, in a way that is compatible with both Git's detection\nmodel and with custom backends that have more complicated tracking of copy\ninformation. This design affects the output of diff commands as well as the\nresults of rebasing across remote copies."],[1338,"Summary: This Document documents the design of a new run command for\nJujutsu which will be used to seamlessly integrate with build systems, linters\nand formatters. This is achieved by running a user-provided command or script\nacross multiple revisions. For more details, read the\nUse-Cases of jj run."],[1296,"Superproject gitlink can be updated to an existing submodule commit"],[4034,"Support background filesystem monitoring via watchman triggers enabled with\nthe core.watchman.register_snapshot_trigger = true config."],[180,"Support for copies and renames"],[4640,"Support for open commits has been dropped. The ui.enable-open-commits config\nthat was added in 0.5.0 is no longer respected. The jj open/close commands\nhave been deleted."],[4396,"Support for the Watchman filesystem monitor is now bundled by default. Set\ncore.fsmonitor = \"watchman\" in your repo to enable."],[427,"Supported features"],[1215,"Supporting future extensions"],[112,"Suppose you have a commit \"Add new feature\":"],[2189,"Suppose you have a list of words in a text file in your repository, and you want\nto keep the file sorted alphabetically and remove any duplicate words."],[2192,"Suppose you want to keep only the 10 smallest numbers in a text file that\ncontains one number on each line. This can be accomplished with sort and\nhead, but execution order is important."],[2186,"Suppose you want to use clang-format to format your *.c and *.h files,\nas well as sorting their #include directives."],[110,"Suppose your repository tracks a file like secret_config.json, and you make\nsome changes to that file to work locally. Since Jujutsu automatically commits\nthe working copy, there's no way to prevent Jujutsu from committing changes to\nthe file. But, you never want to push those changes to the remote repository."],[2680,"surround(prefix: Template, suffix: Template, content: Template) -> Template:\nSurround non-empty content with texts such as parentheses."],[1840,"Symbolic link support"],[2347,"Symbols"],[2980,"Syncing with rsync, NFS, Dropbox, etc"],[3447,"T6 (@tjjfvi)"],[2367,"Tag name"],[1540,"tags (not implemented, but should be the same as branches)"],[1547,"tags (not implemented, but should be the same as branches)"],[443,"Tags: Partial. You can check out tagged commits by name (pointed to be\neither annotated or lightweight tags), but you cannot create new tags."],[2480,"tags([pattern]): All tag targets. If pattern is specified,\nthis selects the tags whose name match the given string\npattern. For example, tags(v1) would match the\ntags v123 and rev1 but not the tag v2. If a tag is\nin a conflicted state, all its possible targets are included."],[4391,"Tal Pressman (@talpr)"],[4480,"Tal Pressman (@talpr)"],[4556,"Tal Pressman (@talpr)"],[4633,"Tal Pressman (@talpr)"],[4072,"Tal Pressman (@tp-woven)"],[1175,"TBD"],[1528,"TBD: \"HEAD\" - remotes[\"git\"].head (unused)"],[1249,"Teach jj to use multiple commit backends and store Git submodules as Git\nbackends. Since submodules are separate from the 'main' backend, a repository\ncan use whatever backend it wants as its 'main' one, while still having Git\nsubmodules in the 'alternate' Git backends."],[2904,"Template fragments are usually labeled with the command name, the context (or\nthe top-level object), and the method names. You can customize the output\ncolors by using these labels."],[3600,"Template functions truncate_start() and truncate_end() gained an optional\nellipsis parameter; passing this prepends or appends the ellipsis to the\ncontent if it is truncated to fit the maximum width."],[2635,"Template type"],[3678,"Templater now supports list.filter(|x| ..) method."],[2599,"Templates"],[2935,"Templates"],[3415,"Templates can now do arithmetic on integers with the +, -, *, /, and\n% infix operators."],[3605,"Templates now have format_short_operation_id(id) function for users to\ncustomize the default operation id representation."],[4452,"templates now support additional string\nmethods .starts_with(x), .ends_with(x)\n.remove_prefix(x), .remove_suffix(x), and .substr(start, end)."],[4399,"Templates now support an additional escape: \\0. This will output a literal\nnull byte. This may be useful for e.g.\njj log -T 'description ++ \"\\0\"' --no-graph to output descriptions only, but\nbe able to tell where the boundaries are"],[3258,"Templates now support any() and all() methods on lists to check whether\nany or all elements satisfy a predicate. Example: parents.any(|c| c.mine())\nreturns true if any parent commit is authored by the user."],[3366,"Templates now support json(x) function to serialize values in JSON format."],[4227,"Templates now support logical operators: ||, &&, !"],[3601,"Templates now support stringify(x) function and string method\n.escape_json(). The latter serializes the string in JSON format. It is\nuseful for making machine-readable templates by escaping problematic\ncharacters like \\n."],[3792,"Templates now support the == and != logical operators for Boolean,\nInteger, and String types."],[3746,"Templates now support the >=, >, <=, and < relational operators for\nInteger types."],[4228,"Templates now support the self keyword, which is the current commit in jj\nlog/obslog templates."],[3602,"Templates now support trim(), trim_start() and trim_end() methods\nwhich remove whitespace from the start and end of a String type."],[3595,"templates.annotate_commit_summary is renamed to templates.file_annotate,\nand now has an implicit self parameter of type AnnotationLine, instead of\nCommit. All methods on Commit can be accessed with commit.method(), or\nself.commit().method()."],[2064,"templates.config_list for jj config list"],[2071,"templates.evolog for jj evolog"],[2072,"templates.log for jj log"],[4177,"templates.log_node"],[2078,"templates.log_node for commits (with Option<Commit> keywords)"],[2074,"templates.op_log for jj op log"],[4178,"templates.op_log_node"],[2079,"templates.op_log_node for operations (with Operation keywords)"],[2075,"templates.op_show for jj op show"],[2073,"templates.show for jj show"],[344,"Temporarily put away the current change"],[541,"Terminology summary"],[203,"Testimonials"],[3326,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[3374,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[3422,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[3473,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[3505,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[3556,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[3615,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[3699,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[3759,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[3813,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[3860,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[3918,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[3945,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[3998,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[4055,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[4100,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[4150,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[4193,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[4249,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[4300,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[4323,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[4341,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[4382,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[4404,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[4461,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[4540,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[4617,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[4668,"Thanks to the people who made this release happen!"],[1828,"That will bring up the built-in diff editor with a\ndiff of the changes in the \"ABCD\" commit. Expand the file by clicking on (+)\nor with right arrow, then select the sections/line to include by clicking or\nusing space. Once complete, press c to confirm changes, or q to exit without\nsaving. You can also use the mouse to click on the menu items to see more\noptions (keyboard navigation is currently limited)."],[1206,"That works for calculating the resulting tree, but it does not seem to allow for\ndoing the conflict algebra we currently do. That means that things like\nparallelizing commits and then serializing them again would lose copy\ninformation."],[1994,"That's probably enough TOML to keep you out of trouble but the syntax guide is\nvery short if you ever need to check."],[4423,"The --allow-large-revsets flag for jj rebase and jj new can no longer be\nused for allowing duplicate destinations. Include the potential duplicates\nin a single expression instead (e.g. jj new 'all:x|y')."],[4422,"The --allow-large-revsets flag for jj rebase and jj new was replaced by\na all: before the revset. For example, use jj rebase -d 'all:foo-'\ninstead of jj rebase --allow-large-revsets -d 'foo-'."],[4129,"The --revision option of jj rebase is renamed to --revisions. The short\nalias -r is still supported."],[3256,"The --what flag on jj undo is deprecated. Consider using\njj op restore --what instead."],[4562,"The -i option to jj restore was removed in favor of new --from/--to\noptions to jj diffedit."],[4610,"The -R/--repository path must be a valid workspace directory. Its\nancestor directories are no longer searched."],[4421,"The : revset operator is deprecated. Use :: instead. We plan to delete the\n: form in jj 0.15+."],[4430,"The ..x revset is now evaluated to root()..x, which means the root commit\nis no longer included."],[3411,"The .normal_hex() method will be removed from the CommitId template type.\nIt's useful only for the ChangeId type."],[926,"The .rs files generated from .proto files are included in the repository,\nand there is a GitHub CI check that will complain if they do not match."],[3540,"The 'how to resolve conflicts' hint that is shown when conflicts appear can\nbe hidden by setting hints.resolving-conflicts = false."],[4717,"The \"(no name/email configured)\" placeholder value for name/email will now be\nreplaced if once you modify a commit after having configured your name/email."],[130,"The \"featureA\" change is currently at commit 31a:"],[3967,"The \"fileset\" language is now enabled by default. It can\nstill be disabled by setting ui.allow-filesets=false."],[4596,"The [alias] config section was renamed to [aliases]. The old name is\nstill accepted for backwards compatibility for some time."],[2362,"The @ expression refers to the working copy commit in the current workspace.\nUse <workspace name>@ to refer to the working-copy commit in another\nworkspace. Use <name>@<remote> to refer to a remote-tracking bookmark."],[1810,"The @ indicates the working-copy commit. The first ID on a line\n(e.g. \"mpqrykyp\" above) is the change ID. The second ID is the commit ID. You\ncan give either ID to commands that take revisions as arguments. We will\ngenerally prefer change IDs because they stay the same when the commit is\nrewritten."],[4702,"The $JJ_CONFIG environment variable can now point to a directory. If it\ndoes, all files in the directory will be read, in alphabetical order."],[3752,"The $NO_COLOR environment variable must now be non-empty to be respected."],[4688,"The $NO_COLOR environment variable no longer\noverrides the ui.color configuration if explicitly set."],[4703,"The $VISUAL environment is now respected and overrides $EDITOR. The new\nui.editor config has higher priority than both of them. There is also a new\n$JJ_EDITOR environment variable, which has even higher priority than the\nconfig."],[1812,"The 00000000 commit (change ID zzzzzzzz) is a virtual commit that's called\nthe \"root commit\". It's the root commit of every repo. The root()\nfunction in the revset matches it."],[1129,"The 2:bar->1:foo means that copy ID 2 (i.e. hash of the CopyHistory\nstruct) has file bar, which was copied from copy ID 1, where it was\ncalled foo."],[2355,"The all: modifier"],[3306,"The all: revset modifier and ui.always-allow-large-revsets setting is\nplanned to be removed in a future release.\n#6016"],[4395,"The ancestors() revset function now takes an optional depth argument\nto limit the depth of the ancestor set. For example, use jj log -r\n'ancestors(@, 5) to view the last 5 commits."],[3367,"The ANSI 256-color palette can be used when configuring colors. For example,\ncolors.\"diff removed token\" = { bg = \"ansi-color-52\", underline = false }\nwill apply a dark red background on removed words in diffs."],[1100,"The APIs should be defined in a way that makes it easy for custom backends to\nignore copy info entirely until they are ready to implement it."],[3255,"The argument <operation> of jj undo is deprecated in favor of\njj op revert <operation>."],[4565,"The author/committer templates now display both name and email. Use\nauthor.name()/committer.name() to extract the name."],[2945,"The Backend trait defines the interface each\ncommit backend needs to implement. The current in-tree commit backends\nare GitBackend\nand SimpleBackend."],[1133,"The bar and baz files have unrelated copy graphs, i.e. the copy graphs for\nthe bar file in commit L and the bar file in commit M are disjoint, and\nthe same is true for the baz file. Therefore, we break up their diffs into two\nseparate diffs for each file."],[1390,"The base command of any jj command should be usable. By default jj run works\non the @ the current working copy."],[4375,"The branches/tags/git_refs/git_head template keywords now return a\nlist of RefNames. They were previously pre-formatted strings."],[3552,"The built-in diff editor now correctly handles deleted files.\n#3702"],[3553,"The built-in diff editor now correctly retains the executable bit on newly\nadded files when splitting. #3846"],[2116,"The built-in pager does not support mouse input."],[2101,"The built-in pager supports both navigation via arrows and Vim-style navigation.\nBeyond that, here are some useful keybindings for the pager:"],[1983,"The built-in settings. These cannot be edited. They can be viewed in the\ncli/src/config/ directory in jj's source repo."],[4146,"The builtin diff editor now allows empty files to be selected during\njj split."],[3420,"The builtin diff editor now correctly handles splitting changes where a file\nis replaced by a directory of the same name.\n#5189"],[3470,"The builtin diff editor now tries to preserve unresolved conflicts.\n#4963"],[4575,"The builtin jj update and jj up aliases for jj checkout have been\ndeleted."],[3668,"The builtin pager is switched to\nstreampager. It can handle large\ninputs better and can be configured."],[3892,"The builtin template branch_list has been renamed to bookmark_list as part\nof the jj branch deprecation."],[4692,"The checkouts template keyword is now called working_copies, and\ncurrent_checkout is called current_working_copy."],[1831,"The child change (\"ABCD\" in our case) will have the same content state after\nthe jj squash command. That means that you can move any changes you want into\nthe parent change, even if they touch the same word, and it won't cause any\nconflicts."],[161,"The CLI is not stable either, so you may need to make your tool detect the\ndifferent versions and call the right command."],[86,"The colocation documentation describes the\ndrawbacks but the most\nimportant ones are:"],[3838,"The color label op_log from the [colors] config section now only\napplies to the op log and not to the other places operations are displayed. In\nalmost all cases, if you configured op_log before, you should use the new\noperation label instead."],[3536,"The command jj config list now supports showing the origin of each variable\nvia the builtin_config_list_detailed template."],[2126,"The command jj util exec will simply run any command you pass to it as an\nargument. Additional arguments are passed through. Here are some examples:"],[1351,"The command should build enough infrastructure for jj test, jj fix and\njj format."],[1354,"The command shouldn't be too smart, as too many assumptions about workflows\nmakes the command confusing for users."],[2942,"The commit backend to use when loading a repo is specified in\nthe .jj/repo/store/type file. There are similar files for the other backends\n(.jj/repo/index/type, .jj/repo/op_store/type, .jj/repo/op_heads/type)."],[2937,"The commit data model is similar\nto Git's object model\n, but with some differences."],[4653,"The commit ID was moved from first on the line in jj log output to close to\nthe end. The goal is to encourage users to use the change ID instead, since\nthat is generally more convenient, and it reduces the risk of creating\ndivergent commits."],[844,"The commit message should describe the changes in the commit;\nthe PR description can even be empty, but feel free to include a personal\nmessage. We start the commit message with <topic>:  and don't use\nconventional commits. This means if\nyou modified a command in the CLI, use its name as the topic, e.g.\nnext/prev: <your-modification> or conflicts: <your-modification>. We don't\ncurrently have a specific guidelines on what to write in the topic field, but\nthe reviewers will help you provide a topic if you have difficulties choosing\nit. How to Write a Git Commit Message is a good\nguide if you're new to writing good commit messages. We are not particularly\nstrict about the style, but please do explain the reason for the change unless\nit's obvious."],[4075,"The commit_summary_no_branches template is superseded by\ntemplates.branch_list."],[167,"The complete list of the available documentation pages is located in\nthe sidebar on the left of the page. The sidebar may be hidden; if so,\nyou can open it either by widening your browser window or by clicking\non the hamburger menu that appears in this situation."],[2976,"The concept is copied from Mercurial, but the syntax is different. The main\ndifference is that the top-level expression is a template expression, not a\nstring like in Mercurial. There is also no string interpolation (e.g.\n\"Commit ID: {node}\" in Mercurial)."],[439,"The configuration of remotes ([remote \"<name>\"]). Only the names and URLs\nare respected (simple fetch refspecs are respected when branches are not\nexplicitly specified on the CLI, and only the last\npushurl is respected)."],[2021,"The configuration works identically to using the --sort option for\njj bookmark list. The following sort keys are supported: name, author-name,\nauthor-email, author-date, committer-name, committer-email,\ncommitter-date. Suffix the key with - to sort in descending order. Multiple\nkeys can be supplied here, the first key is the most significant."],[3252,"The conflict label used for coloring log graph nodes was renamed to\nconflicted."],[1174,"The copy graph contains all past paths and copy IDs of a file, so when doing\njj log <filename>, we might want to translate that to a revset that's similar\nto files() but matches specific (path, copy ID) pairs instead of specific\npaths."],[4772,"The current list of Maintainers:"],[1121,"The data structure might look like this:"],[1045,"The deadline must be announced at the beginning of the voting period -\nonce voting has begun, the deadline cannot change."],[598,"The deeper understanding of conflicts has many advantages:"],[1557,"The default branch will be tracked regardless of git.auto_local_branch\nconfig. This isn't technically needed, but will help users coming from Git."],[571,"The default can be changed by setting the config git.auto-local-bookmark = true.\nThen, jj git fetch tracks every newly fetched bookmark with a local bookmark.\nBranches that already existed before the jj git fetch are not affected. This\nis similar to Mercurial, which fetches all its bookmarks (equivalent to Git's\nbranches) by default."],[3989,"The default commit description template\ncan now be configured by templates.draft_commit_description."],[2128,"The default editor is set via ui.editor, though there are several places to\nset it. The priority is as follows (environment variables are marked with\na $):"],[3240,"The default editor on Unix is now nano instead of pico."],[4425,"The default editor on Windows is now Notepad instead of pico."],[1711,"The default for both git.fetch and git.push is \"origin\"."],[3965,"The default immutable_heads() set\nnow includes untracked_remote_branches() with the assumption that untracked\nbranches aren't managed by you. Therefore, untracked branches are no longer\ndisplayed in jj log by default."],[2037,"The default is 3."],[4577,"The default log format now uses the committer timestamp instead of the author\ntimestamp."],[4219,"The default log output no longer lists all tagged heads. Set revsets.log =\n\"@ | ancestors(immutable_heads().., 2) | heads(immutable_heads())\" to restore\nthe old behavior."],[2093,"The default pager is can be set via ui.pager or the PAGER environment\nvariable. The priority is as follows (environment variables are marked with\na $):"],[4125,"The default template aliases were replaced as follows:"],[2908,"The default templates and aliases() are defined in the [templates] and\n[template-aliases] sections of the config respectively. The exact definitions\ncan be seen in the cli/src/config/templates.toml file in jj's source\ntree."],[2067,"The default value for revsets.log is\n'present(@) | ancestors(immutable_heads().., 2) | present(trunk())'."],[3249,"The deprecated --config-toml flag has been removed. Use\n--config=NAME=VALUE or --config-file=PATH instead."],[3657,"The deprecated --siblings options for jj split has been removed.\njj split --parallel can be used instead."],[3405,"The deprecated --skip-empty flag for jj rebase has been removed. Use the\n--skip-emptied flag instead."],[3656,"The deprecated -l short alias for --limit in jj log, jj op log\nand jj obslog has been removed. The -n short alias can be used instead."],[3739,"The deprecated [alias] config section is no longer respected. Move command\naliases to the [aliases] section."],[3247,"The deprecated config option git.auto-local-branch has been removed. Use\ngit.auto-local-bookmark instead."],[3658,"The deprecated fix.tool-command config option has been removed."],[3406,"The deprecated jj branch subcommands have been removed. Use the jj\nbookmark subcommands instead."],[3248,"The deprecated Signature.username() template method has been removed. Use\nSignature.email().local() instead."],[4037,"The descendants() revset function now accepts an optional depth argument;\nlike the ancestors() depth argument, it limits the depth of the set."],[3603,"The description of commits backed out by jj backout can now be configured\nusing templates.backout_description."],[4567,"The description template keyword is now empty if no description set.\nUse if(description, description, \"(no description set)\\n\") to get back\nthe previous behavior."],[1125,"The details of the algorithm is left for the implementation. The following\nsections provide some examples to hopefully show that it's feasible."],[3679,"The diff commit template keyword now supports custom formatting via\ndiff.files(). For example, diff.files().map(|e| e.path().display()) prints\nchanged file paths."],[4573,"The diff.format config option is now called ui.diff.format. The old name\nis still supported for now."],[3680,"The diff.stat() template method now provides methods to get summary values."],[908,"The different versions of documentation are managed and deployed with\nmike, which can be run with\nuv run mike."],[1993,"The docs below refer to keys in text using dotted notation, but example\nblocks will use heading notation to be unambiguous. If you are confident with TOML\nthen use whichever suits you in your config. If you mix dotted keys and headings,\nyou must put the dotted keys before the first heading."],[892,"The documentation for jj is automatically published online at\nhttps://jj-vcs.github.io/jj/."],[854,"The documentation website sources are Markdown files located in the docs/\ndirectory. You do not need to\nknow Rust to work with them. See below for instructions on how to preview the\nHTML docs as you edit the Markdown files.\nDoing so is optional, but recommended."],[1768,"The dynamic completion script provides completions for jj subcommands and\noptions, as well as additional completions, including bookmarks,\naliases, revisions, operations and files. Dynamic completions can be context\naware, for example they respect the global flags --repository and\n--at-operation as well as some command-specific ones like --revision,\n--from, and --to."],[2017,"The editor content of a commit description can be populated by the\ndraft_commit_description template. self is a Commit\nobject."],[3453,"The editor-*.jjdescription files passed to your editor by e.g. jj describe\nare now written to your system's temporary directory instead of .jj/repo/."],[2765,"The email field of a signature may or may not look like an email address. It may\nbe empty, may not contain the symbol @, and could in principle contain\nmultiple @s."],[4586,"The empty condition in templates is true when the commit makes no change to\nthe three compared to its parents."],[3398,"The experimental support from release 0.29.0 for transferring the change ID\nto/from Git remotes has been enabled by default. The change ID is stored in\nthe Git commit itself (in a commit header called change-id), which means\nit will be transferred by regular git push etc. Please let us know if you\nrun into any problems with it. You can disable it setting\ngit.write-change-id-header. Note that some Git remotes (e.g GitLab) and\nsome Git commands (e.g. git rebase) do not preserve the change ids when\nthey rewrite commits."],[2047,"The external diff tool can also be enabled by diff --tool <name> argument.\nFor the tool named <name>, command arguments can be configured as follows."],[2972,"The file format contains a lookup table followed by concatenated values. The\nlookup table is a sorted list of keys, where each key is followed by the\nassociated value's offset in the concatenated values."],[3984,"The file() revset function now accepts fileset as argument."],[2267,"The files in the conf.d directory are loaded in lexicographic order. This allows\nconfigs to be split across multiple files and combines well\nwith Conditional Variables."],[1990,"The first thing to remember is that the value of a setting (the part to the\nright of the = sign) should be surrounded in quotes if it's a string."],[3526,"The flags --all and --tracked on jj git push by themself do not cause\ndeleted bookmarks to be pushed anymore, as an additional safety measure. They\ncan now be combined with --deleted instead."],[2575,"The following aliases are built-in and used for certain operations. These functions\nare defined as aliases in order to allow you to overwrite them as needed.\nSee revsets.toml\nfor a comprehensive list."],[3660,"The following change introduced in 0.25.0 is reverted:"],[1999,"The following colors are available:"],[3735,"The following configuration variables are now parsed strictly:\ncolors.<labels>, git.abandon-unreachable-commits,\ngit.auto-local-bookmark, git.push-bookmark-prefix, revsets.log,\nrevsets.short-prefixes, signing.backend, operation.hostname,\noperation.username, ui.allow-init-native, ui.color,\nui.default-description, ui.progress-indicator, ui.quiet, user.email,\nuser.name"],[3652,"The following deprecated commands have been removed:"],[3520,"The following deprecated revset functions have been removed:"],[3524,"The following deprecated template functions have been removed:"],[2069,"The following example will prioritize the change with the description \"megamerge\" with a fallback\nto trunk() in case no such change exists:"],[2663,"The following functions are defined."],[437,"The following list describes which Git features Jujutsu is compatible with. For\na comparison with Git, including how workflows are different, see the\nGit-comparison doc."],[2684,"The following methods are defined."],[2725,"The following methods are defined."],[2730,"The following methods are defined."],[2734,"The following methods are defined."],[2753,"The following methods are defined."],[2766,"The following methods are defined."],[2796,"The following methods are defined."],[2810,"The following methods are defined."],[2816,"The following methods are defined."],[2865,"The following methods are defined."],[2873,"The following methods are defined."],[2878,"The following methods are defined."],[2901,"The following methods are defined."],[2779,"The following methods are defined. See also the List type."],[2782,"The following methods are defined. See also the List type."],[697,"The following operators are supported:"],[2651,"The following operators are supported."],[2332,"The following operators are supported. x and y below can be any fileset\nexpressions."],[2372,"The following operators are supported. x and y below can be any revset, not\nonly symbols."],[2323,"The following patterns are supported. In all cases, we do not mention any shell\nquoting that might be necessary, and the quotes around \"path\" are optional if\nthe path has no special characters."],[1101,"The following sections describe some scenarios and how we would ideally handle\nthem."],[2124,"The following technique just provides a convenient syntax for running\narbitrary code on your system. Using it irresponsibly may cause damage\nranging from breaking the behavior of jj undo to wiping your file system.\nExercise the same amount of caution while writing these aliases as you would\nwhen typing commands into the terminal!"],[2165,"The following tools can be used out of the box, as long as they are installed:"],[3233,"The format is based on Keep a Changelog,\nand this project adheres\nto Semantic Versioning."],[4513,"The formatter now supports a \"default\" color that can override another color\ndefined by a parent style."],[907,"The full jj website includes the documentation for several jj versions\n(prerelease, latest release, and the older releases). The top-level\nURL https://jj-vcs.github.io/jj redirects to\nhttps://jj-vcs.github.io/jj/latest, which in turn redirects to\nthe docs for the last stable version."],[2090,"The function must return a timestamp because the return value will likely be\nformatted with format_timestamp()."],[2964,"The git module contains functionality for interoperating with a Git repo, at a\nhigher level than the GitBackend. The GitBackend is restricted by\nthe Backend trait; the git module is specifically for Git-backed repos. It\nhas functionality for importing refs from the Git repo and for exporting to refs\nin the Git repo. It also has functionality for pushing and pulling to/from Git\nremotes."],[4170,"The git_head template keyword now returns an optional value instead of a\nlist of 0 or 1 element."],[1511,"The git.auto-local-bookmark config knob is applied when importing new remote\nbranch. jj branch sub commands will be added to change the tracking state."],[3360,"The git.push-bookmark-prefix setting is deprecated in favor of\ntemplates.git_push_bookmark, which supports templating. The old setting can\nbe expressed in template as \"<prefix>\" ++ change_id.short()."],[3898,"The git.push-branch-prefix config has been deprecated in favor of\ngit.push-bookmark-prefix."],[3516,"The git.push-branch-prefix config has been removed in favor of\ngit.push-bookmark-prefix."],[2947,"The GitBackend stores commits in a Git repository. It uses libgit2 to read\nand write commits and refs."],[4572,"The global --no-commit-working-copy is now called --ignore-working-copy."],[1289,"The goal is to land good support for pure Jujutsu repositories, while colocated\nrepositories will be supported when convenient."],[123,"The goal is to restore change lnvvtrzo to its previous state and split the\ndifference into a new child commit."],[1339,"The goal of this Design Document is to specify the correct behavior of jj run.\nThe points we decide on here I (Philip Metzger) will try to implement. There\nexists some prior work in other DVCS:"],[1495,"The goal of this plan is to implement"],[1010,"The governance working group was appointed by recommendation from Martin (jj's\noriginal author and current sole maintainer), without recommendation or approval\nfrom the broader jj community. This isn't a problem in itself - but it does\nmean that the governance working group (Austin Seipp/aseipp, Waleed\nKhan/arxanas, Martin von Zweigbergk/martinvonz, and Emily Shaffer/nasamuffin)\nneeds to get some community approval before setting policy for the entire jj\nproject. If we skip this step, we risk being perceived as exercising excessive\ncontrol over the project."],[3836,"The HEAD@git symbol no longer resolves to the Git HEAD revision. Use\ngit_head() or @- revset expression instead. The git_head template\nkeyword now returns a boolean."],[4492,"The heads() revset function was split up into two functions. heads()\nwithout arguments is now called visible_heads(). heads() with one argument\nis unchanged."],[1694,"The hyphen after your-feature comes from the revset syntax."],[491,"The index"],[1345,"The initial need for some kind of command runner integrated in the VCS, surfaced\nin a github discussion. In a discussion on discord about\nthe git-hook model, there was consensus about not repeating their mistakes."],[2938,"The jj binary consists of two Rust crates: the library crate (jj-lib) and\nthe CLI crate (jj-cli). The library crate is currently only used by the CLI\ncrate, but it is meant to also be usable from a GUI or TUI, or in a server\nserving requests from multiple users. As a result, the library should avoid\ninteracting directly with the user via the terminal or by other means; all\ninput/output is handled by the CLI crate . Since the library crate is meant\nto usable in a server, it also cannot read configuration from the user's home\ndirectory, or from user-specific environment variables."],[4040,"The jj file subcommand now contains several existing file utilities."],[2185,"The jj fix command allows you to efficiently rewrite files in complex commit\ngraphs with no risk of introducing conflicts, using tools like clang-format or\nprettier. The tools run as subprocesses that take file content on standard\ninput and repeat it, with any desired changes, on standard output. The file is\nonly rewritten if the subprocess produces a successful exit code."],[855,"The jj help docs are sourced from the \"docstring\" comments inside the Rust\nsources, currently from the cli/src/commands\ndirectory. Working\non them requires setting up a Rust development environment, as described\nbelow, and may occasionally require adjusting a test."],[3606,"The jj init/jj revert stubs that print errors can now be overridden with\naliases. All of jj clone/init/revert add a hint to a generic error."],[4173,"The jj log command no longer uses the default revset when a path is\nspecified."],[4494,"The jj sparse command was split up into jj sparse list and\njj sparse set."],[4171,"The jj sparse set --edit/--reset flags were split up into jj sparse\nedit/reset subcommands respectively."],[4172,"The jj sparse subcommands now parse and print patterns as workspace-relative\npaths."],[4561,"The jj touchup command was renamed to jj diffedit."],[3519,"The jj untrack subcommand has been removed in favor of jj file untrack."],[3535,"The JJ_CONFIG environment variable can now contain multiple paths separated\nby a colon (or semicolon on Windows)."],[1470,"The Josh Project implements partial git\nclones in a way similar to how sparse patterns try to work."],[4487,"The jujutsu and jujutsu-lib crates were renamed to jj-cli and jj-lib,\nrespectively."],[3692,"The Jujutsu documentation site now publishes a schema for the official\nconfiguration file, which can be integrated into your editor for autocomplete,\ninline errors, and more.\nPlease see the documentation for more\non this."],[2013,"The key names are called \"labels\". The above used commit_id as label. You can\nalso create rules combining multiple labels. The rules work a bit like CSS\nselectors. For example, if you want to color commit IDs green in general but\nmake the commit ID of the working-copy commit also be underlined, you can do\nthis:"],[4521,"The last seen state of branches in the underlying git repo is now presented by\njj branch list/jj log as a remote called git (e.g. main@git). They can\nalso be referenced in revsets. Such branches exist in colocated repos or if\nyou use jj git export."],[1844,"The line endings conversion won't be applied to files detected as a binary files\nvia a heuristics regardless of the settings. This behavior is subject to\nchange when we support the text git attribute."],[2259,"The line endings conversion won't be applied to files detected as binary files\nvia a heuristics regardless of the settings. This is similar to git."],[4130,"The list of conflicted paths is printed whenever the working copy changes.\nThis can be disabled with the --quiet option."],[580,"The local bookmark must not be conflicted. If it is, you would\nneed to use jj bookmark move, for example, to resolve the conflict."],[2278,"The location of the jj user config files/directories can also be overridden with the\nJJ_CONFIG environment variable. If it is not empty, it will be used instead\nof any configuration files in the default locations. If it is a path to a TOML\nfile, then that file will be loaded instead. If it is a path to a directory,\nthen all the TOML files in that directory will be loaded in lexicographic order\nand merged. Multiple paths can be specified by separating them with a\nplatform-specific path separator (: on Unix-like systems, ; on Windows)."],[1780,"The log command and \"revsets\""],[1239,"The main challenge with this approach is that the submodule repo can be in a\nstate that is internally valid (when considering only the submodule's repo), but\ninvalid when considering the superproject-submodule system. This will be managed\nby requiring all submodule interactions go through the superproject so that\nsuperproject-submodule coordination can occur. For example, jj will not allow\nthe user to work on the submodule's repo without going through the superproject\n(unlike Git)."],[1352,"The main goal is to be good enough, as we can always expand the functionality\nin the future."],[609,"The markers <<<<<<< and >>>>>>> indicate the start and end of a conflict\nrespectively. The marker +++++++ indicates the start of a snapshot, while the\nmarker %%%%%%% indicates the start of a diff to apply to the snapshot.\nTherefore, to resolve this conflict, you would apply the diff (changing \"grape\"\nto \"grapefruit\") to the snapshot (the side with every line in uppercase),\nediting the file to look like this:"],[4560,"The minimum supported Rust version (MSRV) is now 1.61.0."],[4491,"The minimum supported Rust version (MSRV) is now 1.64.0."],[4419,"The minimum supported Rust version (MSRV) is now 1.71.0."],[4216,"The minimum supported Rust version (MSRV) is now 1.76.0."],[3515,"The minimum supported Rust version (MSRV) is now 1.84.0."],[3409,"The minimum supported Rust version (MSRV) is now 1.85.0."],[1171,"The model and algorithm described in this document would result in a conflict\nin the copy ID at both paths after propagating the renames."],[2992,"The most important piece in the lock-free design is the \"operation log\". That is\nwhat allows us to detect and merge divergent operations."],[1822,"The most useful command is jj undo, which will undo your last operation."],[3319,"The new bisect(x) revset function can help bisect a range of commits to\nfind when a bug was introduced."],[3239,"The new command jj bisect run uses binary search to find a commit that\nintroduced a bug."],[3257,"The new command jj redo can progressively redo operations that were\npreviously undone by multiple calls to jj undo."],[3900,"The new config option snapshot.auto-track lets you automatically track only\nthe specified paths (all paths by default). Use the new jj file track\ncommand to manually tracks path that were not automatically tracked. There is\nno way to list untracked files yet. Use git status in a colocated workspace\nas a workaround.\n#323"],[3263,"The new configuration option git.colocate=boolean controls whether or not\nGit repositories are colocated by default."],[4657,"The new global flag -v/--verbose will turn on debug logging to give\nsome additional insight into what is happening behind the scenes.\nNote: This is not comprehensively supported by all operations yet."],[4522,"The new jj chmod command allows setting or removing the executable bit on\npaths. Unlike the POSIX chmod, it works on Windows, on conflicted files, and\non arbitrary revisions. Bits other than the executable bit are not planned to\nbe supported."],[4644,"The new jj git remote rename command allows git remotes to be renamed\nin-place."],[4696,"The new jj git remotes list command lists the configured remotes and their\nURLs.\n#243"],[4693,"The new jj interdiff command compares the changes in commits, ignoring\nchanges from intervening commits."],[4695,"The new jj print command prints the contents of a file in a revision."],[4645,"The new jj resolve command allows resolving simple conflicts with\nan external 3-way-merge tool."],[3598,"The new jj sign and jj unsign commands allow for signing/unsigning commits.\njj sign supports configuring the default revset through revsets.sign when\nno --revisions arguments are provided."],[4705,"The new revset function connected(x) is the same as x:x."],[4648,"The new revset function empty() finds commits modifying no files."],[4647,"The new revset function file(pattern..) finds commits modifying the\npaths specified by the pattern..."],[4706,"The new revset function roots(x) finds commits in the set that are not\ndescendants of other commits in the set."],[4376,"The new template keywords local_branches/remote_branches are added to show\nonly local/remote branches."],[1240,"The notable workflows could be addressed like so:"],[562,"The notion of tracked bookmarks serves a similar function to the Git notion of an\n\"upstream branch\". Unlike Git, a single local bookmark can be tracking remote\nbookmarks on multiple remotes, and the names of the local and remote bookmarks\nmust match."],[3402,"The old libgit2 code path for fetches and pushes has been removed,\nand the git.subprocess setting along with it."],[347,"The old working-copy commit remains as a sibling commit. The old working-copy commit X can be restored with jj edit X."],[4217,"The on-disk index format changed. New index files will be created\nautomatically, but it can fail if the repository is colocated and predates\nGit GC issues #815. If\nreindexing failed, you'll need to clean up corrupted operation history by\njj op abandon ..<bad operation ID>."],[3253,"The on-disk index format has changed. jj will write index files in both old\nand new formats, so old jj versions should be able to read these index\nfiles. This compatibility layer will be removed in a future release."],[4273,"The only difference between these commands and jj new, which also creates\na new working copy commit, is that new can create a working copy commit on\ntop of any arbitrary number of revisions, so it can handle both the previous\ncases at once. The only actual difference between these three commands is the\ncommand syntax and their name. These names were chosen to be familiar to users\nof other version control systems, but we instead encourage all users to adopt\njj new instead; it is more general and easier to remember than both of\nthese."],[894,"The only thing you need is uv (version 0.5.1 or newer)."],[1782,"The operation log"],[695,"The operation log allows you to undo operations one-by-one (jj undo) or even\nrevert a specific one which isn't the most recent operation (jj op revert). It\nalso lets you restore the entire repo to the way it looked at an earlier point\n(jj op restore)."],[1312,"The operation log captures changes in the submodule."],[2993,"The operation log is similar to a commit DAG (such as in\nGit's object model),\nbut each commit object is instead an \"operation\" and each tree object is instead\na \"view\". The view object contains the set of visible head commits, bookmarks,\ntags, and the working-copy commit in each workspace. The operation object\ncontains a pointer to the view object (like how commit objects point to tree\nobjects), pointers to parent operation(s) (like how commit objects point to\nparent commit(s)), and metadata about the operation. These types are defined\nin op_store.proto The operation log is normally linear.\nIt becomes non-linear if there are divergent operations."],[671,"The operation log is the\nDAG formed by\noperation objects, much in the same way that commits form a DAG,\nwhich is sometimes called the \"commit history\". When operations happen in\nsequence, they form a single line in the graph. Operations that happen\nconcurrently from jj's perspective result in forks and merges in the DAG."],[502,"The operation log replaces reflogs. The operation log is similar to\nreflogs, but is much more powerful. It keeps track of atomic updates to all\nrefs at once (Jujutsu thus improves on Git's per-ref history much in the same\nway that Subversion improved on RCS's per-file history). The operation log\npowers e.g. the undo functionality. Details."],[2998,"The operation objects and view objects are stored in content-addressed storage\njust like Git commits are. That makes them safe to write without locking."],[3971,"The original configuration syntax for jj fix is now deprecated in favor of\none that allows defining multiple tools that can affect different filesets.\nThese can be used in combination for now. See jj help fix for details."],[1791,"The parent commit, for example, has the change ID orrkosyo and the commit ID\n7fd1a60b."],[3362,"The parents() and children() revset functions now accept an optional\ndepth argument. For instance, parents(x, 3) is equivalent to x---, and\nchildren(x, 3) is equivalent to x+++."],[4796,"The person proposing a decision to be made (i.e. technical, project direction,\netc.) can offer a proposal, along with a 2-to-4 week deadline for discussion.\nDuring this time, Maintainers may participate with a vote of:"],[795,"The place to describe all new interfaces and interactions and how it plays into\nthe existing code and behavior. This is the place for all nitty-gritty details\nwhich interact with the system."],[3917,"The present() revset now suppresses missing working copy error. For example,\npresent(@) evaluates to none() if the current workspace has no\nworking-copy commit."],[3305,"The previously predefined amend alias has been removed. You can restore it\nby setting the config aliases.amend = [\"squash\"]."],[117,"The private commit wwwwwwww is the second parent of the merge commit."],[1165,"The problem here for Mercurial is that after squashing C into A, the new A has\nfile bar but no record that it used to be called foo. The design proposed\nabove handles this case because we keep the copy ID of bar after squashing,\nso we can detect that the modifications to foo in commit B should be\npropagated to bar."],[4099,"The progress bar (visible when using e.g. jj git clone) clears the\nremainder of the cursor row after drawing rather than clearing the entire row\nbefore drawing, eliminating the \"flicker\" effect seen on some terminals."],[4512,"The progress display on jj git clone/fetch now includes the downloaded size."],[1116,"The proposal is to update tree objects to also contain information about a\nfile's past names. For example, if file foo gets renamed to bar in one\ncommit and then to baz in another commit, we will record that baz previously\nhad names bar and foo."],[1053,"The proposal may be abandoned (if rejected)"],[1052,"The proposal may be revised and begin again at stage 2 (if rejected)"],[1051,"The proposal will be implemented (if accepted)"],[4424,"The push.branch-prefix option was renamed to git.push-branch-prefix."],[1135,"The remaining file is bar on the source side. Its closest relative on the\ndestination side is baz. Since we already used baz as a rename target for\nfoo, we won't consider bar renamed to it. So we consider bar as copied\ninto baz."],[4332,"The remote_branches() revset no longer includes branches exported to the Git\nrepository (so called Git-tracking branches.)"],[4358,"The remote_branches() revset now includes branches exported to the Git\nrepository (so called Git-tracking branches.) This change will be reverted\nin 0.12.0."],[1985,"The repo settings. These can be edited with jj config edit --repo and are\nlocated in .jj/repo/config.toml."],[2963,"The repo view at the current operation determines the desired working-copy\ncommit in each workspace. The WorkingCopy determines what is actually in the\nworking copy. The working copy can become stale if the working-copy commit was\nchanged from another workspace (or if the process updating the working copy\ncrashed, for example)."],[2958,"The RepoLoader represents a repository at an unspecified operation. You can\nthink of as a pointer to the .jj/repo/ directory. It can create\na ReadonlyRepo given an operation ID."],[2955,"The repository doesn't know where on disk any working copies live. It knows, via\nthe view object, which commit is supposed to be the current working-copy commit\nin each workspace."],[3021,"The repository is the source of truth: Most commands should operate on the\ncommit graph. The working copy is just one way of editing commits."],[3010,"The resulting tree contents is calculated on demand. Note that we often don't\nneed to merge the entire tree. For example, when checking out a commit in the\nworking copy, we only need to merge parts of the tree that differs from the\ntree that was previously checked out in the working copy. As another example,\nwhen listing paths with conflicts, we only need to traverse parts of the tree\nthat cannot be trivially resolved; if only one side modified lib/, then we\ndon't need to look for conflicts in that sub-tree."],[4762,"The role of a Contributor is less formal. In situations where opinions become\nnumerous or contentious, it is acceptable for the maintainers to assign more\nweight to the voices of the more established Contributors."],[4761,"The role of the Maintainer is formally defined. These are the people empowered\nto collectively make final decisions about most aspects of the project. They are\nexpected to take community's input seriously and to aim for the benefit of the\nentire community."],[677,"The root commit is a virtual commit at the root of every repository. It has a\ncommit ID consisting of all '0's (00000000...) and a change ID consisting of\nall 'z's (zzzzzzzz...). It can be referred to in revsets by the\nfunction root(). Note that our definition of \"root commit\" is different from\nGit's; Git's \"root commits\" are the first commit(s) in the repository, i.e. the\ncommits jj log -r 'root()+' will show."],[4429,"The root revset symbol has been converted to function root()."],[1349,"The run command should be able to work in any commit, the working-copy commit\nitself or any other commit."],[919,"The script sets up a few environment variables and invokes uv run mike deploy\nwith some default arguments and whatever arguments were passed to\ndocs-build-deploy. Run uv run mike help deploy to find out what the\narguments do."],[2510,"The search paths can be narrowed by the files expression. All modified files\nare scanned by default, but it is likely to change in future version to\nrespect the command line path arguments."],[799,"The section for things which could be added to it or deemed out of scope during\nthe discussion."],[1430,"The set of files identified by the Sparse Patterns is all paths which match\nany provided prefix. This governs what gets materialized in the working copy on\ncheckout, and what is updated on snapshot. The set is stored in working copy\nstate files which are not versioned in the Op Store."],[2220,"The setting git.colocate is a boolean option that controls whether or not the\njj git init and jj git clone commands should create colocated repositories\nby default. By default, git.colocate is set to true."],[4520,"The shortest unique change ID prefixes and commit ID prefixes in jj log are\nnow shorter within the default log revset. You can override the default by\nsetting the revsets.short-prefixes config to a different revset."],[3742,"The Signature.username() template method is deprecated for\nSignature.email().local()."],[3612,"The signing.backends.ssh.allowed-signers configuration option will now\nexpand ~/ to $HOME/.\n#5626"],[2211,"The signing.behavior configuration option has four different options for what\nto do with signing commits on modification of a change (e.g., rebasing or edits)."],[3597,"The signing.sign-all config option has been deprecated in favor of\nsigning.behavior. The new option accepts drop (never sign), keep (preserve\nexisting signatures), own (sign own commits), or force (sign all commits).\nExisting signing.sign-all = true translates to signing.behavior = \"own\", and\nfalse translates to \"keep\". Invalid configuration is now an error."],[2951,"The SimpleBackend is just a proof of concept. It stores objects addressed by\ntheir hash, with one file per object."],[79,"The simplest way to monitor how the history as shown by jj log evolves is by using the watch(1) command (or hwatch or viddy).\nFor example:"],[1680,"The simplest way to start with Jujutsu is to create a stack of commits first.\nYou will only need to create a bookmark when you need to push the stack to a\nremote. There are two primary workflows: using a generated bookmark name or\nnaming a bookmark."],[1355,"The smart caching of outputs, as user input commands can be unpredictable.\nmakes the command confusing for users."],[714,"The snapshot.auto-track config option controls which paths get automatically\ntracked when they're added to the working copy. See the\nfileset documentation for the syntax. Files with paths matching\nignore files are never tracked automatically."],[4148,"The snapshot.max-new-file-size option can now handle raw integer literals,\ninterpreted as a number of bytes, where previously it could only handle string\nliterals. This means that snapshot.max-new-file-size=\"1\" and\nsnapshot.max-new-file-size=1 are now equivalent."],[1099,"The solution should support recording and retrieving copy info in a way that\nis performant both for Git, which synthesizes copy info on the fly between\narbitrary trees, and for custom backends which may explicitly record and\nre-serve copy info over arbitrarily large commit ranges."],[2096,"The special value :builtin enables usage of the integrated\npager."],[4737,"The standard $NO_COLOR environment variable is now\nrespected."],[1767,"The standard completion script provides completions for jj subcommmands and\noptions."],[790,"The state of the feature you want to improve and where it currently falls\nshort. If there's nothing to compare to, leave it out."],[4420,"The storage format of branches, tags, and git refs has changed. Newly-stored\nrepository data will no longer be loadable by older binaries."],[2953,"The Store type also provides caching of commits and trees."],[2952,"The Store type wraps the Backend and returns wrapped types for commits and\ntrees to make them easier to use. The wrapped objects have a reference to\nthe Store itself, so you can do e.g. commit.parents() without having to\nprovide the Store as an argument."],[1225,"The submodule storage format should support the workflows specified in the\nsubmodules roadmap. It should be obvious how \"Phase 1\"\nrequirements will be supported, and we should have an idea of how \"Phases 2,3,X\"\nmight be supported."],[1241,"The submodule would fetch using the equivalent of jj git fetch. It remains to\nbe decided how a \"recursive\" fetch should work, especially if a newly fetched\nsuperproject commit references an unfetched submodule commit. A reasonable\napproximation would be to fetch all branches in the submodule, and then, if the\nsubmodule commit is still missing, gracefully handle it."],[1237,"The superproject using a non-git backend"],[2077,"The symbols used to represent commits or operations can be customized via\ntemplates."],[2975,"The tables are named by their hash. We keep a separate directory of pointers to\nthe current leaf tables, in the same way as we\ndo for the operation log."],[3801,"The tags() revset function now takes an optional pattern argument,\nmirroring that of bookmarks()."],[1624,"The target of forgotten remote branch is absent"],[3262,"The template language now allows arbitrary whitespace between any operators."],[4568,"The template.log.graph and template.commit_summary config keys were\nrenamed to templates.log and templates.commit_summary respectively."],[3277,"The test suite no longer optionally uses Taplo CLI or jq, and packagers can\nremove them as dependencies if present."],[702,"The top-level --at-operation/--at-op option allows you to load the repo at a\nspecific operation. This can be useful for understanding how your repo got into\nthe current state. It can be even more useful for understanding why someone\nelse's repo got into its current state."],[2957,"The Transaction object has a MutableRepo and metadata that will go into the\noperation log. When the transaction commits, the MutableRepo becomes a view\nobject in the operation log on disk, and the Transaction object becomes an\noperation object. In memory, Transaction::commit() returns a\nnew ReadonlyRepo."],[2959,"The TreeState type represents the state of the files in a working copy. It\nkeep track of the mtime and size for each tracked file. It knows the TreeId\nthat the working copy represents. It has a snapshot() method that will use the\nrecorded mtimes and sizes and detect changes in the working copy. If anything\nchanged, it will return a new TreeId. It also has checkout() for updating\nthe files on disk to match a requested TreeId."],[2960,"The TreeState type supports sparse checkouts. In fact, all working copies are\nsparse; they simply track the full repo in most cases."],[3594,"The ui.allow-filesets configuration option has been removed.\nThe \"fileset\" language has been enabled by default since v0.20."],[4493,"The ui.default-revset config was renamed to revsets.log."],[2134,"The ui.diff-editor setting affects the default tool used for editing diffs\n(e.g. jj split, jj squash -i). If it is not set, the special value\n:builtin is used. It launches a built-in TUI tool (known as scm-diff-editor)\nto edit the diff in your terminal."],[4607,"The ui.diff-instructions config setting can be set to false to inhibit the\ncreation of the JJ-INSTRUCTIONS file as part of diff editing."],[3410,"The ui.diff.format and ui.diff.tool config options have been merged as\nui.diff-formatter. The builtin format can be specified as :<format>\n(e.g. ui.diff-formatter=\":git\" for Git diffs.)"],[2164,"The ui.merge-editor key specifies the tool used for three-way merge tools\nby jj resolve. For example:"],[4488,"The ui.oplog-relative-timestamps option has been removed. Use the\nformat_time_range() template alias instead. For details, see\nthe documentation."],[4570,"The ui.relative-timestamps option has been removed. Use the\nformat_timestamp() template alias instead. For details on showing relative\ntimestamps in jj log and jj show, see the documentation."],[588,"The use of bookmarks is frequent in some workflows, for example, when\ninteracting with Git repositories containing branches. To this end,\none-letter shortcuts have been implemented, both for the jj bookmark\ncommand itself through an alias (as jj b), and for its subcommands.\nFor example, jj bookmark create BOOKMARK-NAME -r@ can be abbreviated as\njj b c BOOKMARK-NAME -r@."],[753,"The use of sexualized language or imagery, and sexual attention or advances of\nany kind"],[1984,"The user settings. These can be edited with jj config edit --user. User\nsettings are located in the user config files, which can be found with jj\nconfig path --user."],[4654,"The username and hostname that appear in the operation log are now\nconfigurable via config options operation.username and operation.hostname."],[2255,"The value can be specified using a human readable string with typical suffixes;\nB, MiB, GB, etc. By default, if no suffix is provided, or the value is a\nraw integer literal, the value is interpreted as if it were specified in bytes."],[1467,"The View object will be migrated to store working copy patterns via id. The\nindirection will save on storage since working copy patterns are not expected to\nchange very frequently."],[82,"The wiki lists additional TUIs and GUIs beyond the terminal: GUI-and-TUI"],[660,"The word \"revision\" is used as a synonym for \"commit\"."],[2359,"The words \"revisions\" and \"commits\" are used interchangeably in this document."],[118,"The working copy (vvvvvvvv) contains changes from both."],[687,"The working copy contains the files you're currently working on. It is\nautomatically snapshot at the beginning of almost every jj command, thus\ncreating a new working-copy commit if any changes had\nbeen made in the working copy. Conversely, the working copy is automatically\nupdated to the state of the working-copy commit at the end of almost every jj\ncommand. See here for details."],[494,"The working copy is automatically committed. That results in a simpler and\nmore consistent CLI because the working copy is now treated like any other\ncommit. Details."],[524,"The working copy is effectively backed up every time you run a command."],[711,"The working copy is where the current working-copy commit's files are written so\nyou can interact with them. It is also where files are read from in order to\ncreate new commits (though there are many other ways of creating new commits)."],[1019,"The working group lets the community know about upcoming policy drafts they're\nintending to share for approval. This must happen at least a week before\nentering stage 3, and ideally should happen even earlier."],[1046,"The working group may set the voting period longer to encompass two\nweekends (for more participation around day jobs), for less urgent or more\ncomplex proposals, or to account for holidays during the voting period."],[1028,"The working group will consider these recommendations in good faith, but may\nchoose not to adopt them."],[2961,"The WorkingCopy type has a TreeState but also knows which WorkspaceName it\nhas and at which operation it was most recently updated."],[2962,"The Workspace type represents the combination of a repo and a working copy (\nlike Git's 'worktree' concept)."],[97,"Then future edits will go into a new working-copy commit on top of the now\nformer working-copy commit. Whenever you are happy with another set of edits,\nuse jj squash to amend the previous commit."],[931,"Then go to https://ui.perfetto.dev/ in Chrome and load /tmp/trace.json from\nthere."],[929,"Then just open the link it prints."],[1859,"Then only use the ~/my-repo workspace from Linux."],[2195,"Then to use the tool in a specific repository, set the enabled config:"],[980,"Then try to find the right GitHub username for each person and copy their name\nand username from the GitHub page for the person\n(e.g. https://github.com/martinvonz)."],[3355,"Theo Buehler (@botovq)"],[3496,"Théo Daron (@tdaron)"],[4120,"Théo Daron (@tdaron)"],[4167,"Théo Daron (@tdaron)"],[3886,"Theodore Ehrenborg (@TheodoreEhrenborg)"],[579,"There are a few cases where jj git push will succeed even though the remote\nbookmark is in an unexpected location. These are the cases where jj git fetch\nwould not create a bookmark conflict and would not move the local bookmark, e.g.\nif the unexpected location is identical to the local position of the bookmark."],[468,"There are a few downsides to this mode of operation. Generally, using colocated\nrepos may require you to deal with more involved Jujutsu and Git concepts.\nYou can disable colocation with the --no-colocate flag on the commands\njj git init and jj git clone or by setting the configuration\ngit.colocate = false."],[2939,"There are a few exceptions, such as for messages printed during automatic\nupgrades of the repo format"],[1813,"There are also operators for getting the parents (foo-), children (foo+),\nancestors (::foo), descendants (foo::), DAG range (foo::bar, like\ngit log --ancestry-path), range (foo..bar, same as Git's). See\nthe revset documentation for all revset operators and functions."],[651,"There are also pluggable backends for storing other information than commits,\nsuch as the \"operation store backend\" for storing\nthe operation log."],[879,"There are also ways of having Rust use mold by default, see the \"How\nto use\" instructions."],[1233,"There are certain extensions we may want to make in the future, but we don't\nhave a timeline for them today. Proposed approaches should take these\nextensions into account (e.g. the approach should be theoretically extensible),\nbut a full proposal for implementing them is not necessary."],[1829,"There are many other diff editors you could use.\nFor example, if you have Meld installed and in the\nPATH, you can use it via jj squash -i --tool meld or a fancier config with jj\nsquash -i --tool meld-3. You can configure the default with the\nui.diff-editor option; those docs also explain how\nto specify a path to an executable if it is not in the PATH."],[1444,"There are many ways to represent functionally equivalent WorkingCopyPatterns.\nFor instance, the following 4 rule sets are all functionally equivalent:"],[2229,"There are multiple remotes with conflicting views of that bookmark,\nresulting in an unhelpful conflicted state."],[4088,"There are now prebuilt binaries for aarch64-linux-unknown-musl.\nNote, these are cross compiled and currently untested.\nWe plan on providing fully tested builds later once our CI system allows it."],[1740,"There are pre-built binaries of the last released version of jj for\nWindows, Mac, or Linux (the \"musl\" version should work on all distributions)."],[1817,"There are several things worth noting here. First, the jj rebase command said\n\"Rebased 2 commits\". That's because we asked it to rebase commit B2 with the\n-s option, which also rebases descendants (commit C in this case). Second,\nbecause B2 modified the same file (and word) as B1, rebasing it resulted in\nconflicts, as the output indicates. Third, the conflicts did not prevent the\nrebase from completing successfully, nor did it prevent C from getting rebased\non top."],[158,"There are some trade-offs and there is no definitive answer yet."],[569,"There are two situations where jj tracks bookmarks automatically. jj git\nclone automatically sets up the default remote bookmark (e.g. main@origin) as\ntracked. When you push a local bookmark, the newly created bookmark on the remote is\nmarked as tracked."],[4760,"There are two special roles for participants in the Jujutsu projects:\nMaintainers and Contributors."],[136,"There are two ways to resume working on an earlier change: jj new then jj squash,\nand jj edit. The first is generally recommended, but jj edit can be useful. When\nyou use jj edit, the revision is directly amended with your new changes, making it\ndifficult to tell what exactly you change. You should avoid using jj edit when the\nrevision has a conflict, as you may accidentally break the plain-text annotations on\nyour state without realizing."],[1688,"There are two workflows for addressing review comments, depending on your\nproject's preference. Many projects prefer that you address comments by adding\ncommits to your bookmark. Some projects (such as Jujutsu and LLVM) instead\nprefer that you keep your commits clean by rewriting them and then\nforce-pushing."],[3675,"There is a new builtin_op_log_oneline template alias you can pass to jj op\nlog -T for a more compact output. You can use format_operation_oneline and\nformat_snapshot_operation_oneline to customize parts of it."],[4189,"There is a new global --quiet flag to silence commands' non-primary output."],[4339,"There is a new jj util gc command for cleaning up the repository storage.\nFor now, it simply runs git gc on the backing Git repo (when using the Git\nbackend)."],[2228,"There is a remote with a lot of historical bookmarks that you don't\nwant to be exported to the colocated Git repo."],[103,"There is a tracking issue you can check for updates."],[2162,"There is also a meld merge tool\nthat can be useful, but does not support displaying the merge base while\nmerging."],[2148,"There is also the diffedit3-ssh which is similar to diffedit3 but does not\ntry to open the web browser pointing to the local server (the URL\nprinted to the terminal) automatically. diffedit3-ssh also always uses ports in between\n17376-17380 and fails if they are all busy. This can be useful when working\nover SSH. Open the fold below for more details of how to set that up."],[259,"There is no support for cloning non-Git repos yet."],[263,"There is no support for fetching into non-Git repos yet."],[267,"There is no support for pushing from non-Git repos yet."],[271,"There is no support for pushing from non-Git repos yet."],[1471,"There may be some scenarios where it is valuable to configure working copy\npatterns via a configuration file, rather than through explicit commands.\nGenerally this only makes sense for automated repos, with the configuration\ncoming from outside the repo - there are too many caveats and edge cases if the\nconfiguration comes from inside the repo and/or is fought with by a human."],[476,"There may still be bugs when interleaving mutating jj and git commands,\nusually having to do with a branch pointer ending up in the wrong place. We\nare working on the known ones, and are not aware of any major ones. Please\nreport any new ones you find, or if any of the known bugs are less minor than\nthey appear."],[1350,"There should exist some way to signal hard failure."],[878,"There will be no indication that a different linker is used, except for\nhigher CPU usage while linking and, hopefully, faster completion. You\ncan verify that mold was indeed used by running\nreadelf -p .comment target/debug/jj."],[503,"There's a single, virtual root commit. Like Mercurial, Jujutsu has a\nvirtual commit (with a hash consisting of only zeros) called the \"root commit\"\n(called the \"null revision\" in Mercurial). This commit is a common ancestor of\nall commits. That removes the awkward state Git calls the \"unborn branch\"\nstate (which is the state a newly initialized Git repo is in), and related\ncommand-line flags (e.g. git rebase --root, git checkout --orphan)."],[2974,"There's no garbage collection of unreachable tables yet."],[495,"There's no index (staging area). Because the working copy is automatically\ncommitted, an index-like concept doesn't make sense. The index is very similar\nto an intermediate commit between HEAD and the working copy, so workflows\nthat depend on it can be modeled using proper commits instead. Jujutsu has\nexcellent support for moving changes between commits. Details."],[4287,"There's now a virtual root operation, similar to the virtual root\ncommit. It appears at the end of jj op log."],[619,"Therefore, a resolution of this conflict could be grapefruit\\n, with the\nterminating newline character added."],[1599,"Therefore, no local commits should be abandoned"],[1987,"These are listed in the order they are loaded; the settings from earlier items\nin the list are overridden by the settings from later items if they disagree.\nEvery type of config except for the built-in settings is optional."],[865,"These are listed roughly in order of decreasing importance."],[1981,"These are the config settings available to jj/Jujutsu."],[2859,"These are the exact same as the String pattern type in revsets, except that\nquotes are mandatory."],[1234,"These extensions are:"],[906,"These instructions are relevant if you are working on the versioning of the\ndocumentation that we currently do with mike."],[1037,"Think of this like a code review; the